      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <p>Sir,—ln your issue of the 13th inst. is an article relating to the denial of any physical existence whatever by Christian Scientists and most Buddhists.</p>
        <p>Through this statement perchance some of your readers might be led to believe that Chris- tian Science has some relationsllip to Buddhism. Perhaps you will permit me to refute such a suggestion.</p>
        <p>Christian Sciende is just the teaching of Christ Jesus understood and demonstrated. It reveals one God, Spirit, one universe, and man in God's image and likeness, hence spiritual. This revelation of the Allness of Spirit and His spiritual creation, necessarily precludes any possibility of the existence of matter. The so- called mind which believes in the existence of matter and objectifies its belief has been termed by. Mrs. Eddy "mortal mind." Paul named it "carnal mind," which "is emnity against God" (divine mind).</p>
        <p>Attributing all-power, all-science, all-presence to Divine Mind, Christian Science not only shows physical existence to be the phenomena of mortal mind, hence untrue, unreal, but re- veals true existence to be spiritual, or mental, found only in the knowledge of God, Divine Mind. Jesus said: "This is life eternal, that they might know Thee, the only true God." Paul said: "For in Him (mind) we live, move, and have our being."</p>
        <p>Christian Scientists are finding that in pro- portion as they are putting off the so-called mortal mind (the old man) and putting on that "Mind which was also in Christ Jesus" (the new man), the works of the flesh and the pain- ful effects of these works as seen in disease, suf- fering, poverty and sorrow, are being elimin- ated; and the fruits of th,tt spirit, as expressed in peace, joy, health, &amp;c., are becoming a daily experience. Preaching without practice, words without deeds, theology without healing, is not satisfying to-day, and it is because Christian Science is practical, and is fulfilling the com- mand of Jesus by healing the sick and destroy- ing sin, that it is making such rapid progress the world over.</p>
        <p>JOHN W. HARWOOD. ' Christian' Science COmmittee on Publication for Lancashire. 51, Corporation-street, Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>OUR UNTIDY STREETS.</head>
        <p>OUR UNTIDY STREETS.</p>
        <p>often spend a holiday here, and this time I cannot but be more than usually struck with the untidiness of the streets; there are tram tickets and papers—in many cases lai:ge sheets—simply everywhere. If one set to work to pick them up "twelve baskets" would not be sufficient. Seven'years last August I came to Warrington on a visit for the first time, and then remarked upon the littered paper, and was told there was a "Beautiful Warrington Society." I hear this society has been making an outcry re the output of smoke. Would it not be well to turn the attention of the folk to the littering of papers? Of course, it is in the hands of the people themselves. Street cleaners can do much, but the papers ought never to be thrown down. Individuals are of importance here. All can do their part in keeping the town beautiful. Might not the teachers train the children from their earliest years not to throw down their lunch papers, orange peel and banana skins? I am a teacher myself, and find the best plan to seep the yard tidy is to have a yard monitor, and this child sees that no paperd are littered. Anyone caught in the act has to pick up the offending paper and put it in the waste paper box. The monitor feels it an im- portant position, and I have little troublO to look after the work myself. Could not ladies and gentlemen slip their car tickets in the box provided or else in their, pockets, and put them on the fire -.Then they get home; and the same with scraps if newspaper 9 In Germany the policeman would e at'er cien- ders. I heartily approve of planting tree, but untidy litter spoilsrfie effect.</p>
        <p>A rIS TOR.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln replying to J. W. Mealor's letter in your last week's issue let me try, in my humble way, to answer the various points he has raised in his answer to my letter of the 6th inst.</p>
        <p>He commences with the statement that I am in the fighting ranks, but I cannot see for smoke. Well, I can see through the smoke clear enough the enerhy I am firing at, and knowing from actual experience, by living amongst them in my own past life, the evil that enemy creates in the homes of our citizens, I am fully pre- pared to go on fighting with powder and smoke, if in the end the victory is on the Temperance side. He says that if we use the smokeless powder of temperance suburbs we could see how the battles go, and what gains we are making on the enemy's fort. Well, anyone who has any knowledge of fighting in any cause knows that the best way to gain the victory or admit defeat is to come to grips with the foe. If our noble country had always fought her battles from afar, instead of boldly charging the foe, _England would not hold the proud and honoured position she holds to-day. Therefore let us take example from our ancestors and charge boltily on the foe. We, in this present day, can see from afar the advantages gained by their victories; so with this great national evil of the drink trade. If we, as Temperance Reformers, want to win this battle, so that the children of to-day, who will be the future generations, may reap the benefit of Ipur battles, fought on their behalf, we must be prepared to fight the foe face to face.</p>
        <p>Then he says he could never see the wisdom or sense of justice in trying to get shorter hours, Sunday closing, reduction in licences, or in any way interfering with the trade. Well, however little a person does in tem- perance work, by doing that work he is inter- fering with the Trade; even if he only gets one drunken man or moderator to sign the pledge he at once interferes with the publican's trade where that one person has been in the habit of spending his money, as by even one less customer the publican loses so much, accord- ing to what the reformed drunkard has spent in his house on beer or spirits.</p>
        <p>Then" be states again that in this way of working we are using smoky powder, and in using smoky powder we cannot see what head- way we are making. With all the smoky powder, as he terms it, that I use, I can prove to him that I am making headway in this glorious work for Temperance. I have not been .resident in this town 12 months until the new year comes, and at the various meetings that I have addressed. and attended I have suc- ceeded in getting over 120 pledges signed in Warrington and district. He speaks of persons breaking their pledges. I venture to say that if those who are the means of getting them signed keep in touch with these who have signed the pledge, there would in the end be fewer pledges broken. By the result I have gained personally I do not see the failure of smoky powder, for out of all the number I have stated above which I have got there is not one yet who has broken his pledge. He seems very sore about creating a strenuous opposition from the moderator. Personally, I am not afraid of the strongest opposition the enemy can bring against me, for when I was a soldier in the ranks of her late Majesty's Army we were taught and drilled on this principle; that the stronger the opposition the harder we must fight to gain the victory, and I am fighting to-day against this evil on that same principle. In the next 'part of his letter, in my judg- ment, he contradicts his own statements, for he caller the policy of Local Option, which we advocate, a sneaking policy, with total abolition before it is due. Well is he not aware that if he created, as he suggests, a temperance suburb, where there had to 'be' no drink trade, there would have to be not only Local Option, but total prohibition passed by law, or the brewer. seeing that the population was sufficient, would buy up a plot of land in that area? Therefore, although he gives this policy such a bad name, yet he himself, as a total abstainer, would have to vote for it in his temperance suburbs. He says the opposition would cease from the moderator if we took a more fair and reason- able stand, as the moderator would see to it that his fellow-worker, the barman, should have his seventh day rest, like himself (what about the drunkard?). No, my dear friend. the moderator does not go to the public-house on the Sunday because of our strenuous opposition, but because he wants his -glass of beer, and when he has drunk it he never for a moment thinks that it is for such as him that the bar- man has to work on Sunday. Taking into con- sideration the evil effects bf the drink trade, I do not see why a total abstainer should say that our policy is either unwise or unjust.</p>
        <p>J. W. BRADLEY, 12, Gandy's-terrace, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Sir,—You will doubtless permit me to reply to the remarks passed by Mr. J. W. Mealor, in your issue of the 20th instant, with reference to the above. With unique inconsistency, your correspondent expresses the wish that I might have enumerated some of the benefits which would accrue to abstainers from the_establish- ment of " temperance suburbs." In any case, these, I believe, are fairly apparent; but surely the foundation of these suburbs would not be advocated by .my friend unless all parties con-- cerned were to reap some benefit, the fact always being borne in mind that no blame could be attached to any of those parties. lii con- trary circumstances, might I ask what methods would be employed in order to prevail upon so great numbers to be put to the trouble and expense involved by removal without the receipt of any recompense therefor? To treat those temperate in such a manner would only be to deaden all amicable interest in the movement, for the populace would be certain to resent so drastic an action. The-young people—so runs a passage in my friend's letter—would have food for reflection when they perceived so marked a difference in one part of the town from the other part. Undoubtedly they would, but I question whether they would trouble to devour that food; for, of course, all children would be living in the regions of sobriety, being therefore remote from temptation. (That, by the way, may be classified amongst the benefits which would fall to the lot of the abstainer from the "temperance suburb." He would have the pleasure of watching the growth and subsequent development of a community every member of which resembled him in the temper- ance respect.) That a child should be com- pelled to pass its existence in the midst of an inebriate community would, of course, be incon-</p>
        <p>gruous, no matter how drunken were its parents. A deliberate attack upon the well- being of the nation, it would amount to noth- ing less than a crime. To say that the moderator would feel "greatly obliged" to his sober brothers for clearing out is to beg the question in a ridiculous manner, for he would have been subjected to one of the most degrading and humiliating insults imag- inable. If we consider for a moment, the incontest- able fact will be recollected that the great majority of our society leaders, as well as many who hold important public positions, would be excluded from the company of the sober section of the populace. What would happen? Would they hastily conceal their weakness beneath a cloak of hypocrisy? No! says my friend; they would have to reform.</p>
        <p>"Indeed, indeed, Repentance oft before I swore—but was I sober when I swore? And then and then came Spring, and Rose- in-Hand My threadbare Penitence apieces tore." But why debate? The entire system is so colossal as to be impracticable. In any case, it coul,' only be engendered by an egotistical motive. AQUAM 8180.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>INCREASING RENT AND STANDARD</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENT AND STANDARD</p>
        <p>WAGES.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Your correspondent "Free Lancq " in his contribution to the discussion on "Increased Rents and Standard Wages" is somewhat vague and very amusing. To mix up travelling in the South of England with the sale of private Christmas cards and cheap and expensive motor cars with trade unionism and the minimum wage question, and motor driving with increased retits, is, at least, bringing in a range of sub- jects which has" very little to do with the ques- tion at issue. As regards his own trade, motor driving, if we are to believe! the advertisements,' anyone can be made proficient in a month. NOW he says is it fair to expect that a driver of a cheap car should expect as much wage as a driver of a more expensive one. If a cheap car is going to run on less attention and with less expense, who is going to buy the big-priced ones? In every other thing the cheap needs more attention, and the wise man makes bis first cost best. But is "Free Lance" going to contrast his profession • with the long appren- ticeship of the bricklafer and joiner? If my experience counts for anything, it is -during this period that the inefficients are weeded out. In a recent discussion in the Warrington papers on "Motor Cars and their Dangers," I think ' " Globetrotter " gave us this information: "That anybody can get a driver's certificate if ho has the legal fee to pay for • it." Ido not know if "Free Lance" thinks that the possession of a saw and hammer makes a joiner, or having a trowel and spirit level a bricklayer. His figures of 10 per cent. ineffieients are like his 35 per cent. lower rents in the South of England: too much like "lumping it." Higher rents are the result mostly of two things, increased cost of building materials and dear land. Nobody can build cheap cottages on land that costs 3d. to 6d. per yard per annum. "Free Lance" says increased rents are the result of trade -unionism, and then he goes in for the extreme trade unionist • doctrine of the minimum wage. I wonder how he would fix that minimum? Doubtless low enough.</p>
        <p>As to his idea of -the sale of Christmas cards, is it only confined to trade unionists? If any- one wants to earn an extra •shilling, what has it got to do with "Free- Lance"?. I".inthing. And, as far as I can make out, neither has his letter anything to do with the question under 'which he writes.</p>
        <p>FREER LANCE.</p>
        <p>Thelwall.</p>
      </div>
