      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SUNDAY MORNING CARS.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY MORNING CARS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—lt was with great pleasure that I learnt of the action of the Tramways Committee in arranging to give the men working the trams every alternate Sunday off. They are a splendid body of men, and it is, in my opinion, very hard for anyone,to really have to work seven days a week, and certainly economically, and, in every sense, bad. Although having to go to .Latchford three times each -Sunday in all weathers, I would gladly do without the •trains on that day in order that the men might also have the oppor- tunity of going to Divine service, and being with their families. I think our worthy aldermen and councillors are to be heartily congratulated in removing what is a great hardship, for the existence of which, however, no one is to be blamed, as it is one of those things that sometimes develops from the best of intentions. (REV.) W. H. COCK.</p>
        <p>Palmyra-square, Warrington. [As will be seen from our news columns, the committee have since rescinded their resolu- tion regarding Sunday morning, cars.—En.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>INCREASING RENTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING RENTS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—" Trade Unionist" repeats the same stale arguments. I started out on the question of economic rents, but found it necessary to turn from my course to prove that the average trade unionist is as much an unscrupulous capi- talist as the master who employs him! I have proved every argument that I put forward. "Trade Unionist" has allowed all my -state- ments to go unchallenged. He does not deny that the Union joiner measures his lengths with a German rule, and that he saws and planes his master's timber with American tools. He even refuses to use these American tools to fix a Canadian door. He does not object to Ameri- can bacon for breakfast, Australian lamb for dinner; nor does he object to wear the pro- ducts of sweated labour on his back. He even preaches I" Protection" to his brother workers, and asks "If Mr. Smith is not antagonistic to trade unions why his name is not inscribed on the 'Protectionist' roll." My reply to this is that as long as this country allows "Free Trade" I cannot afford to be a "Protection- ist." I ,prefer consistency, and therefore am compelled against my own, inclination to be a "Free Trader."</p>
        <p>Trade Unionist" now threatens the pro- perty owner with extinction. The, Co-Operative Society have decided to supply the demand for additional houses; upon which none but Union labour will be engaged, and no tenant will be asked to pay any increased rent. This is no new movement, on behalf of this society, as the Grappetihall and Sankey Garden Suburbs prove. After. seven years of ard'uous labour two houses lravhee'n -erected at Grappenhall 'and several at Sankey. Who built these houses for the society? Was Union labour engaged? Are there any Canadian doors,there? Was factory joinerY used in their construction? The conditions of labour were such as specu- lative builders in Warrington insist upon, and I will give to "Trade Unionist" the largest Canadian turkey he can buy if he will prove to the contrary. So mitch for the Joiners' Union and .the. poor champion they have selted to fight their cause. • If the Co-operative Society are in earnest they will have to travel at a quicker pace than here- tofore. The houses built at Sankey are no better than those built in town. All the offers held out to supply capital have failed to attract six purchahrs. It does not matter whether the Co-operative Society, build, or the Corporation; the same amount of capital will be required. If members are to have cheap houses they will have to be satisfied with less dividends on the goods they purchase. They cannot eat a cake and have it. The members of the society get from 4 per cent. to 5 per cent. on all moneys de- posited, and I fait to see how the society can afford to lend this back at a less rate than they themselves pay. They pay approximately 15 per cent., or 3s. 2d. in the .2, on all the goods sold, and if this is an honest profit to the members, it appears to be a foolish policy to invest in property that will not re- turn 5 per cent. The builder does not fear honest; competition, and the society ought to push forward without loss of time, if they can do any more,than they have already done. The people of Warrington are afraid to buy pro- perty, and declare it pays them better to pay rent and invest "their, money in the Stores. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Capital follows high dividends. This is true of the' small investor as well as the large. The Sankey estates would have flourished if people could have made a fair return for the money in- vested. Everything has been done to attract the investor to build on their estates; even the services of the poet have been requisitioned to laud the advantages to be gained by living at Sankey. I have appropriated a few lines as a sample: Come, for the cuckoo calls you; come where the bluebells. grow; Come where the 'brooklet gurgles; come where ' the corncrake cries; Come to the woodbine hedges, under the sun- lit skies.</p>
        <p>Builders have heard the cuckoo calling before; they know him. He does not build a nest for himself or anyone else. He is too great an authority on the ejectment question. "Trade Unionist" even knows him.</p>
        <p>In answer to Mr. Rathbone, I would say that for the length of his letter and the amount of ignorance confessed therein, I have never yet net its equal. Ile says f` I know nothing about rating, but it has attractions." I ao not object tio Mr. Rathbone's interest in rating, but I do object to a man who has confessed to knowing nothing .of the subject under discussion sug- gesting remedies before he has learned his lesson. He says "I should like to take Mr. Smith .to task about that .218 for improve- ments. In his first epistle to the unrighteous he made it ,clear that the. Corporation allows this to be paid back in seven yearly instalments, making ~t 2 us. 6d. per year. And yet he sug- gests that it is fair to charge the tenant at the rate of 5 per cent. on the full £lB from the beginning as though the landlord had paid the .tlB in one • amount. I suggest that justice 'would be met by 5 per cent. on ..t2 us. 6d. the. first year," &amp;c. Why should the landlord have the audacity to charge the tenant 5 per cent. on .C 18 What right has he to be compensated at that rate? Mr. Rathbone- no doubt in his own mind is capable of making very clever deductions, yet he is incapable of seeing why 5 per cent. is charged on an improvement that costs •£lB. See how the "something for nothing" rises • in hi 9 mind. He thinks the Corporation lend their money free of interest. If you put money in the *bank they will give you something for letting them. Even the Co-operative Society pays 4 per cent. to 5 per cent. to the depositors. and claims for itself the right to be termed a demo- cratic institution. But the " bloated land- lord," because he asks for the same return, is Proffered the:scales of justice to be held between the delicate fingers of Mr. Rathbone. Mr. Rathbone says, "How delightfully naive were Mr. Smith's concluding remarks, since it is not by enterprise they make profits, but by simply running their works night and day, and thus dodging. the rate collector, and the run-</p>
        <p>fling night and day trebles the output." I ask I each reader to answer the folloWing questions. for himself. Do the masters run their works night and day so that the worker may take eight hours to do what, before night turn was intro- duced, took four hours? Does this system of tax evasion turn out more work? Would it be cheaper for the manufacturer to double his plant and increase the size of his works? Again, Mr. Rathbone says, "I am interested in the rating and the assessment of house pro- perty." I am interested also in the rating of works, because I see companies put down on their balance sheets the value of their works at thousands of pounds above the value they accept for rating purposes. There are firms in Warrington which (if they paid rates according to the valuation they themselves have made) would be rated at treble what they now pay; works that threaten the people with starvation if they are interfered- with. "We will leave the town," they say, "unless you let us have o-ic own way." "Twenty per cent. philanthropist's." If Mr. Rathbone knows of any particular company producing n balance sheet and one that works -day-turn only, and will send me that balance sheet I shall be pleased to show him how a place rented at a hundred pounds may, by working night and day, double its out- put. I will also explain why that .firm should pay more in rates. -• Mr. Ratlilmine says: If Mr. Smith can double the output by working night and day, there are lots of firms who would be glad to engage him and thus save him the trouble and worry of his property." In answer to this ,I say a little influence is of far more servibe to a man looking for a post than the 'ability to run works night and day, or, to see why five per cent, • should be charged on improvements that cost .RlB. There are directors Of companies who prefer to receive their • dividends tacked on to their salaries rather than let people know; how much dividend they could pay if. they only tried, and directors who are such, not as a result of their abilities, but because they are members of a family of owners. It is better to keep their affairs within a small circle, so that they may the more easily dodge the rate and tax collec- tor. 'or they do dodge and .eVade ,payment of their just share of taxes.</p>
        <p>How gratifying, especially to property owners, is Mr. Eathbone's concluding remark. "Watch tilt) Chancellor of the Exchequer," hc says. A full measure of joy and defiance he conveys in those few words. They vomit threats of fur- ther taxation. How much land and property does he own? If he had acquired, by'his ability and enterprise, a few rows of property, what a soul-inspiring protest he would raise! Threats would melt away into human sympathy. He would argue that the load already- borne by the builder and small property owner must be lightened. He would say, with loud acclama- tion, that the Chancellor of the Exchequer was right when he promised to reduce that burden. I. do not hesitate, to say the Chancellor would strongly disapprove of his name being used as an incentive to taunt the building section of society.</p>
        <p>- - In conclusion, it is well to remember the note of warning struck by the Premier in his recent speech, namely,- "taxing of "all incomes; yea, wait and see."</p>
        <p>"Fair Rent" has selected the wrong nom-de-, plume. I suggest the mores appropriate title of "Free Rent." It is not necessary to wade through the whole conglomeratioff of useless patter. One fact, will, or ()light to, outweigh such vapid and uncalled-for remarks. The question at issue is how to recover £1 Bs. 6d. additional rent starting from a gross rental of A:l3, or ss. per week. "Fair Rent" says: "The.problem is how to recover a charge of SA. Bs. 6d. at the least possible loss to the tenant." A cottage rented at ss. per week pays in rates £2 9s. 3d., after deducting the composi- tion; substract this from £l3 and we have £lO 10s. 9d., the amount actually received by the landlord. . .</p>
        <p>"Fair Rent" says: "Let me now give the one and only. way which should satisfy all parties concerned. The rent is raised from ss. to s. Bd. per week, giving a gross rental of „tl4 14s. Bd., rateable value £9, on which £4 2s. 9d. is due in rates, leaving a net rental of £lO lls. 11d. Your readers can now make their choice between a weekly rent of ss. Bd. or Mr. Smith's weekly rent of 6s. 4d." Can they? Now analyse these two statements. When the rent was ss. per week the landlord received £lO 10s. 9d. after naying the rates. By raising the rent to 58. 8(.1 (" Fair Rent's" suggestion) the landlord receiN es .210 us. 11d, after paying the rates, or is. 2d. per year more for a rental of ss. Bd. than for a previous rental of ss: To put the matter in a nutshell, by *he ,alteration the landlord receives is. 2d. a year towards ,the, £1 Bs. 6d. required to pay the increase in rates and interest on, paving. Surely out of the prizes *Fio generously- offered to his friends by "Fair Rent" for the solution of a simple elementary problem that a school- boy could answer without a moment's hesita- tion, he ought to retain the " booby" for him- self.</p>
        <p>To settle this matter, and to arrive at an :unbiased decision, I throw out this challenge to "Fair Rent." He can select any one or two Warrington chartered accountants to prepare a full statement showing the amount :received by the ownei from houses rented at ss. and those rented at ss. Bd. The disputant that is proved to be at fault shall pay the accountant's fees. The balance .sheet shall be Published in •" The Examiner." I .do not hesitate to say that "Fair Rent" does not know what he is talking about. He started dut by saying sixpence a week would pay the landlord: now he says eightpence. I don't expect to couvert him all at once; it is very hard to have to climb down, and it would be harder still if we knew who he</p>
        <p>was. To prove that I am not 'anxious to put him to any expense I have engaged one of the leading accountants of this town to , make out a statement for the benefit of "The Examiner" readers. It is not necessary for them to accept this statement; if they doubt the accountant there is no reason why they should not engage one of their own. GEORGE SMITH. The Limes, Orford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>ACCOUNTANTS' CERTIFICATE.</head>
        <p>ACCOUNTANTS' CERTIFICATE.</p>
        <p>Dear Sirs,— Further to your instructions we beg to certify that the amount payable for rates (in- cluding water) for a house let at 6s. 4d. a week, in the County Borough of Warrington, is £4 10s. 10d., and that the amount payable for a house let at ss. a week is 1:2 9s. 3d. Further, that a property owner in Warring- ton, in order, to recoup himself £1 7s. 9d. per annum, must increase a weekly house from ss. to 6s. 4d. per week, or, say, is. 4d. per week, viz., extra rates as above £2 is. 7d., extra for land- lord £1 7s. 9d., making a total of £3 9s. 4d.— We are, yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>BERTRAM SILCOCK &amp; CO. 8, Egypt-street, Warrington, 22nd December, 1913.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE AND CONSCIENCE_ Sir,—Now at last we know from Where the credulous Mr. liarod Gray atains the mate- rials for his lettars to your open His last epistle contains verbatim aimust the Isl3.cle of a pamphlet issued by 'one of the little group financed in this country, whose aim in life eems to be to interfere in the domestic affairs of Aus- tralia. These good folk quote endless figures about Australia, but they n..-3/n mention the. fact that every man and woman has the ,vote in. Australia, and that every successful candidate at the last election was in favour of compulsory military training. If there had been any real feeling against national service, surely the electorate, • composed of every adult, man and. woman, would have expressed their xishes. No, the agitation is ,left to English sentimentalists to finance, and their partisan statements, Manu- factured for English consumption; impose on men like Mr. Gray. • Let me %give ,one illustration to explain how Mr. Gray has been misled. He quotes one figure-12,373 prosecutions in 'Australia—but does not inquire how this figure is made up. These prosecutions were largely for the neglect to register, and the vast majority of those who neglected to register, live in exempted areas, where, owing to the great distance from train- ing centres, training is not conducted, and -where those Who register are, .on registration, immediately exempt. Naturally, many lads, unaccustomed to a now law, were under the im- pression that, as they were not required to train, they need not register •their names. Bat the Australian people, determined to make the law a reality, in every case instituted a purely formal prosecntion. But in reality,, this skir- mishing with figures, which ,need accurate ex- planation, if they are not to mislead, is rather beside the point as far as national service in this country is concerned. Here is our posi- tion. Lord Haldane states that a home defence army .of 310,000 is essential for safety. We are still over 60,000 short. If the recruits will not volunteer we must, said Lord Haldane, try com- pulsion. If .Mr. Gray believes in voluntary training, has he been 'trained himself, or sup- plied one single recruit to the 4th South Lama- shire Regiment? Then he can oppose national service.</p>
        <p>Finally, although Christmas has gone by. there is one turkey left for Mr. Gray if he can prove his insinuation about our Manchester meeting. Meanwhile we accept his confession that he printed as sober fact a hearsay state- ment which he received frOm a "person now out of the town " ! I am sure that Mr. Gray will be equally honest when he finds that he was misinformed by this "person" and will tender the National Service League his apologies for unwitting misrepresentation.</p>
        <p>B. S. TOWNROE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Concerning Lord Roberts's Manchester speech, I am now able to confirm my statement, contained in a previous letter, which ran: "The speech of Lord Roberts delivered recently in Manchester was printed almost in full by sev- eral 'Colonial newspapers. This nifty have arisen because his speech was cabled out free of charge to any paper that cared to print it." Mr. Townroe's challenge runs: "As for his (refer- ring to myself) delightful idea that we in War, rington, who were responsible for Lord Roberts's Manchester meeting, actually cabled the chief speech out free of charge to Colonial papers . . . . is absolutely untrue." You will notice that I did not say the cost of ,the cable was paid either by .the, Warrington branch of the National Service League, or any organisa- tion in this country. My contention, expressed in other Words, was that Colonial newspapers were offered the speech in full, without any demand for phyinent. Someone else paid the bill. That is the gist of the whole matter. My informant is the official representative in Great Britain of the Society/ of Friends, who has this, and allied questions, under his special care. Needless to say, he is a man of wide experience and undoubted authority on such matters, and is in closest touch with Australasian affairs. He says: "Lord Roberts's speeches were sent to the Colonial Defence League, ,who offered them free to papers throughout New Zealand -have Papers With the speeches' fully reported." It will be voted that other speeches of Lord Roberts, apart from the Manchester one, may be included in the same category. I await, therefore, Mr. Townroe's denial of this statement, or the belated arrival of the promised turkey. GRAY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>THE, TRUMPET OF j. DEATH.</head>
        <p>THE, TRUMPET OF j. DEATH.</p>
        <p>Toy Which Had a Fatal Sequel.</p>
        <p>The danger of celluloid toys was demonstrated at an inquest held by Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, at Warrington, on Monday. The in- quiry concerned the death of Patrick Egan, three years of age, son of John Egan,_ a forge labourer, 87,. Catherine-street, who dies in the Infirmary as the result of burns. Annie Egan, mother of the child, said that on Thursday, December 18, she left the house for the purpose of shopping. The deceased, along with two other children, were in charge of her daughter Mary, aged twelve years. She had bought the child a trumpet from a shop close by, and when she left the house the toy—was fastened round his neck by a woollen cord. The trumpet she had since been informed was made of celluloid. She was on her way home, when she was met in Scotland-road, and was told her son had been taken to the Infirmary. She found lie had been serioly burned about the head and body, and he did the same evening.</p>
        <p>Mary Egan: aster of the deceased, stated that while she was talking to a friend at the door, Patrick and his brnther_entereiLtlk_e_honse,Thei. had with them a sthall lamp, and the trumpet was then suspended from Patrick's neck. She heard a scream, and on rushing into the iircTien saw the deceased's clothing on fire. Neighbours were called and with their assistance the flames were extinguished and the child removed to the Infirmary. Mr. Jones said warnings had been issued with respect to the sale of celluloid articles. The danger was obviously greater at Christmas time. There was no penalty for selling these celluloid articles, but they could draw the attention of the Chief Constable to the case. Detective-Sergeant Holland stated that he bought a trumpet from the same shop. It was thrown on the fire in Mr. Egan's house, ana "it was gone in no time."</p>
        <p>In --reply to questions by the Deputy Coroner, he stated that the Home Office warnings had been issued in the the, but he could not say that one. was sent to this particular shop.</p>
        <p>A verdict of "Accidental death" was re- turned.</p>
        <p>A new mechanics' laboratory with the neces- sary apparatus for the third and fourth years' engineering courses in the Walkden Technical School was opened on Saturday by Dr. H. Lloyd Snape.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>OUR TRIPPING AUTHORS.</head>
        <p>OUR TRIPPING AUTHORS.</p>
        <p>This is how the "Boston Transcript " makes merry over some of the little slips our novelists make:</p>
        <p>A line by W. J. Locke runs, "He talked inces- santly all the time." O, Willyum Writes Arnold Bennett, "She won 15,000 francs in as many minutes." Pretty long ses- sion. that!</p>
        <p>Another novelist says, "Her eyes filled with silent tears." Generally they boom like billows, you know.</p>
        <p>According to another, the hero "brushed her hair with his lip's." Scarcely an improvement on the old method, we think. Another writer remarks, "Charlotte Von Stern was, when Goethe first met her, several years older than himself." But latert, of course, 'twos otherwise.</p>
        <p>"He rested his feet on the back of a chair and New smoke-rings with half-closed eyes." We've seen it done with the mouth.</p>
        <p>"Marjorie would often take her eyes from the deck and cast them far out to sea." As a caster, old Ike Walton had nothing on'Madie, believe ms I</p>
        <p>"'Speechless with horror and lothing, I -t-atrertalrielfirtB-rttragarnsti-trie•itanb;'"----we believe that the mess was cleaned up in the next chapter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>LONGEVITY.</head>
        <p>LONGEVITY.</p>
        <p>It is generally maintained that WC live longer than the ancients did. But researches among the tombs of Roman times in Italy and Spain are lur from proving this. There was certainly a larger infant mortality, but the. expectation of .life between fifty and sixty in ancient Rome Was equal to our own, and after sixty the Romans had the advantage. Soldiers, in par- ticular, lived to an extreme old age, in some cases reaching 100 years. The longevity of the Romans was due to a high infant mortality. Only the. fit survived. By taking great care of the young we have reduced this mortality, but at the same time We have not added to the aver- age length of life. This is not difficult to under- stand, for if the weakly and the delicate survive childhood, they do not as a rule live to old .age. A high.rate of infant mortality means in fact a low death-rate after sixty, and vice-versa. The ancient Roman had a less chance of living to twenty, but if he survived he had a greater chance of living to 100 than we have.</p>
      </div>
