      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>FEEDING FOR EGGS.</head>
        <p>FEEDING FOR EGGS.</p>
        <p>Some Advice to the Amateur Poultry</p>
        <p>Farmer.</p>
        <p>A VALUABLE CROSS BRED.</p>
        <p>The question of condition for laying is an ust now. Because a hen may important one i be too fat to lay, it does not necessarily follow that sire should be poor. But there .is a happy medium, erring, however, on the side of gener- osity, and to attain that happy medium you must see that the birds have plenty to eat and plenty of exercise. Food without exercise will merely make them fat and unfit, arid exercise without food will not produce eggs. Then, again, the food must be good, and it must be given judiciously. It is no use giving a hearty meal of warm soft stuff early in the morning. Its effect will soon pass, but if half a meal of soft stuff is given, followed by the other portion of hard corn scattered among the litter the effect will be lasting and produc- tive,</p>
        <p>PREJUDICE AGAINST INCUBATORS. A prejudice exists in the minds of some poul- try-keepers against the use of an incubator, under the impression that chickens hatched ar. • tificially are not so robust, nor do they develop so rapidly, as those brought out in the natural way. One ounpe of practical experience is worth a ton of theory, and in this connection we may say that we have tried both systems very evtensively during the las Mew years, and provided that a reliable machine is used, and that it is properly attended to, there is noth- ing whatever to choose between artificially and naturally hatched chickens.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>HOUDANS AND LEGHORNS.</head>
        <p>HOUDANS AND LEGHORNS.</p>
        <p>Among the flop-sitting races there is no cross of greater value than that of a Houdan male with white Leghorn hens. In reversing mat- ters—that is, a white ' ,Leghorn male with Houdan hens—infertile eggs, are more numer- ous. This is somewhat difficult to account for, i unless it be that the cock takes exception to the , crest. Be this, however, as it may, the first-, named is a better cross. The Houdan is a , fairly old bushed breed, and, so far as can be gathered, has existed for upwards of a hun- dred and fifty years. It is consequently so strong in its prepotency that the type is truly reproduced in the chickens of any breed with which it may be mated. An objection to the Houdan pure is the large crest, of feathers on the head. This is liable to make it somewhat subject to cold, owing to the amount of water it retains during the wet weather. A very serious objection to the Leghorn, and one that is almost insurmountable is the diffi- culty of lucratively disposing of the cockerels —and there is usuallya preponderance of.them. Both these respective objections may be over- come by crossing, since the Leghorn blood will somewhat lessen the size of crest, and the Houdan will contribute its share of improve- ment by adding to tlie edible qualities. The Houdan is the best of the non-sitters on the table, and thus the surplus cockerels will realise an enhanced "price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CURSE OF FEATHER-EATING.</head>
        <p>THE CURSE OF FEATHER-EATING.</p>
        <p>This extremely troublesome and objection- able habit is common among poultry that are penned in close confinement,' hence smaller poultry-keepers are called upon to deal with 444 he trouble more frequently than are those whose poultry have a wider range. But con- fined runs are not alone at fault, for the fact is that many different causes conspire to promote the habit of feather-eating. That it is begot- ten of idleness there can be little doubt, and a pen of young chickens kept almost exclusively cooped up, with a natural curiosity to peck at everything and anything handy, will soon dis- cover that the stubs of growing feathers are soft and succulent, and learn to pick them just as easily as a child learns to bite its nails. In the same way adult fowls beconi addicted to it, and generally the pernicious example is set by one or two of these, the rest learning by imitation. When the culprits can be convicted on evidence, they should be at once removed rom the others, and unless of particular breed- ing value, may with advantage be consigned to the cook.</p>
        <p>The irritation of lice or the heating of the skin by improper food during the moult are often also the starting-point of the tendency, and when found to exist these influences are, of course, to be met by appropriate remedies. But perhaps the most common cause of the craving is a lack of sufficient animal food and of the salts of potash and sulphur in the diet allowed.. The treatment then is a complete change of food for a few days, and a diet almost entirely of oatmeal, chopped meat, cab- bage, and chopped onions, with no grain but oats, will be found effective. With the morning mash can be mixed some flowers of sulphur and bicarbonate of potash, ,in te pro- portion of a teaspoonful of each to every ten fowls. As a local application, I have found it useful to dust the skin and feathers with sul- phate of quinine, mixing with it three parts of insect powder to make it o further. The bitterness will help to cure the depraved taste, and continued attention to diet will prevent its recurrence.</p>
        <p>VETERAN COLLIER KiLLED AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at St. Helens on Patrick Higgins (70), who was killed in Ashton's Green Colliery by a falling stone. His mate found him with a stone weighing 4 cwt. or 5 cwt. resting on him. For the cqlliery company the fireman was called, lie said he had inspected this road four days before, and saw nothing dangerous. The road had been standing in the same condition for fifteen months. The jury re-, turned' a verdict of "Accidental death."</p>
      </div>
