      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Filitiii iiiio GARDEN.</head>
        <p>Filitiii iiiio GARDEN.</p>
        <p>There has been -considerable discussion on the topic of disinfecting cowsheds, and we have wondered sometimes why thorough whMwash- ing,—which is one of the best disinfectants, should be so little recommended. For a num- ber of years, says a farmer, we have kept a whitewash spraying machine, and we have found it of great value in promoting the health and purity of the stable. Almost everyone, however, makes the mistake of making the whitewash too thick and heavy. It should be almost as thin as water, so that when first blown on to the woodwork of the stable it hows but little more colour than would water. Then as soon as dry a second coat should be given. Special care should be taken to drench the floors of all breeding pens frequently with hot whitewash. Put it on by the pailful; and spread it with an old broom. Such pens are very apt to hold puerperal infection, often proving fatal tb cow and calf. After thorough whitewashing, other disinfectants can be used frequently with profit to the health and vigour of the herd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>GRASSES SOWN FOR PASTURE.</head>
        <p>GRASSES SOWN FOR PASTURE.</p>
        <p>One would naturally expect the part of the field where the oats were cut green to show a better and inore forward plant of the grasses. It is difficult for us to decide such an important matter as the ploughing up or leaving of the other portion without actually seeing its condi- tion. Of course, no grasses that have not yet made an appearance will come now; every seed has either germinated or, failed. But a young plant like that is capable of such tremendous,. improvement that it is never wise to be too hasty in deciding to Plough it up. It there is a fairly regular plant, though thin, we should leave it, and give a heavy rolling as soon as the land is dry enOugh in spring followed by a dressing of 1 cwt. nitrate of soda per acre at the end of March or early in April. This treat- ment will often put a very different face on such a matter. If, however, there are many patches quite bare of herbage, we should plough up and resow. It is a pity that the ribgrass appears to be most prominent at present, as this is not a very desirable plan, seldom included in mix- tures for pasture, except for poor, dry, high- lying land. Much the same remarks apply to burnet. On land where you can get better species to grow these are practically weeds.</p>
        <p>• A NEW BREED OF PIGS. In the West of England at least some benefit is likely to be derived from the live stock scheme issued by the Board of Agriculture in connec- tion with the Agricultural Development Grant. Bristol University was particularly .successful- in selecting a highly practical live Stock, officer in Mr. W. Nixon. In his tour around the, counties under his supervision he found that there was an exceptionally good race of local pigs generally known as the Gloucester Spots. He was thus confronted with a problem in his distribution scheme, as only pure-bred or regis- tered boars could be used under the scheme. When the, local farmers heard 'of this they promptly began to look around and see what 'was to be done. They were determined that they would not lose their local pigs, renowned as they were for prolificacy, rapid feeding, hardy qualities, and a fine pork or bacon car- case with an abundance of lean flesh. There was seemingly but one thing to be done; that was to form a society. Such was the enthu- siasm with which this was tagen up that in less than a month meetings had been held, a founda- tion life membership of twenty members at £5 each had been formed, and other life members and annual members enrolled. The narne,, of the, society is ".The .Gloucestershire Old Spots Pig Breed Society." , A committee of inspection visited markets and farms in Oloudestershire, and a scale of points and standard of type lias been drawn up and adopted. The curious Part of the establishment of this breed society is that it has been entirely a farmers' movement, and that every promised subscription has been paid up.—" Rural World."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>IN THE G4RDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE G4RDEN.</p>
        <p>Pot bulbs of lilium auratums in, a compost of loam, leaf-mould, a little decayed manure, anti a dash of sand. Place low down in the pot. Plunge the pot in ashes over the winter. During fine weather frame lights should be tilted for ventilation. Little water will, be re- quired, it being best to keep the occupants on the dry side. Remove all decaying leaves to prevent damp spreading. Stir the soil occasionally between the plants. Sweep all leaves off the lawn, as these, if allowed to congregate, do harm to the grass. The mowing machine will not now lie re- quired, and after cleaning and oiling the parts, it should be put away in a dry place., Sweep the grass' frequently to remove wormr casts, and run the roller over when the grass is dry.</p>
        <p>Cit-o-0-0-0-0-0--GO-0-0-0-0-0-043-00-0-0-0-0-000-0-0-0 (1) [ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.] 0 O 0 (1) A CHRISTMAS SURPRISE 0 O 0 0 60-o-o-e-o--o-o-o-o-o-o-o-o-eo-o-o-ao-o-o-o-eo-o-o-</p>
        <p>By ANNIE S. SWAN,</p>
        <p>Author of " Aidersyde," "The Last of the Race," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>It could hardly be said to be a pleasant surprise for Tom Disney, though it was not altogether unexpected. When the head of a firm dies or retires. there are generally some changes made in the place released from his control. Barnicotes was no exception to the rule. Old Silas had been steadily failing for some considerable time before his death. though he hardly relaxed his hold on thi reins of government in the warehouses and offices in Golden Lane associated with his name. His father had started the business; that of wholesale distributor of certain article, in use in most households, in a very small way, and it had grown, as every commer- cial concern will when nursed so assiduously and conscientiously as Barnicotes had been. It was rather by solid characteristics than brilliant ones that the firm had become known; in fact, latterly they had fallen de- cidedly behind the times. American patents, hustling methods, and advertise- ment had left Barnicotes in a backwater, so to speak; and when, a few years before his death, Silas had sold an interest in the busi- ness to a younger man, he had had to stand a good deal of plain speech on the conduct of his business. The few old employees tottering down the hill with, him were of the unanimous opinion that in altering, or allowing. another person to alter, the policy of Barnicotes, Silas had signed his own death-warrant. He was a lonely man and a bachelor, so the changed policy affected nobody but him- self and the few employees aforesaid, who were mentally banned by the new member of the firm. Disney, however, was not old, and ought not Properly to have been numbered with those whom the modern hustling spirit de- cided to fire out. He was the head of the counting-house department, and if his work was solid rather than brilliant, at least his record was not one to be ashamed. of. During the few weeks of the old master's illness, when he came in contact daily with Maurice More- ton, the new member of the firm, he had had to listen to a good deal of criticism of everything pertaining to Barnicotes, and had more opportunity than the others of forming an estimate of the man. He realised that even if he were so fortu- nate as to be retained, the life would be en- tirely different, more exacting, and much less pleasant. Hustle undoubtedly would be the -watch- word of the new Barnicotes. Disney himself was forty-two years of age, and did not look more. His salary in the last five years had been three hundred pounds, on which he had managed with the help of his excellent and devoted wife, to maintain a very comfort- able and entirely happy home in a little house at Streatham, for which he paid the sum of thirty-five pounds per annum. For this he had two sitting-roctras and four bedrooms, and a little square karden, which, next to his children, was the joy of his life. Disney was a rose-grower, and achieved some quite wonderful results at astonish- ingly little expense. He had four children, and as he and Lucy were determined to give them the very 'best education in their power, it can be under- stood that there was not much margin left after the. income wa.s. apportioned. But the Disneys were simple people, con- tent with one another and their lot in life. But as Christmas; drew near—the first Christmas after Mr. Barnicote's death— Disney began to be assailed by a very demon of anxiety. He had a sickening consciousness within that it was going to be his last Christmas. He did not mention it to his wife -.because, loving her truly, and grateful as lie was for her true sympathy and help in all the prob. lerns of his life, she was not in very robust health, and he feared to give her the smallest cause for fresh. - anxiety. The blow he expected on .the evening of the twenty-third .of 'December, when Bar- nicotes was about to close for the Christmas holidays. Mr. Moreton did not _ send for Disney, simply because he happened to be in the private room with the day ledger towards the close of the afternoon. When they had, settled the immediate point under discus- sion, Mr. Moreton pulled the end of his slightly aggressive moustache, and regarded Disney oddly. The two presented rather a coirtrast, for Moreton was short and squat, though powerfully built, while Disney was long and willowy, with a clear-cut, sensitive, face and kind blue eyes covered by spec- tacles. They were near of an age, but More- ton looked older. "Ah, Mr. Disney," said the head of the firm, with a slightly apologetic note in his voice, "you will not be surprised—that is, I think you know—that I 'have a good many changes in contemplation here. In fact, I may as well tell you that I am going, to amalgamate Barnicotes with 7113' former business next. month, and convert it into a limited liability company." , "Yes, sir? " said Disney, when Moreton panted as if to weigh the effect of his words. "I have a large staff at Breakwater Street, and when the amalgamation takes place I think of transferring this business there and letting the premises. I am there- fore reluctantly compelled to reduce the staff—in fact, practically to dispense with it altogether. I am sorry to part with you Disney, but there is no post open to you in the new combine. I thought it better to tell , you now, and to offer, you three months' salary in lisu of longer' notice." "Then I am not to come back, sir?" said Disney;' and if his face 'whitened a little, it was not to be wondered at, for there w..a.s much at stake. "I think not. This is a good opportunity to make the break, as the holidays inter- vene. The only members of this staff who will go to 'Breakwater Street will be a'ord and Whewell." • "Very well, sir," answered Disney, and his voice sounded a little thick as he gripped the ledger and prepared to go. Moreton watched him leave the room in silence, and felt a little uncomfortable. "Poor beggar ! " he murmured to himself. "I suppose he has a large small family con-. coaled in some back street. They always have." Disney felt a strange disinclination to see or speak to anybody that day. To discuss what had happened, even with those who were to share a like experience, was im- possible. He sat down at his desk and tried to collect his thoughts, but the thing in front of him was like a blank wall, rising higher and higher until it shut out the sky. Disney had no illusions about himself, his marketable qualities or his future. Though a quiet man, he was not unobservant, and he was fully aware that his training and experience would count for little in the busi- ness -world. Tho mark of Barnicotes, so to speak, was upon him. Perhow there had been some disappoint- ment in Barnicotes that the old man had left no recognition of his old employees in his will. The actual suss of money he had left had been duly quoted in the news- papers, but of its disposition nothing seemed to be known. . It was thought probable that most of it would pi to charitable purposes, chiefly in the direction of orphan homes, in which he had been interested in his lifetime, having been early orphaned himself; he had a particular kind of sympathy with those for whom no man or woman was responsible. How to tell Lucy was the query looming large in front of Tom Disney for the rest of that dismal afternoon.</p>
        <p>THE BEST PROOF of genuine value is an increasing sale. Mothers, daughters and grand- daughters have used Steedman's Powders, which still stand as EL./ unrivalled medicine for children. Observe the EE in Steedman, These powders contain no poison.</p>
        <p>At the appointed time he took down his coat and hat, locked up his desk, put the key in the usual place, and walked out. Boy and man he had come to that familiar place for the last twenty-six years, and the manner of his going did not make a single ripple on the surface of things. The office cat came to him on the stairs and rubbed itself for the last time against his legs. He stooped down and took her up in his arms, and put his cheek against her sleek side, and then feeling his eyes smart, put her down hastily, and took ~the last stens at a run. It was the only good-bye he said to Barni- cotes. He was going to Ludgate Hill to catch a particular train, and had to push his way through the terrific throng attracted by the toy-sellers on the edge of the kerb. He has- tened on, however, without , taking the smallest interest in that odd market. Any- thing in the nature of preparation for Christmas was hateful for the moment in his sight. On one paint, however, he made up his mind. He would keep his dismal secret absolutely to himself until Christmas was over. It was the children's festival; neither they nor their mother should be cheated of what was theirs by right. So just before he reached the turning at the Circus he took his turn with the rest, and filled his pockets to the tune of half-a-crown. The quaint little devices -would help to fill up the odd corners in the Christmas stockings. Perhaps by the time another Christmas came round even penny toys would be beyond the reach or capacity of his purse. Although naturally a quiet man, Disney was not of a despondent cast of mind, and by the time he had reached Streatham be had managed to throw off his depression. After all, he had three months' salary in hand, and a corresponding period in which to find work. Perhaps Lucy need not even know he had been out of work until after it was all over. Meanwhile there were five days' , holiday. Christmas falling on a Friday gave an opportunity for an excep- tionally long week-end. By the time he reached Cecilia-road he was fairly cheerful again, and able to meet Lucy with a smiling _face. Lucy was short ; p, with a merry laugh and a happy eye, but Disney knew that her temparament was easily depressed, and that she would take his divnissal very seriously. "There you are, dear," she said brightly, as she came out of the dining-room to greet him. "All the children are out. Molly has taken them to Clare Barclay's party, so we shall be all alone till nine at least. You never saw anything so sweet as they looked, and Billy in his Eton suit was a perfect treat. I'm sure there won't be anything half so pretty as our quartette at Mrs. Bar- clay's this afternoon." Disney smiled as he kissed his wife. Of eeiirse, he perfectly agreed, as every hus- band and father does when a fact so obvious is' brought to his notice. "When do you go back?" she asked, pausing before on her way to the kitchen. "Have they given you Monday after all? That would be the best day to take the children to the Zoo, because I've got some Monday tickets Mr. Barnicote gave me ever BO long ago, poor old dear!" "Yes, they've given me Monday," answered Disney as he turned away to hang up his coat. "Good!" answered Lucy with a satisfied nod. "I shall be ready by the time you've washed your hands. As we were to be quite alone, and it is so near Christmas, I thought I'd give you a treat." The house was certainly, filled with an appetising odour which had surprised Disney, for their evening meal was gener- ally very, very simple. Motives of economy necessitated that the most substantial meal of the day should be eaten in the middle thereof. -Disney himself invariably dined in the City at a quarter-past one at one of the old-fashioned chop houses where a cut from the joint could be relied on. In her morning's shopping Mrs. Disney had been tempted with a pair of not quite youthful partridges, going cheap, which the casserole had converted into a most appe- tizing stew. She was very bright and talkative all through the meal, and noticed nothing amiss with her husband. Next day, instead of going to golf as he had planned and intended, he went down to the City about an hour later than usual, and prowled about all the morning, making inquiries regarding possible work. The chances were not much in his favour, how- ever, for most of the important houses had closed, and the two places where he was suc- c6sful in obtaining an interview had nothing to offer him. But he felt that he had done his duty in making use of his first free morning, and as he returned to early dinner at Cecilia Road he determined to go down on Monday also on the off-chance of hearing of something to suit. It was not a comfortable experience for a man of Disney's temperament, especi- ally one so happy in his home life, to bear the burden of an unshared secret, but he was determined to keep it entirely to him- self for a week at least, and, if possible, until he had obtained another ost took the children off for a- long walk, from which they returned tired and happy for tea between four and five o'clock. Much of the talk during the walk was of Christmas pre- sents, and he had confided to him all the little surprises in store for mother. Molly, the eldest of the family, was now a sweet maiden of fifteen, very tall for her age, and with an air of demure cleverness which quite belied her merry, fun-loving dispositio•n. She was the very light and sun- shine of the home, her mother's right hand already, and it had been Disney's hope to be able to keep her there, and not, send her out into the world to earn her own Hiring. She had sometimes talked of going to the City to learn typewriting and shorthand, but Disney had a. curious shrinking from letting, his sweet girl do that. He was old-fashioned in some directions, besides having some knowledge of the hardships many business girls have to endure. Molly noticed that her father was much quieter than usual, and while the boys were bounding on in front with Nipper, - The fox terrier who loved a ramble on the Common beyond everything, she slipped her hand in</p>
        <p>Ilmshouse</p>
        <p>"Aren't you very well, daddy?" she asked wistfully. "Quite,, darling," answered Disney with a start. "Why •do you ask that?" "I thought you looked tired, and you don't talk much. Of course, the boys have been chattering enough. Don't you think We are going to have a lovely Christmas? Mummy has such a surprise for you." "Has she?" asked Disney with a smile. "Now I wonder what that can be?" "I mustn't tell; you have got to wait and see," she said rather archly. "Daddy, 1 do wish we could give mummy some new fura. Hers are so shabby. I saved up all I could, but it wasn't nearly enough. I know the kind she wants, but there isn't time to get .them now." No, darling; we shall have to wait another year. Perhaps by then we shall be able, if we all club together, to buy her something really handsome, and worth having. I'll bear it in mind, and put away a 'little in a private box for the purpose." "Oh, that would be a lovely plan; and if you let me know where the box is kept, daddy, we'll put our pennies in too, as many as we can spare, and then. think of the excitement when the time comes to open it ! "</p>
        <p>"I wish I could have bought a new piano for you, dear," Etaid Disney regretfully, for Molly's hobby waB music, and she played very well _indeed for her years. "Oh, I can wait for that, and the old one is really quite nice when it has been newly tuned," said said the child cheerfully. She was always bright and contented. Indeed, all the Disney children were, because their parents had taught them to be grateful for, and' to take joy in simple things. On Christmas Day two people came to share the Disneys' Christmas dinner—a little home dressmaker, who had no other chance of a Christmas dinner, and an old</p>
        <p>man whom Disney had known in happier circumstances, and who now lived in an</p>
        <p>It was a beautiful Christmas dinner, and there were plenty of crackers and fun, and even a little speechmaking afterwards, when the old gentleman, as was his invariable custom, insisted on proposing the health of</p>
        <p>his host and hostess.- Then The sang a song afterwards to Molly's accompaniment, and the boys had rigged up a magic lantern en- tertainment in the biggest attic. So they had a, very, very happy day. Dinner had been made at four o'clock in the afternoon, _</p>
        <p>_ because the old gentleman had to get back to his little house at the other side of the Common by eight. Then the little dress- maker had to jourpey as far as Brixton in the tram, and it ''as a very foggy night, so that after looking out 'Mrs. _Disney decided that it would not be safe for her, and in- sisted that she should stay the night. About nine the children had all gone to bed, and the husband and wife were taking a look at the new den, which had been fur- nished as a Christmas surprise for the head of the house. It was the little morning- room which opened on the garden (you never saw a more surprising house for thirty-five pounds), and the children had all out-grown this little room where they used to keep their toys. It was very cheaply but prettily furnished, and Mrs. Disney had been saving up for two whole years for it. A little bookcase with his favourite books was there, and a big, comfortable easy chair, and a round table for the lamp, and a pair of heavy curtains which Molly had embroidered to keep out the draught from 'the French window. Disney's eyes had filled when they led him triumphantly into it at breakfast-time that morning, and he had not bt.en able to 'say 'anything for quite a while. Lucy thought she had never seen him quite so easily moved at Christmas before, but on the whole it was a very happy one, and not disappointing in the Least. The turkey was done to a turn, the pudding full of more surprises than usual, and the mincepies as light as a feather. S'he was very tired- when she sat down at last, smiling at the thin, worn face of the little dressmaker, who was so grateful for her invitation to spend Christmas in a real home. "Yes, I'm tired, Miss Piggott, but happy. We've had a nice Christmas, haven't we l'om, quite as nice as any we've had in this house."</p>
        <p>"Not a bit better than you deserve nor nearly as good," said Miss Piggott, getting a little mixed in her desire to express her appreciation. "If I were rich I should send big, big cheques to Cecilia Road, because I would know -how much happiness would be got out of them for other people." Lucy smiled, leaning back in her old nursing-chair, with her small -feet in their very smart shoes on the new round foot- stool which the little dressmaker had made for her.</p>
        <p>"Do light -a pipe, Tom; it'll seem more like a real homey-timer I'm glad Christmas doesn't come very often, but don't the chil- dren love it. I wish I could forget about all the poor people and little children who have nothing to keep it on. Where are you going, Tom?" "Cats in the garden, love," he answered, and went out rather quickly. "Mr. Disney looks tired, don't you think?" said the little dressmaker anxiously. "He wasn't so bright as usual to-day. I'm sure the few days' rest will do him good." "I'm sure it will, and he has till Tuesday, thank goodness! I think we'll all go early to bed, Miss Piggott, if you don't mind. Oh, I do hope poor, dear old Mr. Thurston got safely to the almshouses. I should have kept him all night, but I really hadn't a corner to put him in. We've outgrown this house, my dear. Perhaps some day Mr. Disney may be able to give us a bigger one." Disney caught these voids as he returned to the room, but made no comment on them. Boxing Day and Sunday passed without event, and on Monday morning Disney ex- cused himself early and went to the City immediately after breakfast, arranging to meet his wife and children at the Zoo gates at a given time. He had no luck in the City, but forced himself to appear cheerful when he met them. They had a great 'afternoon in the Gardens, and afterwards went on to Madame Tussaud's, where they had tea before seeing the wax figures. On Tuesday morning, feeling distinctly guilty and very low-spirited, Disney went off to catch his usual train at Streatham Station, -without saying a word to his wife. By this time the burden of his unshared secret had be- come well-nigh intolerable, and he made up his mind to tell Lucy that very day. It was another day of deep disappoint- ment and heartache, such as visits a middle- aged man ' when he realises that his value in the world's mart has gone doain to the lowest ebb. Nobody had an opening for a middle-aged clerk; some of those to whom he made application' were barely civil. About four o'clock in the afternoon he made his way back to his home. Like most happy man Disney was very dependent on his own -people, and he- felt that come -what would, he must now confide in his wife. But his heart failed him a little as he ap- proached the door, and pictured her little start of ,dismay and surprise at sight of him. Of course, she would think be was ill, no other reason would occur to explain his appearance at such an hour. He fitted his key in the door, anck when he opened it Lucy was in the narrow hall looking the pic- ture of excitement, her cheeks flushed pink like a girl's. "Oh, Toni, however did you know to come at the very moment you were wanted! There's a gentleman in the dining-room who has come all the way from Gray's Inn to see you. Disney's eyes brightened involuntarily, and putting down his coat and hat hastily, ed' he follow his wife into the dining-room. A young man with fair hair and a shrewd, clever face was standing by the table on which lay some legal-looking documents. "Ah, good afternoon, Mr. Disney, you re- turn at the psychological moment," he said affably. "I went down to Golden Lane ex- pecting to see you there, and they gave inc your address." Lucy did not even ask how it happened that he had not been found there as usual. "My name is Williams. I come from Messrs. Roscoe and Lathom, solicitors, Gray's Inn, in reference to the estate of the late Mr. Barnicote, under which you are a_ substantial legatee." Disney's face paled,. and be seemed to steady himself against the table. His ex- pression was tense, and Lucy wondered at the imploring look in his eyes. "Mr. Barnicote has left to you the faim of eight thousand pounds, _Mr. Disney, frce of legacy duty. t I have to congratulate you. Of course, we knew by the way in which our late client gpoke of you to us that he held you in very' high estimation." "Yes," said Disney, and the room seemed to swim round him. When he came to him- self he was sitting on the sofa, and there was nobody there but Lucy, kneeling by his side, all her wifely love and anxiety written on her. face. "What happened?" he asked weakly. :Have I been dreaming? Wasn't. somebody here talking about old Barnieote's "Yes, darling. Don't you remember? And he has left you eight thousand pounds." "Eight thousand pounds, at four per cent. three hundred and twenty pounds; then it won't matter though I have to wait a bit. Thank God! thank God! You knew I was fired from Golden Lane, Lucy? Did you guess it?" "Not till to-day; and to think you knew all through Christmas and kelit it from inc, Toni! 1. don't- know, now I am going to ioi- give you." He answered by putting, his arm about her shoulders and drawing her very closely to him, "God has' been good, and my faith was shaken, Lucy. It was all dark to-day, how dark you will never know!" Silas Barnicote had done many deeds of unostentatious kindness in his lifetime, but not one ever bore richer fruit than the legacy which came as such a precicial Christmas surprise to the Disneys.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>NATIONAL ROSE SOCIETY'S _LIST._</head>
        <p>NATIONAL ROSE SOCIETY'S _LIST._</p>
        <p>_ The following is an up-to-date list of the latest recommendations of good roses for gar- den cultivation, by the National Wise Society: Hybrid Teas.—Augustine Gninoisseau, Betty, Caroline Testout, Cynthia Fordo, Dorothy Page lioberts, Ectirlate, General McArthur, G. C. Wand, Gustav Grunerwald, Joseph, Hill, Lady Astitown; Lady Pirrie, La Bosco.; Madame Abel "Chatenv, Maclaine Jules Grolez, Madame Leon Pain, Madame Melanie Soupert, Madame liavary, Mrs. Alfred Tate, Mrs. E. G. hill, Mrs. Amy Hammond, Pluirisaer, Prince de Bulgarie, litchi-110nd.</p>
        <p>Teas.—Molly Sharman Crawford. Hybrid Perpetuals.7—Frau Karl DrUschki, Hugh Dickson, Mrs. John Laing. The hybrid teas, it will be noticed, are re- placing the long-popular hybrid perpetuals. The following is the National Rose Society's latest selection of perpetual-flowering roses suit- able for pergolas or other climbing purposes: Alister Stella Gray (N), Bouquet d'Or (Dijon T), climbing Aimee Vibert (N), climbing Caroline Testout (H.T.), Conrad F. Meyer (rug,), Gloire de Dijon (T), Longworth Rambler, (H.T.), Madame Alfred Corriere (H.N.), Paul's Single white (I-LT.), Reine Olga de Wurtemburg (11. T. neve d'Or (N)t W. A. Richardson (N). Rose hedges are Suitable for any part of a garden where a hedge or screen is wanted, and the following varieties are useful: Chinas, Aus-. Map Briars, rugosas, and Penzance Briars. There is the additional beauty of the .bright red heps attached to the briars and rugosas.</p>
        <p>General McArthur is a good garden rose, The growth is upright and the blossoms are held well up. In colour it is a righ bright scarlet or crimson, and is deliciously fragrant. :Blos- soms are produced all through the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LINCOLN'S LOCK OF lIAIR.</head>
        <p>LINCOLN'S LOCK OF lIAIR.</p>
        <p>• SPllle good stories are told of Colonel, J. S. Mosby, probably the only man still living who played a distinguished part in the .American Civil War, who celebrated his , eightieth birth- day on Saturday. He was the most active and successful of the guerilla leaders on. the Con- federate side. On one of his raids liosby ap- proached so near Washington that he could see the dome of- the Capitol. Stopping a woman -who was driving into the city, he-borroWed a pair of scissors from her and cut off a lock of his own hair. "Give that to Mr. Lincoln," he said to her, "with my 'compliments. Tell him that I am corning to Washington soon, and would like a lock of his hair." The cciumission was fulfilled, and the President is said t 6 have greatly enjoyed the joke. Another story tells ,how Lincoln received the news that Mosby had captured Brigadier-General Stoughton and his staff and a-large number of horses. :` Well," re- marked the President, "there will be no trouble in making another brigadier, but how can I replace those horses?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 27, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 27, 1913.</p>
        <p>civ</p>
      </div>
