      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>LARGE ILLUSTRATED CATALOGUE</head>
        <p>LARGE ILLUSTRATED CATALOGUE</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS.</p>
        <p>The Manly Occupations in which</p>
        <p>will convince you of this fact. FIFTY YEARS' REPUTATION FOR VALUE.</p>
        <p>AFIY&amp;PtILES, 34,36,38,40,42.4q46,45. LOPiDOIi /214.R0YM 'CRIIMS: fl/RN/51/INGWYMPOOI. LIVERPOOL</p>
        <p>blue and gold, that is an exact copy of a fabric used in Italy during the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. There are fewer thefts of women's purses now than formerly, when money was carried in a pocket, or the purse held in the hand. The bag is a safe receptable, and that, as well as its convenience, accounts for its uni- versal popularity.</p>
        <p>Lace of Queen Katherine of Aragon. The Queen of Spain possesses some very won- derful lace, given to her by her mother on ter marriage. The lace was brought to this coun- try by Katherine of Aragon, who was herself skilled in the art, of lace-making. Princess Henry found quantities of Queen Katherine's lace in one of the Royal palaces. It had been stowed away in an old chest, and forgottefi/or centuries. It is curious that after sb* many years the lace should return to Spain in the possession of a Queen of British birth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Females Now Engage.</head>
        <p>Females Now Engage.</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS FEASTING.</p>
        <p>With us, the Christmas dinner is Usually re- garded as a more important event than it is among some other peoples, but in every coun- try a distinctive meal of some kind is associ- ated with the season.. Good fare, and plenty of it,. was an all-important feature of our .old- fashioned Christmas celebrations, and it is per- haps still the case that the most vivid imagina- tions called up by the word " Christmas " are those connected with eating and drinking. Happily, however, we no longer attempt to rival the gastronomic feats of our ancestors, and our present mild gluttony is but a pale re- flection of the gross eating and drinking of former days at Christmas-time. Thera were periods when a fat ox cost less than £2; a pig or a goose could be had for 4d; a chicken for a penny; and a hundred eggs for 6d. But wages balanced prices, and relatively Christ- mas fare was not so cheap in the "good old times" as such figures might suggest. House- keepers will appreciate the fact that we live in more enlightened times, and anything like ex- cessive Yuletide feasting is, to a very great ex- tent, a thing of the past.</p>
        <p>• The Woman's Century. That Miss Bebb would fail to convince the judges of the Court of Appeal that she was a " person " and included in the Act that admitted men to practice as solicitors, was perhaps a foregone conclusion. , It was neyertheless a keen disappointment to other ladies, who awaited the judge's decision with no little anxiety, as they aspired also to the dignity of entering the legal profession. However, perse- verance will prevail in the end, and what so many other countries have granted cannot long be withheld by our own Law Societies. • The Convocation of Diirham University has, re- solved to admit women graduates to member- ship, on the same conditions as men, which is another step forward to the full admission of women to all the rights and privileges of a university and professional career.</p>
        <p>A Girl " Ploughman." , Although women may not be lawyers, there are in England and Wales 2,159 female law clerks. A newly-issued Blue Book on the Cen- sus of 1911, gives the figures and also the in- formation that there are 4,830,734 females in England and Wales engaged in occupations. [hose employed as indoor domestics, textile workers, dressmakers, teachers, and laundry workers, account for more, than half, the *total, and at the other end we are given the number of women engaged in such queer occupations as plumbers, masons, "woodmen,". "cabmen," " cleNymen," blacksmiths, shipwrights, gun- smiths, and, In fact, every conceivable trade and calling. We have had a woman "steeple- jack," work which, one would think impossible ,for women, and the latest developments is a girl "ploughman." A young Sussex girl of seven- teen has taken ..up ploughing, and is said to cut as straight a furrow as any skilled labourer in the county. .The dearth of capable agricultural labourers has induced the young woman to take up the work, which she finds healthy and pleasant.</p>
        <p>The Vogue for Plaids. It is neither safe nor wise to predict the styles of either hats or gowns of a season not yerVegun, but materials are quite another matter, for they are prepared a long time in advance. Those for the spring are to be bright in colouring, and plaids will predominate among patterned fabrics., The chess-board plaid is to be much in evidence, but a mixture of colours is favoured, and the chequers are sniall. Cloths, silks, and muslins for the spring all show this design. Many of the new materials are novel, but all are handsome. The finest muslins have velvet stripes in which are plaid mixtures. The new brocaded muslins are very beautiful and made in the loveliest colo'urs.</p>
        <p>Tunics and Blouses.</p>
        <p>Tunics are the most noticeable feature of this season's fashions, and are endless in variety and shape. A new combination of materials that sounds incongruous, but looks well, is a short velvet skirt with tulle tunic and corsage. In black and white the effect is excellent. Tulle blouses are worn much more in Paris than in London during, the winter, for in the former capital the houses are much warmer than in our cities, consequently thinner clothing can be worn. A soft woollen jacket is donned over the transparent blouse, and witlT a fur coat gives the necessary warmth as an outdoor wrap. Blouses are as popnlar as ever, and amazingly cheap in the. shops. They afford a much greater change than a complete dress, • whilst half a dozen can be bought for the cost of a gown. If the skirts worn with them are carefully chosen, the result is smart and satis- factory. • This Season's Jewellery.</p>
        <p>Never has the jeweller's art been carried to such perfection as in this catury. Even the least expensive item of jewellery displayed this year is instinct with originality and refinement. :le prettiest of lace brooches and pins are to bOught for a trifling sum, a-nd the quaintest buckles for Shoes in paste and silver are most inexpensive:. Very charming and beautiful are the displays of semi-precious stones, and many articles of this description are well worth buy- ing. The buttons of jade, or cornelian,•for in- stance, give a note of distinction to the plain- est tailored suit. The green chrysoprase, blush-grey chalcedony, and pink topaz, are shown in delightful rings, costing a few shil- lings only, that would make most acceptable gifts to a young girl, or be welcome to many of her elders. Amber is to the fore, and beads of all kinds are ,shown in every size and length for necklets and chains.</p>
        <p>Variety in riandbags. Handbags appear in greater variety in the shops this year than ever before; in some in- stances windows are filled entirely with them, and, as Christmas and New Year gifts, they take pride of place. The newest sriape resem- bles a pair of bellows, a tassel sometimes quite long—doing duty for the nose. Moire is very much used, as is also velvet, gros .grain, silk, chamois, and, of course, every kind of leather. The fabric bags are daintily worked in little sprigs- of roses, forget-me-nots, and other small flowers, and with their mounts of finely- cut steel, silver gilt or other brightly gleaming metal, are desirable possessions. One firm is showing exquisite bogs made of brocade in dull</p>
      </div>
