      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>IS THERE " GOODWILL " ? At Christmas time, which happens_ on the</p>
        <p>eve of our entry upon another year, we are apt to take stock of our condition and our relationships, and to moralise upon the results that such a stock-taking conjures up for our individual. entertainment. The great central idea about Christinas is, of course, the grand message of • peace and goodwill that CHRIST - brought to -earth nearly twenty centuries ago. What progress has the earth made with that message—what effort is being made to give pradical and concrete shape to a counsel of</p>
        <p>Perfection? If we look around us we see strong , , reasons why we might 'easily rejoice and weep in succeeding moments. The condition of</p>
        <p>mankind has undoubtedly improved; and</p>
        <p>man's well-being has almost universally, and</p>
        <p>especially in lands where the populations are Christial, advanced in a. remarkable degree. In our own favoured land that advance has been most marked. It \ could</p>
        <p>scarcely be otherwise in a country where even in Goalsmrrifs days the peaßent had learned to think of his rights and to venerate himself as man. But the poet's famous couplet, which expresses thought, only gives emphasis to the sad side, of the picture. The peasant • and the working-classes have gone through great tribulation while thinking of their " right" for long and weary ages. They have had a glimmering ,of that "right," which has often stood in danger of extinction. The Education Act, of 1870 relit the flame, and to-day</p>
        <p>we witness a stern, unending ,struggle that is bound to assert the equality of man sooner or later—the good time that is coming, when men will be brothers, and when bricks and mortar and fields and otper things known c.ollectively as "property" will not be able to possess and dominate huinan beings because they happen to bre propertyless,flesh and blood. . The human element will count more and more as the world grows older; man's inhumanity to man will diminish, if not in obedience to the Divine injunction as to goodwill, then because men in the bulk will become more powerful than the small coteries into whose hands undue power and privileges have centred in the past. Of course, if equality and fairplay come by the latter process it will still be open to say that it \vas largely due to goodwill—the goodwill of those who had given the masses the power to work out their own salvation. The' good feel- ing which prevails at Christmas time is a faint indication of what might be "if men were wise and loved each other." After a seasonal dis- play of goodwill. and. generosity—in kind and sentiment—we get back to our old-time hum- drum materialism, and our dreams of future happiness. Still, it is something to have Christmas to show us what might be, and to put us periodically into touch with the better side of human nature.</p>
        <p>So much for our thoughts, more especially regarding our insular concerns.. What of the goodwill among the nations? In Christendom (and in the great nations that appear to be on the verge of eMbracing the ideals of Christian countries) we see a most remarkable sight. In England, which we delight td look Upon as a beacon to which alt others may look for guid- ance, we have increased our naval expenditure in fifteen years from £26,000,000 a year to .E 46,309,000 and next year the sum may be £50,000,000, or equal to the cost of Army and Navy combined twenty years ago. The Eng- lish people do not like to admit that they are under the heel of militarism—a word which, used in its foreign application, is perhaps the best hated word in our language. But here are the facts, and if we are not under the heel of militarism, we are being dragged pretty helplessly at the heels of those countries that are. Germany needs another £50,000,000, and so taxes capital; France needs another £24,000,000; Italy's light-hearted trip into North Africa is to cost her £10,000,000 a year; and Russia is making the most lavish and expensive arrangements to wipe away the ravages of the war with Japan. The United States, Japan, China, and the revived nations of the Balkans are making up all the .weaknesses in their armour. We thus see a world-wide paradox that the peoples, most of whom profess a passionate longing for peace, are taking part in the most colossal competition, of all time for supremacy in armaments. The price of peace threatens to be greater than the price of war has been—and GLADSTONE rejoiced that a merciful Providence had made the cost of war next to prohibitive. If "goodwill" prevailed', and all these millions for war were diverted into channels of social reform, we could almost purchase the Millenium in a day. Internation- ally, as well as nationally, the .ideals of Christianity are at work, and some day, whenl the democracies have taken up the schemes in which the aristocratSes and the plutocraciesl have so signally failed, we shall see the real dawn for which we have waited so long. When that time arrives it will be acknowledged that the light that Christmas has shed in the , darkness of generations contributed in over- whelming measure as a guide to those who led mankind to its destined goal.</p>
        <p>immomMilla</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PASSING WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>No CARS.</p>
        <p>Christmas Day witnessed a Warrington without tramcars or motor 'buses. It • was a concession made in the right spirit by the Tram- ways Committee to a body of employes who de- serve some such consideration occasionally, and the most fitting of all occasions, it appears to us, is Christmas Day. There were some who objected to the innovation. They were willing to prove how inconvenient and even how im- possible the whole arrangement was, but they invariably forgot that more people in Warring- ton live out of the reach of the tram service than live within reach. People who are specially privileged all through the year, it might be argued convincingly, should consent to be placed on a level with 'the rest of the town once a year in order to do an act of justice to a de- serving body of men. Trammen, we have every reason to believe, are capable of enjoy- ing a happy Christmas like the rest of us, and we are glad to know the experiment has been tried of giving them facilities for being happy —though off duty.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY MORNING CARS. If the opposition to the suspension of the car service on Christmas Day was not very pro- nounced, the opposition Lb the committee's de- cision to discontinue the running of cars on Sunday mornings was very keen, and was almost immediately successful. A remarkable Situation was set up. bn the one hand the clergy had made representations that the tram Service should, be stopped in order to allow drivers, conductors and. others to attend a plac,e of worship at least once' a week. An influen- tial deputation, who li:rated upon. the com- mittee this week, proved up to the hilt that it was absolutely necessary that the cars should run, if a large number of ratepayers were to get to church on Sunday 'morning. Church- goers, it seems, travel both ways in large num- bers on Sunday—worshippers going from the centre of the town to places in the outskirts, and others corning from the outskirts to places in town. And so, we believe upon ale principle of doing the greatest 'good to the greatest num- ber, the committee bowed to the solicitations of the deputation and. the 'cars will run on Sun- days as heretofore—for the present. What the carmen will do without their Sunday morning service we do not know.</p>
        <p>The object the committee had in view was to give the men an alternate Sunday off. A counier suggestion was that cars should run on Sundays 'every twenty minutes instead of every. ten, which would nave the effdd of re- leasing half the men every Sunday., and would enable the committee to carry out their idea of letting the men off in batches, so that each man would have a holiday every other Sunday. That suggestion has also been put in the stock- pot--for the present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>LATE FREDERICK TAYLOR.</head>
        <p>THE LATE FREDERICK TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>The late, Mr. Frederick Taylor, whose death we record in to-day's "Examiner," was a typical Warringtonian. Born in one of the town's old- fashioned inns, just seventy years ago, he lived a stienuous life, Sin which he did his duty to the community he served for so many years, and to the somewhat large family of sons it was his good fortune to rear. His life was con- temporaneous with the, growth of Warrington, from a small place to its present position among the "great towns" of England and Wales. When he entered the Borough Treas- urer's office, in the middle of the. nineteenth century, he was the only clerk there. He was best known to the present generation as secre- tary to the Gas Department, a department he had served- under both private and Public ownership for half .a century. He was well known in Friendly Society circles, and in this respect his mantle has fallen upon his eldest surviving ,son, Mr. Henry Taylor, who had for some years taken upon himself much of the routine work. In his domestilations-Mr. Taylor was very happy. Strong and vigorous to a degree, though unfortunately slowly losing his sight, Mr. Taylor was greatly affected by the sudden death of his eldest son, Mr. F. G. Taylor, as the result of an accident, and his life gradually ran out, there being no specific disease to which death could be attributed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>TIIE CHILDREN'S INFIRMARY _BALL.</head>
        <p>TIIE CHILDREN'S INFIRMARY _BALL.</p>
        <p>We hope that in the crush of festivities— which are more plentiful than usual, probably owing to the prosperous times through which, we are passing, as well as to the generous manner in which the Mayor and Mayoress are entertaining in various qdrirters—the claims of the Warrington Infirmary annual children's ball will not be overlooked. This ball was begun some years ago, with the 9bject of assist- ing to endow a child's cot in .infirmary. That object was achieved with creditable speed, thanks to Mr. James Henry Smethurst, Mr. J. M. Murray, and a band of workers, chiefly among the teachers of the town, who went full steam ahead until the goal was won. When one cot had been made secure another one was set up, and it is for the maintenance of this that further efforts are being made. Usually the ball produces £4O, which not only pays for the upkeep of cot No. 2, but leaves a fair balance to go towards the permanent endowment of the cot, which, in the course of time, is to be made entirely free of financial troubles like its neigh- bour, cot No. 1. There is a splendid programme this year for the entertainment of those who patronise the galleries, to which admission may be gained on payment of sixpence. The ball i itself—which s always a delightful affair and a pronounced success—will be specially attractive this year. Tickets for the children--whose ages will range from six to eighteen—are three shil- lings each, and are strictly limited..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>CAN NOTHING BE DONE?</head>
        <p>CAN NOTHING BE DONE?</p>
        <p>There seems to be no el-1-d tc-i-t-he—v;ickedness of some people when they are intent on making a living for themselves—and there is no end to the foolishness of ,Parliaments for allowing that wickedness full range when it could easily be stopped. The inquest held this week on the three-year-old boy, the son of a Catherine-street forge labourer, revealed a state of affairs that ought not to be tolerated for a moment in any well-regulated State, to say nothing of a Chris- tian community. The child's mother bought a trumpet, gave it to him, left him enjoying' the gift—and returned to find he had been -;n to the Infirmary to die. The trumpet, it turned out, was made of celluloid ;' it had caught fire, set the child aflame—and that Was the end of his happy Christmas and his short career. Cannot the law make it impossible for toy manufacturers to turn out toys that may so. easily make the offspring of their pitrons into the most horrible kind of victim imaginable? Dealers and purchasers are largely ignorant of the nature of this material, but those who make up the toys know quite I well what they are doing. Warnings have been issued by the 1-lonic Office, but is that sufficient? It appears to be about as useful as to issue warnings to sane people not to hang themselves. We have made the non-use of fireguards an offence at law. Could we not prohibit the use of danger- ously inflammable material in the making of children's toys?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>MAYOR AND THE BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR AND THE BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Last week the Mayor once again expressed q confident hope that the new Warrington Bridge would be opened during his Mayoralty. Memories of past delays and present appear- ances have led many to doubt whether his Worship is warranted in assuming so optimis- tic an attitude. Some of the "deeper" ones say that all may depend upon how many years Councillor Peacock occupies the Mayoral chair. We, however, take it that the Mayor speaks of the municipal year for which he was elected as the head of the town last November ; and from inquiries we have made we find that his pro-</p>
        <p>phecy is in a fair way towards being fulfilled. The bridge, for the sake of maintaining unin- terrupted communication across the river, was divided into three sections. The old bridge stood while the first section was erected. The first section is now in use and Cie old bridge is being demolished. The second section, on the opposite side •of the old bridge, is so far ad- vanced that the foundations up to the spring of the arch are completed, and the spans to receive the concrete are being thrown across. The third section, which will occupy the sire of the old bridge between sections one and iwo, is being advanced, along with section No. 2. Already the old arches are disappearing, and in some cases the piers have been demolished to three feet below water line. If the year 1914 witnesses the real opening of Warrington Bridge, the year 1914 will have reason to be proud of itself for all time.</p>
        <p>• •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>EXPLOITING CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>EXPLOITING CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Our report of the attack made by Mr. John Upson at last week's meeting of the Education Committee upon the. School Attendance Com- mittee for granting exemptions on too lavish a scale to children under fourteen yeas of age, was unfortunately marred by a printer's error, which made him say that the father of an ex- empted child was earning a minimum wage of 30s. The figure was 365., which makes a con- siderable difference. It was clear from. the facts and figures Mr. Upson produced, that the committee are dealing with these applications in too generous a manner, and that, to reverse a famous Shakesperian quotation, they are really being kind to be cruel. That, there are necessitous cases, we all know, but the worth- less exploiter of child 'labour ought not to find it possible to get through in a rush.</p>
        <p>DECEMBER. R is a day of cloud and mist and moist, chill airs A hawk is poised above an arching field on the left. A great gull, moving coastward, flaps steadily over him, without even a "by your leave." 'Weed, herb and hedgerow—all are dewed with silvery moisture. The bracken in the little coppice has turned to a colour for which! have no name. There are many birches in the coppice, and all are leafless. At its bor- ders is a young oak tree. Though his leaves are all of the same colour as the bracken, not one of them is missing from its petiole! The bright and slender stems of the birches shoot up into a sombre-coloured and cloudy mass'of woven twigs. Here and there the gorse which borders the road puts forth vivid yellow blooms. Farther on, however, some barbarian has tired the gorse (unless its ignition was due to natural causes), reducing it to gaunt wretchedness of blackened stem. Next, a little brook appears, its swollen current submerging the cresses wfiich mark the'normal level of the water. At one port in worn limestone, which episode it duly pro- its course the stream flows over low sill of claims with a good: deal of lyric jubilee. At Re top of the acclivity which now occurs, the prospect opens. Southward is dark dopse- wood ; in front, the loom of a distant [own; to the north ranged fields and quiet farmsteads. The red disc of the dropping sun, glinting for an instant through the drifts, lights luridly the saddened landscape. In a few 'moments more shadows possess the earth. ' •</p>
        <p>FAIRY MUSIC.</p>
        <p>" EUREX."</p>
        <p>Christmas Eve! And all around the snow lay .. . .</p>
        <p>smooth and deep and white, Icicles from leafless branches glittered in the wintry light. Peeping from the snow-clad hedges, glimpses here and there were seen Of the holly's scarlet berries, and its leaves of glossy green.</p>
        <p>From a doorway in the village came a little maiden fair; Blue her eyes as summer blossoms, and of sunny brown her hair; With a store of homely comforts for a neighbour old and poor, Started forth the little maid to reach the cottage on the moor.</p>
        <p>" Do not linger, little daughter, tho' it is not far to go ; Yet the twilight falls so early: yonder clouds may bring the snow." Thus in warning spoke the mother, and the child with footsteps fleet, Waving gaily back her farewell, lightly tripped along the street.</p>
        <p>Down the street and thro' the village, past the church upon the hill, Out upon the open common, by the silent water-milt. Motionless the heavy mill-wheel, bound by ice the little stream; All was cold, and white, and silent—here King Winter reigned supreme.</p>
        <p>Soon her journey was accomplished, and bestowed her simple store; Homeward with her empty basket turned the little girl once more. In the distance rose the forest—scene of many a legend old, Where, 'twas said, at certain seasons elves and fays high revels hold.</p>
        <p>In the moonlight nights of summer, or the dusk on Christmas Eve, Sprites and gnomes, so say the gossips, dance and strange enchantments weave. Oh ! if she could only see them; hear the chime of fairy bells! This may be the magic moment when the wood fays work their spells.</p>
        <p>Heedless of the coming darkness and the clouds that veil the skies, Of the tiny falling snowflakes, towards the darkieme wood she hies. Mother's warning is forgotten, but one thought is in her mind— Just to hear the elfin music and the fairy ring to find!</p>
        <p>Deep within the forest shadows, not a sound the stillness breaks, Darkness, too, is quickly gathering; thicker fall the feathery flakes. Soon the narrow track is covered, every land- mark lost to sight; Can she find the homeward pathway through the dark, cold, winter night?</p>
        <p>Hark! what are these strains of music breaking softly on her ears? Faintly, sweetly, come the echoes of the far-off song she hears. Shaking off the drowsy feeling that her senses would enchain-- Hungry, cold, and wet, and weary, she will hasten on again.</p>
        <p>Following, she knows not wither, distant sounds that come and go. Sounds of bells are gently wafted on the breeze across the snow; Still, she stumbles on and on, as in a strange, fantastic dream, Till, oh glad and joyful sight!—the lights of home before her gleam.</p>
        <p>Were they only carol singers, in the distant village street? Just the church bells ringing out the gladsome Christmas-tide to greet? Maybe, but our little wanderer (and she surely ought to know), Always says the Christmas fairies led her home across the snow! E.C.C.</p>
        <p>PEVERIL Pr mi GRATES never fail to please.— Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>UNSELFISH YOUTH.</p>
        <p>Depends Upon Circumstances. Teacher: "Billy, can you tell me the difference between caution and cowardice?" - _</p>
        <p>Billy: "Yes, ma'am. , When you're-, afraid yourself, then that's caution. But when the other fellow's afraid, that's cowardice."</p>
        <p>The Reason. Clara: "May I borrow your beaded belt, dear?" Bess: "Certainly. But why all this formality of asking permission?" "I can't find it."</p>
        <p>Musical. "And are your daughters musical?" we "I guess so," he replies rather sadly. "One of 'em can sing things at the top of her voice so you can't understand a word, and the ofher can play the piano with her hands crossed."</p>
        <p>The Peacemaker. - • Village Grocer: "What are you running for, sonny?" Boy: "I'm tryin' to keep two fellers fron3 fightin'." Village Grocer: "Who are the fellows?" Boy: "Bill Perkins and me !"</p>
        <p>TOicSiiTiffe:§TiV67— - "1 see you have disposed of that fine horse you bought from the city." "Yes," replied the regretful milkman; "he hurt my business, lie had been used for pulling a sprinkling-cart, and stopped at every hydrant."</p>
        <p>Common Sense.</p>
        <p>"Mamma, will you wash my face? Papa says I can't go down town with him unless it is washed, and he's waiting for me." "Why, Johnny! Aren't you big enough yet to wash your Nee?" "Oh, yes, Fin big enough, mamma, but I don't want to. I Can't wash my face without wetting my hands, and they don't need it."</p>
        <p>Appreciative. Mother (at 11.30 p.m.): What's the matter? John? You look disturbed. Father: "I thought I'd give that young man calling on our daughter a vigorous hint it Was time to go, so 1 walked right into the parlour and deliberately turned out the gas. Mother: Oh, my I And did he get angry? Father: Angry? The young jackanapes said "Thank you."</p>
        <p>All Writers. "The Bousmittens are a literary family, aren't they?" "Yes, they have a common genius." What do you mean by that?" "Well, the son writes plays that nobody will act; the daughter writes poetry that nqbodY will print, • and the mother writes novels that nobody will read." "Does the father write anything?" "Sure! Papa writes checks that nobody will cash !"</p>
        <p>- Tiomoorr - aro -.7.6.7...fi1e5m ...OW,' or Imo. •-• A - - - WP gib</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>TWILIGHT.</head>
        <p>TWILIGHT.</p>
        <p>Light from the breaking day, mine own, Kissing both hill and dell, The rising lark, in song, mine own, Gives voice its joy to tell. I, with the dawn, am waking, dear, My heart for thee is aching, dear, A kiss in thought I'm taking, dear, From lips I love so well.</p>
        <p>REFRAIN. But a passing dream, with its love-filled</p>
        <p>theme, Born at night, with day must die; Thus I ever pray each closing day Will lift my soul on high; That thy lips so sweet with mine may Meet' When the twilight of eve draws nigh.</p>
        <p>Shades of departing day, mine own. Fall on the earth and sea; The daisies bend .in sleep, mine own„</p>
        <p>_ _ _ To Nature's lullaby. Yet still of thee I'm thinking, dear, Potions of love I'm drinking, dear, Thy life with mine I'm linking, dear, Forged in a lover's sigh.</p>
        <p>SECOND REFRAIN. Though a passing dream, with its love-filled</p>
        <p>theme, Born at eve with dawn must die; Yet a love like mine will ne'er decline,</p>
        <p>But grow as time goes by; And thy lips, so sweet, with mine shall meet When the twilight of life draws nigh. R. OSSULSTON-RICIV•</p>
        <p>Mr. R. Ossulston-Riche, the author of the alloy": lines, written specially for "The Examiner, is a well-known actor-poet, not unknovrn to the habitues of the Royal Court Theatre. ne has been a soldier, journalist and war corr. spondent, and has had a strenuous and activ.° career; but he has one of those happy disposi- tions that keep their possessors young ffvell with advancing age. He is closely connected - with the production of "Everywoman," and created the part of the Archbishop in "T" Miracle" at the London Olympia.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>, i 3 iiiiii , • . ~ ~....."</head>
        <p>, i 3 iiiiii , • . ~ ~....."</p>
        <p>al ly at</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>The Scarlet Fewr..</p>
        <p>Ten new cases of scarlet fewer were reported in. Warrington last week. The cases in hospital numbered 52.</p>
        <p>Parish Church New Deacon.</p>
        <p>In the lady chapel of the new Cathedral, Liverpool, the Rev. S. G. Stanton, 8.A., St. John's College, Oxford and Cuddlesdon, was ordained a deacon to the Parish Church, War- 'rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Warrington Market Prices.</head>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>The prices at Wednesday's market were Wheat, 4s. 2d. to 4s. Gd. per 701 b.; oats, 2s. Gd. to 2s. Bd. per 451 b.; wheat straw, 295. per ton; bat straw, 30s. per ton; XX flour, 235. 6d. per 2,401 b.; bran, is. per 201 b.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Life and Death.</head>
        <p>Life and Death.</p>
        <p>The births in Warrington last week num- bered 40, giving a rate of 288. . The deaths totalled 22, giving a rate of 15.4. Pneumonia accounted for four deaths and one person died rom influenga.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Accident on the Ship Canal.</head>
        <p>Accident on the Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>Whilst the Ss. Gibraltar was passing up the Ship Canal on Sunday an accident befel Mr. John D. Pringle, the chief engineer on the boat. He was admitted to the Warrington Infirmary, .suffering from scalds on the head and arms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Aid From Theatre Pairons.</head>
        <p>Aid From Theatre Pairons.</p>
        <p>At the various theatres in Warrington collec- tions were made this week on behalf of the fund to brighten the lot of those who were confined to the Infirmary during Christmas Day. Mr. :L Ti. Smethurst and members of the St. Jqlin Ambulance Brigade gave every assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>St. Mary's Catholic Young Men's</head>
        <p>St. Mary's Catholic Young Men's</p>
        <p>Society.</p>
        <p>As advertised in another column the above .society are holding a whist drive, social and dance on New Year's Eve, in the New- Rooms of the Junior Conservative Association, Old Post Office, Market-street. The tickets are is. .each, including refreshments.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Death hi a Theatre.</head>
        <p>Death hi a Theatre.</p>
        <p>A sensation was caused at the Warrington Hippodrome on Saturday night by the sudden rcleath of John Francis Wisedale (71), 42, Win- wick-road. The deceased attempted, along with a friend, to obtain a seat in the pit, but owing to this part of the theatre being full, he went to the gallery. Whilst his friend was looking .round for a seat Wisedale suddenly collapsed apd died. The cause of death was heart failure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Conservative Club.</head>
        <p>Conservative Club.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Con- servative Club was held on Monday. The fol- lowing officials were elected: Messrs. H. Collin, secretary; W. H. Bellian, treasurer: J. Ful- ton-Smith, W. H. Bellian, H. Collin, John Wells, F. Kerfoot, F. Garnett, H. B. Turner, S.</p>
        <p>J. Hewitt, It. F. Anderton, R. T. Burrows, R. Staww, J. L. Davenport and G. IL Smith, cora- initteemen.</p>
        <p>Territorials' Prize Shoot.</p>
        <p>The annual Christmas shoot of the local Ter- aitorials was fired last Saturday night, when much interest was evidenced. • The prizes, -kindly given by tradesmen of the town, were presented on Tuesday evening. They numbered -over 100, and were valued at about -Z3O. The 'first prize was won by Sergt. A. Wood. and the second by Qr.-111r.-Sergt. Allardice Colr.-Sergt. 'Davies worked hard for the success of the -event. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a fair meeting on Sunday. As it was Christmas Sunday_carols were sung. The Rev. T. T. James presided. Mr. G. Wallington and Mr. William Beswick spoke on seasonable topics. Mr. Wright and Mr. C. .Cotton took part in the meeting. Mr. Payton rendered an ,organ solo, "Bethlehem,' in a most efficient manner. The secretary asked for a collection for Dr. Barnardo's Home and the National Chil- ,dren's Home, which was heartily responded- to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>" Messiah " at Bold-street Church.</head>
        <p>" Messiah " at Bold-street Church.</p>
        <p>The adherents to the Wesleyan cause con- .nected with the Bold-street Church were pro- vided with a musical treat on Sunday, when selections from the "Messiah" were given. The items allotted to the various soloists were ren- dered in taste by Mrs. Booth, Miss Carrie Hast- ings, .Miss Emily Taylor, Miss M. Whitmore, and Miss B. Brough, and Messrs. E. H. Pearce -and W. Warburton. Mr. F. Hickman played at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Trumpet Obligato in Church.</head>
        <p>Trumpet Obligato in Church.</p>
        <p>The ever-popular "Messiah" was given at a number of centres in the town .OR Sunday night, and not the least pleasing was that at the Bew- sey-road Wesleyan Church. The selections were rendered at both the afternoon and evening ser- vices, when good congregations were present. The soloists were Misses Hilda Hipkiss, Win- stanjey, Wardle and Hipkiss, and Messrs. E. IL Pearce and W. Tyuan. For "The trumpet shall sound" a trumpet obligate was played by Mr. H. Pearce. The conductor was Mr: W. Itipkiss, whilst Mr. T. W. Pearce presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Christmastide Lessons.</head>
        <p>Christmastide Lessons.</p>
        <p>Christmastide and the lessons to be derived from it was the theme of an address by the Rev. T. T. dames at the Wycliffe Congregiaional Church on Sunday evening, when a large con- gregation also listened with interest to an ex- cellent rendering of selections from the "Messiah." Mrs. E. A. Sanders and Miss May Atkinson and Messrs. W. Booth and F. E. Brown were the soloists, and acquitted. them- selves in pleasing style. Much satisfaction was given by the well-balanced chorus. Mr. T. F. Wallington ably supplied the organ accompani- ments, whilst Mr. W. H. Payton conducted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Talented Blind Organist.</head>
        <p>A Talented Blind Organist.</p>
        <p>An excellent organ recital was given by a [Mind organist at the Parish Church on Sunday lifternoon, before a large congregation. •Mr. William Stubbs acquitted himself remartably . -well, and earned praise from every side. He lost his sight twelve years ago, when he was thirteen years of age, as the result of a shooting .aceident, and has since successfully studied niusic at the Manchester Blind School of Music. Mr. Stubbs has studied composition, and his vocal quartet, "Love not the world." was awarded second prize in, open competition at the Blind Conference in 1908. This piece was ad- mirably rendered by Master N. Davies and MesSrs. W. Dennett, J. 11. Draper and J. B. poole, all members of the choir. The collection WAS divided between the Warrington branch of the League of the Blind and the organ renova- tion fund. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>Scribes Make Merry.</head>
        <p>Scribes Make Merry.</p>
        <p>There was a happy gathering in the Win- marleigh Cafe on Saturday night, when the members of the National Union of Journalists of the Warrington, Widnes and Runcorn branch held their annual dinner. The chair- man of the branch, Mr. J. Archer, of Widnes, presided, and the vice-president of the union, Mt. F. E. Harmer, editor of the Welsh edition of the "Manchester Guardian?' was the chief guest. Mr. W. Mason, of Warrington, pro- posed the toast of "The N.U.J.," and Mr. Harmer, in responding, gave an address on the Policy and aims of the union, and spoke in high terms of the efficiency of the branch. A pleas- ant musical programme was given, in which the songs of Mr. J. Rabbitt, and Mr. W. W. Whit- tle's humorous items featured prominently. Songs were also rendered by Messrs. G. Archer and H. Walker. Mr. T. F. Wallington showed himself a capable accompanist,</p>
        <p>KITCHEN RANGES, Flll GRATES. TlLES.—Large ei IR toek, reasonable prices.—J. S. GARLAND, IT9II. znonger, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>Old People's Treat.</p>
        <p>The akinual treat to the old people of Wr .- rington is to be given in the Parr Hall on Bth January.</p>
        <p>Emmanuel Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev. William T. Jaye desires to thank the Warrington Needlework Guild for a parcel of clothing for distribution.</p>
        <p>Furniture Sale.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank Hickman, auctioneer, is to sell, at 219, Wilderspool-causeway, a quantity of valuable household furniture at two o'clock on Monday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Catholic Young Men's Dance.</head>
        <p>Catholic Young Men's Dance.</p>
        <p>On New Year's Eve the St. Mary's Catholic Young Men's Society give a whist drive, social and dance, in the Conservative Rooms, Old Post Office, Market-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>High Legh Sensation.</head>
        <p>High Legh Sensation.</p>
        <p>Last night, upon inquiry at the Infirmary,•we were informed that Mr. Joseph Skitt, the un- fortunate victim of the High Legh sensation, was progressing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>Conservative Working Men.</head>
        <p>Conservative Working Men.</p>
        <p>At the Executive Council meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Conservative Working Men's Federation, held in Manchester on Sat- urday, Alderman A. Bennett, of Warrington, presided. Mayor Cheers the Veterans.</p>
        <p>The Maor sought t 9 make the Christmas of the veterans in Warrington more cheerful, and he paid a visit to them individually, chatted cheerfully with the old men, and gave them a gift to make their Christmas more merry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Mill Girl Injured.</head>
        <p>Mill Girl Injured.</p>
        <p>Frances Downridge, who resides in Oxford- street, and is employed at-Cockhedge Mill, was admitted in the Infirmary on Wednesday suffer- ing from bruises to the head and body, re- ceived whilst she was following her employ- ment. Her condition was not serious, and she was not detained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Steam Roller Breaks Down.</head>
        <p>Steam Roller Breaks Down.</p>
        <p>• As a Corporation road roller on Tuesday was crossing the tram lines in Manchester-road in the vicinity of the Cemetery, the axle broke, and as a consequence the tram service was con- siderably interfered with. By the aid of "jacks" the machine was removed to the side of the road, where it awaited repair. It was finally removed yesterday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>"Peace Movement."</head>
        <p>"Peace Movement."</p>
        <p>At St. John's Presbyterian Church on Sun- day evening, the Rev. W. Reid traced the pro- gress of the "Peace movement " through its experiences of neglect and contempt, to its present position of honour and respect. He showed that it had not yet reached its goal of success. He described the obstacles to be 'over- come, and the existing strong desires for the fostering of international friendship.</p>
        <p>Platelayers Settle. The platelayers have settled the dispute and resumed work yesterday. The Cheshire Lines Committee issued the following notice: "A settlement of the platelayers' dispute has been arrived at on the following basis: The men have apologised for their action in leaving duty without notice: and, on the understanding that they will wry out the duties hitherto per- formed by them, they will be reinstated." It is understood that the question of wages and hours will be referred to a special meeting of the Conciliation Board.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Struck by a Motor Car.</head>
        <p>Struck by a Motor Car.</p>
        <p>As a motnr van belonging to Messrs. Bennett, confectioners, of Lovely-lane, Warrington, was proceeding in the. direction of :Widnes on Tues- day night, a labourer named Jervis, of Brough- ton Cottages, Ditton, and a pedlar named R. Newman, of Everton-crossing, Liverpool; were knocked down. Information was given to the police, and it was found that Jervis was suffer- ing from injuries to the head. Newman had a fractured leg in addition to bruises about the head. They were conveyed to Widnes Accident Hospital. The motor van was driven by Her- bert Bennett, a son of the principal of the Arm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Toys at Golborne-street.</head>
        <p>Toys at Golborne-street.</p>
        <p>A jolly evening was spent yesterday in the Golborne-street Baptist Schoolroom, when about 200 teachers, scholars and friends gathered foi the purpose of participating in a tea party and distribution of prizes. The nucleus was formed by the collection of 800 coupons in connection with a newspaper toy scheme. In addition the Mayor had sent 60 toys, all of which were handed to the recipients, mainly Sunday school scholars, by Santa Claus (Mr. T. Davis), undei the presidency of the Pastor, the Rev. H. Ogden. The Christmas tree was generously given by Mr. Ben Few. The Superintendent, Mr. Buck- ley, and one of the teachers, Mr. Roberts, had the tea arrangements in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>Firemen's Christmas Eve.</head>
        <p>The Firemen's Christmas Eve.</p>
        <p>Shortly after 9.30 on Wednesday night, the Warrington Fire Brigade was informed of an outbreak of fire on the premises of Mr. J. W. McGrath, bookbinder, King-street, and they re- sponded with a full complement of men on the new motor fire engine. The first aid jet was got to work, and within a short time the flames were subdued, but not before considerable damage was done, part of which is covered by insurance. About 11 o'clock the same night a telephonic message was received from the Pearson and Knowles works that a fire had broken out at No. 2 forge engine-house. On the arrival of the motor fire engine and a full turn-out of men it was found that the fire did not need the ser- vices of the engine, and the brigade extinguished the fire with the aid of a hydrant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Preachers for To-morrow.</head>
        <p>Preachers for To-morrow.</p>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational—Morning and even- ing, Rev. T. T. James. Crolborne-street Baptist—Morning and even- ing, Rev. 11. Ogden. Bold-street Wesleyan—Morning, Mr. I[. Blane; evening, Rev. H. Williamson. Cairo-street Chapel—Morning and evening, Dr. Mellor.</p>
        <p>Bewsey-road Wesleyan—Morning, Rev. E. Morgan; evening, Rev. W. A. Beckett. Christadelphians, Toft's Assembly Rooms, Bridge-street—Evening, Mr. C. Morris, Stock- port.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptißt—llorning and evening, Rev J. S. Chedburn.</p>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian—Morning and even- ing, Rev. W. Reid.</p>
        <p>Dolls ' for 'Warrington Children.</p>
        <p>Many little hearts were gladdened on Wed- nesday, when, through the agency of the Man- chester Cinderella Club, over 250 presents were handed by the Mayor and Mayoress to the poor children of the town. The distribUtion was made in the Gymnasium, 'and the Mayor must have felt proud of the cheers that went forth from the mites' tiny throats at the conclusion. The Mayor said that they were very much obliged to the Manchester Cinderella Club, and they were doubly obliged to the ladies of War- rington who had dressed 220 dolls. Of et:Ante, they, sent dolls to Manchester every year, but they received a larger number of toys in return. He thanked the officials Of the Town Hall and Education Departments for their assistance. A pleasing innovation was the provision of tea and cakes, which, undoubtedly, the " kiddies " thoroughly enjoyed. The following ladies and gentlemen, in addition to the Mayor and Mayoress, assisted in attending to the children's wants: Messrs. Spinks, W. Wadsworth, J. B. Lawton, S. Jeffery, Price, Rutter, Farrar, Hol- brook, Todd, Knowles, Dennett, Mitchell, and Mesdames Osborne, Lindley, T. Locker and Miss B. Forshaw. The following ladies were present: Mesdames Pemberton, J. T. Locker, Ewart, John Wilkinson, Joseph Wilkinson, Bellian, Dawson, Houghton, Langley and Mitchell, together with the Misses' Short, Ewart, Spinks and Finnay.</p>
        <p>• OSRAM and TANTALum ELECTRIC LAMPS at reduced prices.—Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER -27, 1913.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER -27, 1913.</p>
        <p>FOOTBALL SENSATION. 'DEATH OF MR. F. TAYLOR.</p>
        <p>Demonstration Against the Referee at Yesterday's Match.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON JUST BEATEN.</p>
        <p>An unpleasant scene marked the close of the Warrington—Oldham match at Wilderspool yesterday afternoon. During the latter part of the game the decisions of the referee, Mr. Roe- buck, of Huddersfield, especially in regard to his failure to notice the act of one of the Old- ham half-backs in replacing the ball in the scrum after it had been hooked out, and his failure to penalise the player, instead of giving off-side against the Warrington forwards, as happened, seemed to incense the crowd. At the close of play a section of the specta- tors, most of whom were not regular frequenters of the ground. made after the referee as he sought to get to the dressing pavilion. The police, Warrington and Oldham Committee and a number of the spectators surrounded the referee and escorted him to the pavilion through a dense crowd, who, although they booed, never attempted to molest Mr. Roebuck.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>HOLIDAY FROLIC.</head>
        <p>A HOLIDAY FROLIC.</p>
        <p>This incident, which is almost without piece- dent in Warrington, aroused great excitement and not a. little indignation amongst the usual frequenters of the ground. The crowd hovered around the pavilion for a time, but Mn Roe- buck got out without difficulty, and drove in a taxicab to the Lion Hotel for tea, and finally got to the station without any attempt at molestation. The incident, which created a sensation for the time being, was more like the frolic of a holiday crowd. Mr. Roebuck afterwards said that the game had been a clean and fair one, and that he thought he had been right in his decisions. The crowd was a holiday one, and such little demon- strations Might be expecteii on such occasions.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S HARD LUCK. Warrington were beaten by five points to three, and that the result should have proved so is extremely hard lines on the home team. For four-fifths of the game they were in the Oldham territory, yet failed to score more than one try. The game was rather scrappy, owing to the greasy pitch and ball. It was not long before the Warrington forwards got away, and they manipulated the ball right in the teeth of a stiff wind to the Oldham line, where Fearnley almost dribbled it over. Cook got him down in the nick of time. The Oldham forwards now adopted the War- rington tactics, and for •a time the game re- sembled an Association match for the amount of footwork. This style of play was, without doubt, the most effective amidst such conditions. After Warrington had made several furthei attempts to score, Jones took advantage of a nits-field on the part of Jolley and scored. Fer- guson converted. From a fine Warrington dash Chesters scored a fine try under the posts. Jolley failed with the kick, and at half-time Oldham led by one goal one try to a try. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WARRINGTON LOSE CHANCES.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON LOSE CHANCES.</p>
        <p>With the wind in their favour, Warrington were expected to at least pull level, but expecta- tions were never realised. The home men were continually being penalised for one offence or another, and do what they liked could never get across; the visitors' line. The luck was dead against Warrington, and through sheer -good fortune Oldham were able to keep their line intact.</p>
        <p>Cook, whenever Warrington came near, either deliberately kicked :into touch or kicked dead. Other tricks were adopted, to kill time, and the misfortune was that these tricks succeeded. Warrington failed to score, and were thus beaten by five points to three. If beaten they were not disgraced, and have one consolation that luck favoured the winners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>HARD-WORKING FORWARDS.</head>
        <p>HARD-WORKING FORWARDS.</p>
        <p>The home forwards played a strong game. Chesters worked hard for his try, and Clare and Fearnley were often in the picture. Skelhorne also did some clever things. Young and Nicho- las were good, but were not up to the tricks of the other two half-backs. Ruddick was the life of the threequarter line. His tackling was a feature of the game, and once he took down three Oldhamites at one sweep and got away with the ball.</p>
        <p>Dickenson was also prominent in his tackling. Jolley was not up to his usual form. Evidently he has not quite recovered from the gruelling he got in the Wigan match on Saturday.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" GET THREE POINTS. During the holidays Warrington "A " have been netting in points. At Wilderspool on Christmas Day they defeated Leigh "A" by 18 points to 3. At Oldham yesterday they shared a point in a drawn game of eight points each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>RYLANDS' RECREATION v. ST. HELENS</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECREATION v. ST. HELENS</p>
        <p>RECREATION. This match was played• at the Gorsey-lane enclosure before a fair attendance on Christ- mas morning. St. Helens won the tons and elected to play with the wind. Rylands', who were short of the brothers Horrocks, kicked off, and some pretty play by the St. Helens backs nearly ended in a Score. Just before half-time St. Helens scored at the corner, the goal-kick failing. Half-time arrived with the score: St. llelens, one try (three points); Rylands', nil. One of the visiting players being injured, St. Helens coMmenced the second half with twelve men. Rylands', who were not playing up to their usual standard, should have scored once or twice. but eventually Morris was successful in beating the opposition, though the goal-kick was a failure. Towards the end of the game St. Helens were penalised for obstruction. and Warren landed a goal, the game ending: Rylands' Recs., 1 goal 1 try (5 points); St. Helens Recs., 1 try (3 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>YOUNGS WHO ARE OLD.</head>
        <p>THE YOUNGS WHO ARE OLD.</p>
        <p>It appears that Young is a great name to grow old by. It has been found that the Youngs in the English Who's Who beat all other famill6s in the matter of longevity. One example is Samuel Young, the patriarch of Ire House of Commons, who has recently completed his 90th year. Sir Frederick Young, of the Royal Colonial Instikute, is 96. Now comes news ,of the death at me Ballarat gold field in Australia of William Young, who had reached 108. He left England for Australia when he was a boy of 14, and he was one of the pioneer diggers at 13allarat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Scalding Accident.</head>
        <p>Scalding Accident.</p>
        <p>As the result of a scalding accident at War- rington on Christmas Eve, Susan Cartledge, a young girl, of 23, Turner-street, lies in the In-' firmary, and upon inquiry last evening we were informed that she was in a critical condition.</p>
        <p>Intimation has been received at Bacup to the effect that Queen Mary has accepted a case of slippers. forwarded to her by the Rawtenstall Slipper Company.</p>
        <p>CLOTHS PRAISED BY RUSKIN. The St. George's Guild, of which Ruskin, was master, founded at Laxey, in the Isle of Man, a mill for the production of good honest cloths. Any- one needing Dress Lengths, Suitings, Rugs, etc., as recently supplied to H.M. the King, of unim- peachable quality, and beautiful design, is invited to send for patterns to THE RUSKIN HOMESPUN, 110, St. George's Mill, Laxey, Isle of Man; or G. HOLYROYD &amp; ON 110, Station-street. Huddersfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Late Gas Committee Secretary Succumbs Alter Long Illness.</head>
        <p>The Late Gas Committee Secretary Succumbs Alter Long Illness.</p>
        <p>A RESPECTED CITIZEN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Frederick Taylor, who died on Monday at his residence, Green Bank, Manchester-road, Warrington, was one of the oldest officials of the Corporation, and one of the most respected citizens of his native town. Tr. Taynr was seventy years of age, and had been in failing health for several years, and his feebleness of late had been accentuated by the total loss of sight. He was fortunate in the possession of an extremely solicitous family, and his home life, harmonious to a degree, did much to soften the trying experiences through which he passed. The accidental shooting of his eldest son in 1911 appeared to add the finishing touch to the break that was occurring in the health of a constitutionally strong man and vigorous personality, and the end, which came on Monday afternoon at four o'clock, was awaited by his household with sym- pathy, patience, and a display of kindness, fit- ting the close of a fine life such as that which was clearly ebbing away. Mr. Taylor was born at the Ring o' Bells, Church-street, on June 29, 1843, and was edu- cated at the Warrington Grammar School, which, in his days, was free and under the head- mastership of the Rev. Henry Bostock. In 1856 he was appointed clerk to the Borough Treasurer, the late Mr. Charles Chorley, and was then the only assistant in the department. A few years later, on the advice of the late Mr. Benjamin Pierpoint (father of Mr. Robert Pier- point), who was chairman of the Corporation</p>
        <p>Finance Committee and also of the Gas Com- pany, he was transferred to the old Gas Com- pany. When the Corporation bought that great concern Mr. Taylor was retained as chlerolerk for many years, and on the retirement of the late Mr. Paterson in 1888, he was appointed secretary of the department. This appoint- ment he held up to a fortnight ago, and was always regarded as one of the most efficient and trustworthy of our Corporation officials. As a public servant he always felt his re- sponsibilities. The requirements and requests of gas consumers were numerous, and if he could comply he would, but hi" No" was final. Mr. Taylor was married at the Parish Church on March 11th,1869, to Dorothy, daugh- ter of the late Mr. William Hesketh, builder, Church-street, who died several years ago. He had a family of six sons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MUNICIPAL YEAR.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL YEAR.</p>
        <p>Twelve Months' Doings in the Council and its Departments.</p>
        <p>A BRIEF SURVEY.</p>
        <p>Municipally the year 1913 has been rather a quiet one, although the Council Chamber has rung with prolonged discussions on minor sub- jects. The chief topic, perhaps, was the ques- tion of the price of gas and a series of discus- sions devoted to this resulted in a concession to the slot-meter consumers which has been appreciated. Another subject around which much discus- sion gathered was the question of who should be Mayor. The debates were animated, but were held behind closed doors, so the public were. not able to enjoy the torrent of eloquence then poured forth. Councillor Peter Peacock was finally selected out of the host of candidates for the honourable position, and upon his selec- tion the Council were unanimous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>NEW MAYOR.</head>
        <p>THE NEW MAYOR.</p>
        <p>Councillor Peacock was installed with the customary ceremony, and in the course of his installation address, on November 10th, referred to the cause which led him to sever his long connection with Messrs. Crosfield's. That led to a somewhat lengthy correspondence and series of interviews, which attracted the attention of the newspaper Press nationally. The present Mayor has proved a worthy suc- cessor to Dr. Joseph, who admirably fulfilled the duties for three .years. The popularity of Councillor Peacock was reflected in the great gathering, representative of every section of the community, which accompanied him to the Parish Church on Mayor's Sunday. Since then he has participated in scores of functions, even to making an almost "royal" procession into Fairbourne, in Wales, where he is as popular as he is in the town of his nativity. The Mayor is likely to establish a record during his term of office in the number of public functions in which he has participated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CARE OF THE CHILD.</head>
        <p>CARE OF THE CHILD.</p>
        <p>An important scheme has been adopted almost at the end of the year, whereby children be- tween the age of one and five years will be under medical inspection. In this beneficent work the Health and Education Committees have the chief share, and in this respect the Town Council of Warrington are leading the way to the general country.</p>
        <p>Hitherto there has been -a hiatus between the years of one and five for which no provision has been Made in any of the Acts passed by the Liberal Government for the welfare of the child and Warrington has been the first to fill up the hiatus. Its efforts are being closely watched by other communities. Otherwise educationally there has been no outstanding feature, except it be the bitter con- troversy over the question of school architects. This matter has now been settled and the neces- sary school accommodation will be hurried on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>ELECTRICITY ADVANCE.</head>
        <p>ELECTRICITY ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>In the trading departments of the Council the most striking advance has been made by the Electricity Department, and to meet the demands of customers an extension costing over £16,000 has become necessary. During the year the consumption of electricity, has gone up 25 per cent. above the amount con- sumed the previous year. There has been an increased demapd for lighting purposes, and the new Metallic Filament Lamps are greatly appreciated by economic consumers. There has also been an increased consumption for power purposes and we understand that the department has been unable to negotiate with several very large concerns owing to lack of plant at the works. This state of affairs is brought about by the very rapid developments, and the Committee are energetically dealing with the matter, and propose to put down a new turbine and boiler of the most modern con- struction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ELECTRIC COOKING.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC COOKING.</p>
        <p>The probability is that more mains will have to be run to the various parts of the town to serve the requirements. Electrical heating and cooking are likely to be advanced in the near future, as these are proving very satisfac- tory in other towns, and cheap rates are being offered for this class of consumption. Elec- tricity is being found cheaper than coal and oil in many instances, and during the year steam and coal have been replaced considerably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TRAMWAYS DEPARTMENT.</head>
        <p>TRAMWAYS DEPARTMENT.</p>
        <p>The Tramways Department has been rather prominent in its unobstrusive way, and al- though full statistics will not be available till the end of the financial year, it is expected that financially a most satisfactory report will be available. The number of passengers carried have increased, and the new motor 'buses are proving a great boon to the districts served. Sc popular had the service become it became neces- sary for the committee to order two new 'buses to cope with rush traffic. The reconstruction of the Bridge 1 as occa- sioned much extra work and not a little anxiety to the tramways officials. A skilled re-arrange- ment of the service Overcame the difficulty. The .Tramways Committee announce over- lapping fares and stages, which will prove a boon to those desirous of taking short journeys upon any part of the system. The stages have already been detailed in "The Examiner." It was proposed to stop the Sunday morning ser- vice, but at the request. of the public this scheme has been dropped.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>SMOKE IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>SMOKE IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>How Other Places Deal with the</p>
        <p>Question.</p>
        <p>"The human element in this firing all falls to the ground, and then we come under the ban of the Court?'</p>
        <p>This admission in regard to the black smoke nuisance was made at Ashton by the secretary (Mr. H. Mills) of Messrs. Reyner (1912) Ltd., cotton spinners, who were charged along with Messrs. Abel Buckley and Co., Ltd., with caus- ing a nuisance by the emission of black smoke. The Town Clerk (Mr. F. W. Bromley) said the limit of black smoke emission as fixed by the local health authorities was two minutes in the half hour. Messrs. Buckley and Co. had exceeded this by 1i minutes and _Reyners by 3i minutes. The policy of the local authorities was to summon offenders on the second occa- sion. It was a wise policy to keep down the emission of black smoke in the .town, having regard to the number of manufactories. Mr. Lees (solicitor for Buckley's) asked the Sanitary Superintendent whether he notified the people at the mill that he was about to take observations.</p>
        <p>The Magistrates' Clerk: If he notified them there would be no black smoke. The Town Clerk: I am afraid we should not have many offences. Mr. Lees said the fiieman at Buckley's was warned on the last occasion that if there was a repetition of the offence he would have to hear the fine. The proprietors had done everything possible to minimise the emission ,of black smoke.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mills said they had installed smoke pre- vention appliances at Reyner's, recognising that black smoke was against the economic interests of the firm and of the public health. They were dependent upon the man who had to stoke the fires.</p>
        <p>_ Fines, amounting to .Cl. 12s. &amp;1., were imposed in each case.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF MR. F. TAYLOR—continued.</p>
        <p>trectsurer, was absent owing to family bereave- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Floral Tributes.</head>
        <p>Floral Tributes.</p>
        <p>Wreaths were sent by Mr. and Mrs. Walton and family, Mr. and Mrs. Gill, Mr. W. S. Had- dock, Mr. and Mrs. R. H. Posnett, Office Staff of the Gas Department, the Rev. E. and Mrs. Powell, Miss Hesketh, Mr. J. Hesketh Wright, Mr. A. S. Wright, Miss Wright, Mrs. Atkinson, Dr. and Mrs. Sutherland, Meter Inspectors, Gas Department, Mr. and Mrs. T. Norcott, Mr and Mrs. S. Taylor, Mr. and Mrs. R. J. Garnett, Committee of the Children's Burial Society, Mr. and Mrs. T. Noreott, Councillor and Mrs. S. Taylor, and Mr. and Mrs. Robert Garnett. Messrs. Garnett conducted the arrangements.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NOTED FRIENDLY SOCIETY WORKER.</head>
        <p>NOTED FRIENDLY SOCIETY WORKER.</p>
        <p>His connection with friendly society work was extensive. As a youth he was invited by a few men employed at the Mersey-street Tan- neries to write up the books of their club, which later became attached to a friendly society. The new conditions did not suit him, and he re- signed his appointment, but the seed was sown. He saw what good results could be achieved in a well-conducted society, and joined the " Con- stitution " Lodge of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchester Unity. In 1869 he was appointed secretary. On January 30, 1910, the lodge presented him with a Spaniih mahogany sideboard in recognition of 40 years' secretary- ship. In 1870 he was appointed secretary of the Children's Burial Society in connection with the Oddfellows of the Warrington District, which has a membership of about 2,500 and a capital of over .210,000. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>many years he was assisted in this good work by. his sons, the late Mr. P. G. Taylor and Mr. Henry Taylor, who gradually relieved him of the clerical work, and left him the respon- sible head of the work to which he was so de- voted.</p>
        <p>Mr. Taylor was a Conservative, but never took any active part in politics. He had a life- long connection -with the Parish Church, for -which he was auditor and sidesman for many ,years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Wednesday afternoon. The high esteem in which the deceased was held was manifest by the number of representatives of the Council departments and friendly societies, as well as several prominent townsmen, who attended. Prior to the interment the Rector, the Rev. Canon Willis, assisted by the Rev. T. Vickers, conducted a short service, and after- wards officiated at the graveside. The chief mourners were: Mess Ts. Henry, Wilfrid, Arthur, Herbert E., and Percy Taylor, sons; Mr. John Walton, brother-in-law, and Mrs. Walton, sister; Rev. E. Powell, vicar of Greenfields, Yorkshire, brother-in-law ; Messrs. Stanley Walton, Stanley Wright, F. Taylor (Manchester), Ernest Dyke and J. W. Dyke, nephews; Dr. Langdale, Messrs. W. Watson, J. A. Brookes, Robert Garnett, W. Norcott (Bold), Mr. and Mrs. Phillip Garnett.</p>
        <p>Meter inspectors bore the coffin to the grave. They were Messrs. R. Lees, A C. Smith- son, Peter Lester, T. Lloyd, F. Peters, G. Higham, R. Todd, T. Watson and F. Andrews. The following were the public representatives present :—Corporation officials: Messrs. T. 11. Spinks (town clerk's department), C. Madeley (librarian), W. H. Woodcock (borough collec- tor), F. Timperley (assistant collector), W. Rutter, R. Price, B. Farrar and W. Bennett (school attendance officers), J. Gray (water engineer), W. T. Flood (inspector of nuisances), J. A. Warburton, A. Wood, E. J. Smith (Gas Works), Mr. M. Nicholls (chief constable), Alderman Tinnion, Alderman Evans, Councillor Craik and CouncillOr Locker (Gas Committee), Inspector Snailham (health department). " Constitution " Lodge, 1.0.0. F. : P.P.G.M.'s William Daintith and Lester, 13.G.'s Watson, Smithson, Lloyd, and Bro. Davies. Ancient Order of Druids: Bro. R. Lees. Rational Association: Bro. F. An- drews. "Admiral Nelson" Lodge of Oddfel- lows : P.G. W. Bennett. Children's Burial Society, 1.0.0. F. : P.G. S. Taylor (president), P:P.G.M.'s F. Webster (vice-president), White- legge, Rigby, Boyle, Holbrook, Norbury ; P.G.'s D. Whitham, Ashton, Wadsworth, Houghton, Wai- son and Kennon; the Grand Master of the Warrington District, G. Higham, also attended. "William Savage" Lodge of Oddfellows : P.G. N. McQuire (lodge secretary). Parish Church Council: Councillors Craik, Locker and S. Taylor, and Mr. E. Hall Coates. Others present were the Rev. E. C. E. Carle- ton, Sergeant-Major Day, Messrs. Cecil W. Loy, W. Houghton, J. Wood, E. J. Hall, H. Hatton, Thomas Norcott, Walter Norcott, H. Walsh, and F. White. An escort of police, under Inspector Forster, headed the procession. Mr. W. Dyke (nephew) and Mr. W. S. Had- dock (gas engineer) were unable to attend owing to indisposition. Mr. John Fairhurst, borough</p>
        <p>(Continued in next Illotumn.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WARRINGTON IN 1913.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON IN 1913.</p>
        <p>The "Unlucky Year" Has Proved an Epoch-Making Time.</p>
        <p>FEATURES OF NOTE.</p>
        <p>The year that is departing, notwithstanding its unlucky "13," was, in more ways than one, locally as well as nationally extremely fortunate. We were favoured as the first town which the Sing visited in his Lancashire tour; we have had good trade; municipal developments have taken place; and there has not been that priva- tion and suffering which was manifest in 1912, owing to the coal strike.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VISIT OF THE KING.</head>
        <p>VISIT OF THE KING.</p>
        <p>Municipally, the outstanding features were the Royal Visit and the opening of the first section of the New Bridge by the King in July. It was, indeed, a great day, and the memory of it will be ever fresh in the minds of those who witnessed the patriotic, spectacular and impres- sive welcome the citizens of the good old town accorded their Majesties. The King opened the constructed portion of the bridge by pressing an electric button at the Town Hall, and after- wards visited Messrs. Crosfield's works. Later in the day the Mayor and Corporation walked over the newly-opened section of the bridge, which has since been used by the public. Pro- gress is being made towards the completion of the structure, although we dare not hazard any statement as to when the old order will give place to the new, and the historical old bridge will be displaced by the modern one-span structure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>AMALGAMATION QUESTION.</head>
        <p>AMALGAMATION QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The question of a municipal marriage with the coy damsel of Stockton Heath has been the subject for many discussions in the Town and Parish Councils. Although up to the present nothing has been decided, next year there is a possibility of the Council considering a large scheme, which will embrace not only Stockton Heath, but Grappenhall, Penketh and Padgate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>OLD BRIDGE RELIC&amp;</head>
        <p>OLD BRIDGE RELIC&amp;</p>
        <p>In connection with the Bridge there was a highly important ceremony in the Town Hall on October 11th, when the keystone of the old bridge was explored, and its contents displayed. The laying of the stone having been a Masonic function, a large number of brethren were pre- sent on the occasion of the investigation of the keystone contents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PARR HALL ORGAN.</head>
        <p>PARR HALL ORGAN.</p>
        <p>Local musicians have taken considerable interest in the discussions relative to the Parr Hall organ, and it is their wish that soon an instrument worthy of the town will be erected, and that free recitals be arranged for the poorer classes of people. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>MOTORS FOR FIRE AND TRAVEL.</head>
        <p>MOTORS FOR FIRE AND TRAVEL.</p>
        <p>Probably the greatest item of importance to:. the Gorsey-lane and Lovely-lane inhabitants has been the introduction of the motor 'bus service which commenced in July, and has since been sufficiently successful as to cause jealousy in other parts not yet served, but their wants may be met in 1914.</p>
        <p>Then we have had the motor fire engine intro- duced and other lesser improvements in the other departments of municipal enterprise.</p>
        <p>ACTIVE POLITICAL WORLD.</p>
        <p>Politically, both parties have shown activity. On July 13th a general welcome was accorded the first benefits of the Insurance Act. Mr. and Mrs. Rowland, Hopwood-street, were the first to receive the maternity benefit, and at a later date they, along with others, were the recipients of handsome presents from Mr. A. H. Crosfield. Local Liberals celebrated the advent of the Act on January 16th, the day pre- vious being the first day of the medical benefit under the Act. The day's happenings disproved the assertions that the medical service would break down, and generally doctors were very busy signing tickets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>PROGRESSIVE LABOUR LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>The Progressive Labour League conducted another successful open-air campaign, and the Tories have had a warm time of it with regard to Ulster statements.</p>
        <p>The Women's Liberal Association reports a successful year and claim to completely out- shine the opposition ladies' organisation. In- deed, the whole of the Liberal and Progressive forces, including the Junior Liberals, have cause for congratulation at the results which have attended their efforts to gather recruits to the standard of Lilleralism.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>MEMORIAL HALL.</head>
        <p>THE MEMORIAL HALL.</p>
        <p>On May Bth the foundation-stone of the Memorial Hall in Bewsey-street was laid by Mrs. John Crosfield, who has given the building to Warrington Liberalism, and as a tribute to the, memory of her late husband. In all branches of the party organisation the efforts have been typical of that energetic and pur- poseful nature of all Progressives.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>RELIGIOUS LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE RELIGIOUS LIFE.</p>
        <p>One great event in the Church life of ths town was that in March the Rev. Canon Willis completed his quarter of a century as Rector of Warrington, during which time about .e 25,000 has been subscribed to the Parish Church and its missions. On June sth the Rev. William Reid was ordained to the pastorate of St. John's Presbyterian Church, Wilderspool-causeway, The Rev. T. T James entered upon his pastorate at Wycliffe Congregational Church, in succession to the Rev. John Yonge, on February 2nd, whilst the Rev. A. J. Chessum has succeeded the Rev. William Cooper at the Legh-street Primitive Methodist Church.</p>
        <p>The marriage was celebrated of the Rev. Ogden, pastor of Golborne-street Baptist Chapel, and Miss Wright. Evidence of Con- gregational progress in the town has been very conspicuous. The Elmwood-avenue School Chapel has been set up at a cost of £2,520, whilst Golborne-street Baptists have also gone in for extension. A new chapel has also been opened at Grappen- hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS EVENTS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS EVENTS.</p>
        <p>There have been no exceptionally disastrous fires during the year. In April there was the Health Week, during which much useful advice and many helpful hints were given. There was the brewery strike and the carters' strike in April. The latter lasted eight days and 300 men were involved. Although the men accepted considerably less than originally de- manded, they received an advance in wages and gained an important point in principle. On March 22nd the "Solway Prince," belong- ing to Messrs. Clark and Grounds, Runcorn, was wrecked in the Ship Canal, two miles from Latchford, through a collision with the "Ross- more," and traffic was held up for some time. House rents have increased during the year, and the demand for the working-class house has been more acute. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- A—gigantic Missionary Exhibition was held in the Parr Hall in November, and about .000 was raised for the cause.</p>
        <p>_ The closing weeks of the year witnessed a platelayers' strike on the Cheshire Lines. The works in the town have had a fairly pros- perous year, and everything augurs well for the trade of Warrington during the next twelve months. , In the world of sport there were some inter- esting doings. Warrington N.U. Club, although eighteenth in the League, won their way, by grit and hard play, into the final for the N.U. Cup, and were just beaten by Huddersfield by 9 points to 5. This was the first time they appeared in a final since 1905, when they beat Hull Kingston Rovers by 6 points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>KEW GARDENS FLAGPOLE.</head>
        <p>KEW GARDENS FLAGPOLE.</p>
        <p>The New Gardens 150 feet flagstaff has been taken down owing to decay having weakened it. For over fifty years it has been a land- mark in New Gardens, and on the death of a reigning Sovereign and the Coronation of his successor it has borne a specially large Boyal Standard sent down by the Board of Admiralty. The spar is a remarkably fine specimen of a Douglas fir, and was sent to New Gardens by Mr. Edward Grey, a timber merchant in the Canadian trade, in 1861. It came from Van- couver Island, and was then 250 years old.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>STRIKES AND POLITICS.</head>
        <p>STRIKES AND POLITICS.</p>
        <p>Which Will Best of the I</p>
        <p>LABOUR</p>
        <p>" Labor Omnia ' Mr. Cha</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TO THE EDITOR 01</head>
        <p>TO THE EDITOR 01</p>
        <p>Sir,—Some 15 months ago M</p>
        <p>combated the quality of tb means of bettering the conth. working class. Perhaps your</p>
        <p>remember that I took the negative .</p>
        <p>tried to point out where and how the et- must fail in the object of giving the workers better conditions.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes, on the contrary, asserted the affir- mative that strikes would better the workers'</p>
        <p>condition.</p>
        <p>I am still of the same opinion to-day, but what about Mr. Dukes? .</p>
        <p>How the mighty do eat their own words. Mr. Dukes, the other evening, gave an address upon " The need for political action" to a not large, but, I am certain, an influential gathering.</p>
        <p>Remembering our controversy in your paper a while ago, I went to hear why there WIIB any need for political action, seeing that Mr. Dukes used to say that strikes could better the work- ing class condition. For, we must see that if strikes could benefit the workers, political action would be unnecessary.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dukes now says that we need political action because strikes have failed. After smiting everybody hip and thigh who did not believe in what Mr. Dukes, Ton\ Mann and Oliver Crbmwell would call direct action, he now tries to look serious and tell us, in spite of all the strikes we have had, the working class are worse off than ever.</p>
        <p>The people who advocate strikes, he now says, do not take into consideration the fact that what is known as Syndicalism is but a policy Of pulling down, and that pulling down is not progress; that the Syndicalists do not take into consideration the power of differentiating quali- ties; that the capitalist can *exercise influence amongst the working class by splitting the workers' organisations by differentiating the wages, bonuses, pensions for long service, &amp;c.; and—mark this—that the workers are only human, for if a trade union sender offers them a shilling and an employer were to offer them one shilling and threepence, the workers would unhesitatingly throw their trade union over and take the side of the employer. Another fact the Syndicalist does not con- sider is that trade works in cycles, 'and beside&amp; periods of good trade there are slumps, which, vehemently asserts Mr. Dukes, bring wages down with a clatter. It is then, declares Mr. Dukes, that we shall see the Syndicalists rush- ing away like rats from a scuttled ship. iiy the way, is there not a slump in sight? And is it because of this that Mr. Dukes is—er—let us be polite—changing his opinion? There is no need to draw out the agony oil what Mr. Dukes further said.</p>
        <p>And at the time that I was engaged affirming these premises in opposition to Mr. Dukes, there was enough data, and certainly Mr. Dukes prided himself on having a complete economic education enough to understand that he was then wrong.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>UNIONS AND STRIKES OF SECONDARY</head>
        <p>UNIONS AND STRIKES OF SECONDARY</p>
        <p>IMPORTANCE. But, Mr. Editor, it is not to parade my own individual correctness in economic theory that I ask the pleasure of your space. My purpose ii more serious than that.</p>
        <p>Although many persons may strongly disagree I think Mr. Dukes is a very important person- age amongst us. Mr. Dukes is the secretary of the local branch of the Gasworkers' and General Labourers' Union, and also secretary to the Trades and Labour Council. The Gitaworkers' and General Labourers' Union comprises a local membership of 3,540 men and boys. The impor- tance of this union is easily seen by the number, it icontains, and reckoning family connections the action of this union must affect at least 11,000' persons in this town. Mr. Dukes is a far more important person than the Member of Parliament for this town. For all he says and does affects us here in Warrington and not Ulster.</p>
        <p>And now what mainly concerns me is, what is- Mr. Dukes' relation to these 3,500 men and boys'? For undoubtedly he got these men and boys to join his union with the arguments that ke is now engaged in eating. The membership of the Gasworkers' and General Labourer? UniOn, when Mr. Dukes took over the reias, mood at about 200.</p>
        <p>Is he now going to tell them that, owing to general circumstances and their own downright ignorance, which he went to much Nine to make quite clear, he cannot guarantee He mem- bers n better condition of life through *heir union? Or is he now going to tell them what he told us the other night?—that he has visas the discovery that unions and strikes are of none, or only secondary importance now; and that what we now need is one of those enlieit- ened superior persons known as Labour Mem- bers of Parliament. You see, he now says, that Parliament controls the Army and the Navy. and if you come out on strike the capitalist, having control of Parliament, and therefore the Army and Navy, might shoot the workers down. And, he says, Parliament controls the educa- tional facilities of the country, which in no way enlightens the workers as to their true economic position, therefore any enlightened jackass ought to be able to see what is now the most, the only important thing that now matters, is, that the workers must get hold of the Parlia- mentary machinery, and I suppose Warrington must, incidentally, get itself ready to send a working man to Parliament—one, I suppose, who knows precisely when we are writing where just exactly we must place our commas, full- stops, dashes, &amp;c. Those who know the full facts will best understand the piquancy of this remark.</p>
        <p>iNr ;a1</p>
        <p>Ah, well! such is the pretty pass Mr. Dukes has got himself into. Seeing that he believes the workers of such a gullable nature, and see- ing that he has got them to swallow, meta- phorically speaking, the strike argument, he may now get them to believe his new aspect of the world and its ways. Be things as they may, politics and econotnics trouble me very little nowadays. I have developed a very pessimistic outlook upon humanity in general. But this I feel bound to say, that if Parliament gets packed with men of working class connection, with their hair nicely parted, with a cultivated style of speech, and Karl Mares "Due Kapital " pushed up the back of their jacket out of sight, the workers will not get much from that source. The workers, I again say, - must again look after their business. If they want their own houses they must build them themselves, and not go and ask a gang of property owners, under the guise of the Town Council, to build them for them. And so we might go on.</p>
        <p>But whatsoever the workers do, at present my advice is: Don't send working men to Par- liament, and don't go on strike. Neither is,a_ 'paying game. - ____ --___</p>
        <p>LABOR OMNIA VINCIT.</p>
        <p>//</p>
