      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>.r.ORIMIMINNM/.... ~.. ...__ (~.,,:,,; t...,..,. ~. ....„ .6.i. • , .., ' ' ' 414'''' ' 'ITICT PIEW3 I. . • .-.. 1 ..:_,.....„ . ._._. ,_ ..._ 4,...,_ _•„,. . _. ~,....„.</head>
        <p>.r.ORIMIMINNM/.... ~.. ...__ (~.,,:,,; t...,..,. ~. ....„ .6.i. • , .., ' ' ' 414'''' ' 'ITICT PIEW3 I. . • .-.. 1 ..:_,.....„ . ._._. ,_ ..._ 4,...,_ _•„,. . _. ~,....„.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON ii EATH</p>
        <p>TOWNSHIP TITTLE-TATfLE.</p>
        <p>The. " Heathens " are resting now. They are recovering from Christmas effects, And are getting ready for another Glorious oUtburst in the New Year. Most folk sre thankful the festivetide Is over: if only to get rid of the Waits, carol singers, and folk -for Christmas boxes. These have constituted a very large crop. Man) happy "'Heathens" deserted the Heath For more congenial climes during Christmas. Some went to Scotland, some to London, A few to Brighton, and a couple to the Riviera. Those of us with light purses stayed at home. Stockton Heath footballers have made amends They won a great medal-tie very nicely. . Many Santa Claus were seen about Just prior CO the Day of Christmas. A few carried huge turkeys and geese. One was laden with a huge rocking horse. There were all reminders of the season. N "Heathen" has been sent to prison Because he was fauna asleep in a stable. Is not sleeping in a stable connected Somehow with Christmas time, and was Not this chap seeking to emulate custom?</p>
        <p>" British " and the Stable.—A.. Stockton Heath character, best known as "British," but whose proper name was given by the police as Henry Shaw, spent his Christmas in prison, where waits and carol singers trouble not. He was brought up at a special sitting of the Petty Sessions on Monday, charged with sleeping out at. the stables of the Bridgewater Canal Com- pany at Stockton Heath. The police reported that "British" had 40 previous convictions standing against him in their books. Mr. J. Ilepherd, who was the presiding magistrate, sent accused to prison for 14 days.</p>
        <p>Ten Days' Carol! ing,r—The Stockton Heath Wesleyan Church Choir have been out for ten days' carol singing, with a useful purpose in view. Unlike most of the singers, they had not an eye on personal gain, the money they received going to provide a New Year's enter- tainment to the Sunday scholars early in the year. The choir sang popular carols with taste and skill.</p>
        <p>Hockey.—Stookton Heath were without the services of Dolan, Newton,. Richardson and Whittle in their return fixture with Timperley. Last season the result was a drawn game—one goal each. Timperley pressed for the fir'st five minutes, after which play was fairly even, Dunher opening the scoring for Timperley after 20. minutes. From the bully, W. Menzies obtained possession, and, working right through the defence, scored a second for Timperley. Half-time: Timperley, two goals; Stockton Heath, nil. Irt the second half Timperley scored twice, and Partridge scored for the Heath. Result: Timperley, four goals; Stockton Heath, one. Swinton Park are the visitors to the Stockton Heath ground to-day.</p>
        <p>The Bridge of Sighs.—After a ship had passed thropgh the Swing Bridge at Stockton Heath on Tuesday afternoon two trains entered upon the bridge and came to a stop. The electricity had given out. Another boat was approaching, and it looked as if trains and passengers would have to be swung with the bridge. However, an ingenious conductor managed to locate the defect and turn on the current again. The tram traffic, was delayed for some little time. Old Folk's Treat.—The old folk of Stockton Heath are eagerly anticipating their annual treat, which will take place in the Parish Room on New Year's Day. Formerly this was organ- ised by private subscription, but last year it was taken in hand by a committee represental tive of the local churches, and this season the treat promises to be more successful than ever. Old folk of sixty and upwards will be enter- taineji to a substantial tea, followed by a con- cert, and a parting gift will cheer them on their homeward way. The Vicar. the Rev. J. Collins, will preside.</p>
        <p>The Watch of Toy.—Anthony Donoghue, a sturdily built Irish farm labourer, appeared at a special sitting of the Police Court—before Mr. J. Ilepherd—on Monday, on a charge of stealing a silver watch, the property of John Toy, also a farm labourer. Both men lived in the same shanty on a farm at Appleton.—lnspector Breese said that Toy .left Donoghue in bed in the shanty one morning last week. On Toy re- turning he found both the watch and Donoghue were missing. Accused had absconded. Later her was arrested by the Stockton Heath police, and a pawn ticket relating to the watch was found in his possession, together with another ticket for a pair of boots. The Inspector asked for a remand to the Sessions at Stockton Heath on January 13th, so that prisoner might be further charged with the theft of the.boots.— This the magistrate consented to, and accused was remanded until the January Sessions.</p>
        <p>Memorial Service.—A service in memory of the late Mr. W. Richardson, a prominent woken and member connected with St. Thomas' Church, Stockton Heath, was held there on Sunday morning. He had been a sidesman, and also a manager of the Church Schools, and was interred at Warrington Cemetery last• week, as announced in "The Examiner." The Rev. J Collins, vicar of St. Thomas', in the course of a touching sermon, said Mr. Richardson was fur several years people's warden, and it was his indifferent health which compelled him in 007 to ask the parishioners to select someone in J:is place. Since that time he had been a sidesman. He was a manager of the Church Day,School, and as recently as Friday week was present at a meeting. Personally, he felt he had lost a very valued friend, and he knew many others who felt the same, but no one would feel Mr. Richardson's loss more than her who had joined in his joys and sorrows, and the members of the family. "Lead, kindly light." the deeeased's favourite hymn, was sung, and "0 rest in the Lord" was played. The Rev. W. Braceeamp, at St. Paul's Church, Warrington, also made reference to the death of Mr. Richardson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>" MESSIAH " RECITAL.</head>
        <p>" MESSIAH " RECITAL.</p>
        <p>Successful Rendition by Wesleyan Choir.</p>
        <p>The " Messiah " has long been a popular feature of Christmas with Wesleyans every- where; in tact, no church has done more to popularise this charming oratorio than the Wesleyan Church, and therefore it is but living up to the tradition of the past to present it on or near Christmas. The Wesleyans of Stockton Heath gave their " Messiah " recital on Sunday, -and it was attended with great success. The ' rendition was excellent, and the large congre- gations were thrilled and delighted. In the afternoon Part 1 was given, when a large congregation listened with interest to a really excellent interpretation. The soprano was Madame S. F. Felton, of Stalybridge; con- tralto, Madame Lily Fairhurst; tenor. Mr. Levi Richardson; and baritone, Mr. Richard Tranter. of St. Helens. In the evening Part 2 was given with great effect, when the contralto soloist was Miss Ada Peacock, in the place of Madame Lily Fairhurst.</p>
        <p>A shower of congratulations descended upon Mr. R. Tranter for his renderings of "Why do the nations ?" and "The trumpet shall sound." He deserved all the praise he received. Madame S. Felton made an excellent impression. As usual, Mr. Levi Richardson and Madame L. Fairhurst were very good, as also was the young lady, Miss Ada Peacock.</p>
        <p>The young organist, Mr. J. L. White, played exceedingly, well, especially considering that this was the first time he had ventured to play the selections from the "Messiah." Mr. Albert</p>
        <p>Trow was the pianoforte accompanist, and Mr. F. E. Penketh the conductor.</p>
        <p>In the afternoon Mr. R. IL Meldrum occupied the chair, and the preachers for the day were: Morning, Mr. M. Osborne; afternoon, Mr. S. Moss. The special collections at each service, in aid of the Renovation Fund, met with a hearty response.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>LINES FROM LYMM.</p>
        <p>Local Will—The will of Mrs. Margaret Moles- worth, widow of Canon Molesworth, of Ash- field, Lymm, who died a short time -ago, has been proved and shows estate valued at .4,539.</p>
        <p>Christmas Roses.—Co-mingling with the evi- dences of Christmas, with- which the week has been crowded, lingering reminders of the late summer are still to be found in sheltered corners of the gardens of the district, roses, sweet peas, and even geraniums, testifying to the extreme mildness of the present season.</p>
        <p>ChristmA Morning Service.—At the Wesleyaii Church on Thursday morning a united service was held under the auspices of the Free Church Council. Tht service was conducted by the Rev. J. H. Pawlyn, who preached an appropriate sermon. The Wesleyan Choir gave a spirited interpretation of the "Hallelujah Chorus," under the conductorship of Mr. J. H. Brazendale</p>
        <p>and the organ accompaniment of Mr. H. S</p>
        <p>Brown.</p>
        <p>The Carol Flood.—The outdoor musical cele- brations of the great festival have been more numerous than ever; companies of singers, numbering from two to 22, have paraded the streets, serenaded the inhabitants, trampled on the gardens, ploughed up the lawns,. and woke babies, as of yore. Much of this singing could have been dispensed with, as it added nothing to our reputation as a musical nation, but we must except the performances of the trained choirs of the Congregational', Primitive Metho- dist, and Wesleyan, Churches, who, taking ad- vantage of the tempting weather, came into the open, and sang 'a number of tuneful carols in good style.</p>
        <p>Motor Party's Adventure.—On Christmas Day morning Church-brow was the scene of another motor accident. A party of motorists were going towards Broom Edge in car E.D. 26e; and on reaching the foot of the hill the driver endeavoured to follow the turn in the road; but, as it is supposed, on account of the excessive speed at which the car was travelling, he was unable to do so, with the result that the front wheels struck the curb-stone. The sudden check served to prevent the car from dashing into the wall, but the contact was so great that almost all the spokes were smashed and scat- tered about the roadway. Fortunately no one was hurt and the party continued their journey in one of Mr. Willson's taxi-cabs.</p>
        <p>1/777.1:z4i: r...7vvpittlrfarrfflPAr3v,,,,. A Motor Car Dash.—At Manchester County Police Court on Tuesday Samuel Williamson, chauffeur, Blanche Cottages, Lymm, in the .employ. of Xi% Thomas Wilson, of Lymm, was summoned for driving a motor car at a speed dangerous to the public along Chester-road, Stretford, on December 6th.—P.C. Eastwood estimated the speed from 25 to 30 miles per hour.—Mr. T. H. Hinchcliffe, who ,defended, ad- mitted the offence. The defendant was in the employ of Mr. Thomas Wilson, of Lymm, and On the evening in question the speedometer regis- tered 20 miles per hour. The reason he was going so fast was that he had been told to make haste by his master, who was assisting a clergy- man to catch a train at Manchester Central Station.—The Stipendiary (Mr. Yates, K.C.) said they looked upon the act of taking a clergyman to catch a train very much in the light as that of a doctor being taken to see a patient. The driver was not driving fast for the sake of speed, and the Bench agreed to the withdrawal of the summons on payment of two guineas as costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Christmas Poultry.—A story of a Warrington inan's alleged early morning visit to a Sankey fowlhouse was told at the Occasional Court on Monday, when Samuel Warburton, of 26, Man- chester-road, Warrington, was charged on remand, before Mr. Joseph Bailey, with break- ing and entering the fowlhouse and stealing four hens, value £l, the property of Mr. Thomas Cook, farmer, -Holly House Farm; Sankey, be- tween December 18th and 19th. Prisoner was seen at 5.30 on the morning of December 19th by a member of the Warrington Police Force, who noticed Warburton carrying a sack, con- taining something bulky. The *officer ques- tioned the prisoner, who replied that he had only a "few birds." The constable was not satisfied and took the man into custody. Ser- geant Smith, of Great Sankey, was communi- cated 'with, and he reported that Mr. Cook's fowlhouse had been entered.—Prisoner, whilst admitting the theft, denied that he broke in the fowlhouse, for there was no lock on the door.—Warburton, who had five previous con- victions for fowl stealing, was com.mitted for trial 'to the Liverpool Quarter Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PEN K ETIL</head>
        <p>PEN K ETIL</p>
        <p>Children's Treat.—The children connected with the Penketh Council School spent a very enjoyable time the other evening, when they partook of a Christmas treat. About 250 chil- dren were present, and many prizes were given by Mrs. Murray. The Head' Master and Mn. Bell worked hard for the successful result.</p>
        <p>Generous Employers.—The generosity of, the Penketh Tanning Co. was manifested during, the week, when they gave a number of their senior employes acceptable and seasonable gifts in the form of geese, aiid money prizes to a num- ber of the younger workers. The best time- keepers in the works were the recipients, and it worked out that 41 men had lost no time, whilst 24 had may, lost one quarter. There were 12 boys who had always been up to time, and they received money prizes. The donors of the gifts were heartily thanked, on the proposition of Mr. A. Bowyer. Mr. W. llighani seconded, and in responding _Mr. J. G. Withinshaw said he hoped that the same spirit would Always exist between the men and the employers.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Choir Sermons.—ln connection with the Penketh Wesleyan Crurch the choir ser- mons were preached on Sunday. The Rev. G. Oswald Cornish, of Didsbury College, preached in the morning, and Miss Johnson sang "I know that my Redeemer liveth " in an impres- sive manner. In the evening the Rev. H. Mid- dlewood presided at a choral service. In the anthems Miss Johnson and Mr. W. Tucker took the solo parts. Miss Dodds sang "hear my prayer"; Miss Elsie Wilkinson "A song of thanksgiving"; Miss Moston, "Hark, hark, my soul "; and Mr. H. Elliott, "Sun of my soul." Mr. A. Clatworthy presided at the organ and played "Nearer, my God, to Thee," and the Hallelujah chorus. The conductor was Mr. George Carman. The choir funds will be aug- mented by the collections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>GRA P PENH ALL.</head>
        <p>GRA P PENH ALL.</p>
        <p>Football for Charity.—A match was played between the first- ad second teams of St. Wil- frid's A.F.C., the first team running out win- ners by 5-1. The scorers for the first team were Richards (2), Ford, Peters and .Watson, whilst Cameron netted for the second.. During the course of the match a/Collection was taken, amounting to 0423 ss. 6d., which, after deducting 6s. Gd, for advertising expenses, will leave a balance of £2 19s. to be handed over to the Dis- trict Nursing. Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FlEft *Y-FOR-SERVIGE i7RCOATSI.</head>
        <p>FlEft *Y-FOR-SERVIGE i7RCOATSI.</p>
        <p>OUR OWN MAKE, FROM 45/- NEW AND CHOICE GOODS. George Sutton (late H. S.. Page &amp; Co.), 74 SANKEY ST., WARRINGTON (Legh Street Corner). Tel. 553.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
