      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Temperance Suburbs. Correspondents are just now discussing • the Practicability of temperance suburbs, and at least one letter dealing with the subject has been headed "A Novel Suggestion." But has it hot long since passed the experimental stage? Are not such exclusive communities to be found already in. many parts of the country? It must be more than 40 years ago since the late Sir Titus salt,. Bart., of Bradford, established pre- cisely on thew lines a model village, which by a combination of his name with that of the river close by was called Saltaire. Many other great firms have meanwhile , copied that ex- ample, and there is a conspicuous instance of the kind within a little distance of Warring- ton. Moreover, it is nowadays quite common tor speculators opening up estates for residen- tial purposes to stipulate that' no intoxicants Shall be sold there. I remember a case of a grocer who, acquiring a business within such an area was allowed to go through all the pre- liminary formalities with a view to an applica- tion for a retail " off " licence, and actually got into Court before having his attention directed to a clause in his own deed of 'conveyance, Which expressly forbade -what was about to be asked for.</p>
        <p>Another Suggestion. While on the kindred subject, I 'venture to commend to whom it may concern the idea of a suburb exclusively for householders, members Of whose families happen to be associated with an ambitious brass band, and are so enthusi- astic as to devote the greater part of their leisure to practice. I thought of this as I read the item " From Everywhere," headed "A I‘leighltur's Chance." I-. lately passed an attractive little cottage in a pleasant suburb, and I was really surprised to sec that it was to let. A few days later I went that way again and found it still untenanted. As I mused upon this, there smote Upon mine car a terrific blast front a trombone. It came from next door to the unoccupied house. Then. I understood.</p>
        <p>Rev. Wilson Barrett. In the Lymm news last week I read of the death of this old Primitive Methodist minister, Whom I knew well. I. made Mr. Barrett's acquaintance some 16 years ago, and followed his career with interest to the end. After he had become a supernumerary and was sup- Dosed to have gone into well-earned retirement '-Or the comparative rest assured in such cir- cUrnstances—the case of a struggling mission In one of his old Lancashire circuits appealed to hi rn so powerfully that he voluntarily under- took the charge for three years. lie went thence to Thornton, near Fleetwood, and died at Chesterfield as previously reported. As a matter of fact, he was suffering from the malady which ,ultimately proved fatal , when engaged upon the self-sacrificing work to which I have referred. Celluloid Dangers.</p>
        <p>In the very next paragraph to the item of intelligence just commented uPon was one to the effect that the Lymin authorities had ar- ranged for the distribution or a printed warn- -I,llg concerning the inflammable nature of cel- luloid articles. It was only the other day that 4 chauffeur having occasion to correct some inechanical defect beneath ,his car, sustained Dainful injuries through the 'accidental firing of 4 celluloid collar which unfortunately he was Wearing at the time. A case of the kind came fldef my notice a while ago at the Manchester Ye Hospital. I noticed among the patients a 'ad of 14 or so whose face was badly scarred, 41d I, gathered from him that the ignition of a Celluloid collar was the cause of it all. He had been terribly burned about the head and face, and on being discharged from the infirmary had had to enter the Eye Institution on account ~Of Injury to his eyes. The Lymm C6uncil have u°ne well and wisely in issuing their timely caution, lust,. now, by,the,bye,,' wheu int ton Wool is being so freely used for Christmas decorations, it should be borne in mind that that is not less dangerous.</p>
        <p>A Toast. The editorial Christmas greeting in last Sat- Urday's issue somehow reminded me of the ancient and certainly miscalled "Toast of the enrstant lover," which runs: " Here's to you, my dear, And to the dear that's not here, my dear; But if the dear that's not here, my dear, Were here, my dear, I'd not be drinking to you, my dear l"</p>
        <p>Criticism of Sorts. • r, The minstrel boys gained golden opinions." I read in the report of the People's College concert, and as I read aloud, a frivolous hearer broke in with something 'like this: "I say, have 'l2'll ever thought how absurd is that story of :he minstrel boy as Moore tells it?" I eon - 'eased it 'had never struck me in that way, whereupon the critic proceeded: "All, but, just let us consider it a moment. It begins, you rernember, 'The minstrel boy to the war has gene.' Now, you know, that strikes me as tlitogether too abrupt. Sort of sneaking an item °in the agenda without due notice of motion. une doesn't go to war as if one were catching train; at all events, I know a lot that Wouldn't. Then take that other immortal line, 1,11, is father's sword he has girded on.' It nays seems to me to be a shabby trick to • ve played on the old man—for You can bet ▪ 9,• 11r life it was the best weapon he could lay L'l3 hands on, and that the owner would have Put up with an inferior' blade. And his :vild harp slung behind Him.' Surely an kin- (..N•cssarily heavy handicap. Wild harp,' too! 41! the more likely to give trouble. This hap- as we learn from the next verse. `lhe Ininstrel fell."f here You ,have it. Tripped Laver one of the pedals, no doubt. It would nave L.._ nen in every way better to have left it at home. 'sten! 'The harp he loved ne'er spoke again, ftoril he tore its cords asunder.' Just as I tA° ,11g,nt ; he'd got mad with it for causing him 'ail. Still, it was silly of him to destroy his `,?in property like that, eh? Then there is IlaYdn's 'My mother bids me bind my hair.' Zs You follow the elaborate musical setting of "le siroPle words, you can't help thinking what a_ 10t Of fuss to make about a young woman Putting her hair up!" I laughed at my visitor's tancy—but ought one to laugh at such treat- nlent of the classics?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>DOUBT CANNOT EXIST.</head>
        <p>DOUBT CANNOT EXIST.</p>
        <p>Investigation will only Strenghten the Proof we give.</p>
        <p>flow can doubt exist in the face of such evi- tlence? Head here the endorsement of a repre- sentative resident of Warrington. "After my last confinement I became Subject , to serious kidney disorder," says 3.lrs. Pritchard (2,_f 17, Hamilton-street, near the "Schools, War- .4„,ultrton. "The first symptoms of the complaint Were gravel in the urinary excretions, and cruel kins in my back. I have been nearly crippled these sharp, stabbing pains in my back. could not stoop or do a hand's turn at times. k," There was a scalding pain in. relieving the :Vodder, which did not act naturally. I had opsical swellings in my ankles and in my Pleds. I felt so depressed and miserable that 4 Could not interest myself in anything. b" Nothing did me any good until I began with h°sn's Backache Kidney Pills. This medicine roe been a real blessing to me, and has cor- tiected the bladder weakness and taken away the r,°Psical swellings. They relieved my back ,elendidly, too, and made me feel better in li.e,rY way. I have great confidence in Dean's xPle, and always speak highly of them. (Signed) Pritchard." fr4-ries 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 1319; of all dealers, or 0°133. Poster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, 4:jord-street, London, W. Don't ask for back- liae, or kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DOAN'S Pr944ache Kidney- Pills, the same as Mrs. ,_ —"lord had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 27, 1913. GOLDEN WEDDING CELEBRATION.I TABLE TALK.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 27, 1913. GOLDEN WEDDING CELEBRATION.I TABLE TALK.</p>
        <p>Mr. Alfred Tilling Speaks of the Warrington of Sixty-Five Years Ago.</p>
        <p>TWENTY GRANDCHILDREN AT FAMILY GATHERING.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Alfred Tilling, of Walton Crescent, Lower Walton, celebrated the 50th anniversary of their wedding on Saturday. Mr. Tilling is the son of the late Mr. John Edge- hill Tilling, who lived in Warrington the greater portion of his life, but whose native place was the Isle of Wight. His mother'e maiden name was Sarah Johnson, a member of a Warrington family, and she was educated at the Warrington Blue Coat School. Mrs. Tilling's parents were also residents of Warrington for many years. her father, Thomas Porter, ally coming from Knowsley, and her mother, Hannah Johnson, was a native of Grappenhall. Mr. and Mrs. Tilling have had 11 children, the first two having died in infancy. Eight are still living. One son, Capt. T. P. Tilling, was lost at sea about three years ago. At the "family gathering," held at the Winmarleigh Cafe on Saturday, there were 20 grandchildren</p>
        <p>present. • The aged couple were the recipients Of num- erous congratulatory messages, and various pre- sentations were made. Mr. Tilling, who is 74 years of age, is well known in the town as a</p>
        <p>only illuminating power was supplied through the medium of tallow candles. Young fellows, who now enjoy the incandescent and electric light, and the present supply of cheap reading, can hardly imagine the difficulty we had in the past days of studying with the jumping light of the candle and only a very poor supply of good literature."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <p>• • • 5. . . • • • , - . ' • ' , • , )1111s. • , • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>Photograph by BirtleB. MR. AND MRS. ALFRED TILLING.</head>
        <p>Photograph by BirtleB. MR. AND MRS. ALFRED TILLING.</p>
        <p>friendly society worker, his connection --Cvith 10l Fc,lestry- having extended over 54 years. Mrs. Tilling is in her 71st year.</p>
        <p>directors of the town's airs seemed to have been alive to the necessity of feeding, for Mr. Tilling stated that when he was still a pupil at the National Schools they were marched to an unfinished building, which he afterwards learned was the old public baths in Church- street, almost opposite the end of Howley-lane. Here they were served with soup for dinner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>SOME REMINISCENCES.</head>
        <p>SOME REMINISCENCES.</p>
        <p>A worthy son of a very old Warrington family, Mr. Tilling has spent all his life in the borough, and his memory carries him back to the town long before it aspired to the dignity of a county borough. Speaking of the past days to "The Examiner" representative, Mr. Tilling said: "Sixty-five years ago Warrington was a differ- ent town from what it is to-day. Then it was situated in picturesque surroundings, now—you know what it is like. St. Austin's-lane was my birthplace, and there I was born on the 19th August, 1839. "Well do I remember the gardens which ex- tended without interruption from St. Auetin's- lane to what is now Arpley-street, and there ran a pleasant little brook,. bathed in all its rural simplicity, along the present Wilson-Patten. street, and when it arrived at a spot behind where the Warrington Slate Co.'s offices now are, it was crossed by a small footbridge, which was composed of a couple or so of planks nailed together. A few yards away from this primi- tive bridge there was an orchard, and the cherry trees spread their branches well over the fence. -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Difficult Spot to Pass._</head>
        <p>A Difficult Spot to Pass._</p>
        <p>"It made it an extremely difficult matter," he added with a smile, `,'" for a boy to pass that quarter W. ithout breaking a Commandment. There was no Arpley Station, Arpley Fields being practically a continuation of Spring Gar- dens, which latter, with the fields, occußied the space right from Bold-street across the L. and N.-W. Railway, where now stands Parker-street. " There ' were only a very few houses in this locality, and I remember one which was built on the site now occupied by Mr. Maginn's stables, at the top of Arpley-strpet, or as it was then called Slutcher's-lane.</p>
        <p>"My father was a currier, and worked as foreman for Mr. Furnival, who had a large business in Friars Gate. This gentleman's house adjoined the works, the house in which he lived being afterwards used as the Warrington Museum, before it was removed to the Museum now standing in Bold-street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>Teacher and her Cooking.</head>
        <p>Teacher and her Cooking.</p>
        <p>"In my young days the present educational code which prescribes a limited number of scholars for each teacher, was, of course, not even dreamt of, for, as an instance, at the first school I attended there were only about 18 scholars, but the teacher was so busy sewing and knitting and attending to the culinary de- partment- of her household that it was precious little time we had to be instructed! This 'school' was held in the kitchen of a house occupied by either a Miss or Mrs. Sarah Davis—. I don't remember properly—in River-road, be- hind Wilderspool Causeway. I cannot remem- ber' the alphabet ever being taught, but I sup- pose the idea was to keep us all out of. mischief for a short time each day. I later attended the St. James' Infants' School and the National Schools. At the latter school the boys from the Workhouse received their education, and I recollect their coming to and leaving the school in procession for the Workhouse every morn- ing and afternoon. These Workhouse boyi were mired up and down the school in the various classes, according to age and ability, and as a consequence received the same educa- tion from the same teachers as the rest of the scholars did.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Old Workhouse.</head>
        <p>The Old Workhouse.</p>
        <p>" The old Workhouse adjoined the Bull's Head public-house in Church-street, and it was some years later when the Workhouse in Guar- dian-street was erected by two brothers named Helsby, whose workshop was off Bank-street, where the large gasometers of the Corporation now stand. Speaking of the gasometers brings to my memory that there were very few, if any,, working men's houses at that time which were supplied with gas. I well remember that our</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Working at Nine.</head>
        <p>Working at Nine.</p>
        <p>"I began earning money when I was about nine ears'old by performing such little duties as taking breakfasts and dinners, for which I received a few coppers each week. That further reminds me that these small duties used to take me to the old shipbuilding yard, where the Tayleur, that ill-fated ship, and other vessels were built. My coppers were further aug- mented by pennies from an old woman who lived in School-street, Latchford, for whom, on two days a week, I worked by turning one of the old-fashioned mangling machines for her. Whilst I was engaged in these short journeys I very often saw the old stage coach start from the Rose and Crown Inn, Bridge-street, and at night I frequently witnessed the coach vming into town from Buxton, and the district lying in that direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Proud on 2s. a Week.</head>
        <p>Proud on 2s. a Week.</p>
        <p>"My first job after leaving school was when I obtained employment at Artingstall's in the brush department. I remember how proud I felt when on pay day I received 2/3. Later I became apprenticed to the joinery trade, with Mr. William Gibson, St. James-street, Latch- ford, who afterwards removed to Church-street, to the site of the old Workhouse. I worked for him for about 12 years, and during that time helped in the erection of Walton Lea, the resi- dence of Mr. George Crosfield. "My stay with Mr. Gibson was broken when I accepted an invitation from the late Mr. John Ciosfield to work as a joiner in the soap works, where I remained for about 27 years. There was only one other joiner there at the time, Mr. Joseph Hall. I was often requested to visit Walton Lea for the purpose of alterations and repairs, and on one of these occasions I was astonished, after reviewing a piece of skirting board, to find my name written on the back. Evidently I had put it on when I had fixed the board 30 years before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>First Evening School.</head>
        <p>First Evening School.</p>
        <p>"In my young manhood I was connected with St. James' Sunday School, when the Rev. James Wright, who was the incumbeht of the church, organised an evening school. I have wondered if this was the first school of its kind in the town. I was a teacher, although I had only a bare idea of the rudiments of elementary edu- cation. Anyhow, I did my best, and I could do no more. This school was entirely free; the teachers were voluntary, and the forms, atc., were given for this worthy object.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Fishing in the River.</head>
        <p>Fishing in the River.</p>
        <p>"I read some time ago of the river pollution in your paper, and it made me think of the changes that had taken place during my time. People would now shake their heads in pity for you if you or anybody else took to fishing in the Mersey, but I well remember the time when I cast my line into the river, and, what is more, did catch fish. Old fishermen in their boats used to skim over the surface of the water at dusk to fix what they called 'junkets,' which were long-tapered baskets, and which they left in the water all night.</p>
        <p>Shrimping was the favourite pastime of the lads, who used to make their catches near the Weir. Then when the chemical and dye works towards Manchester commenced to run their sewage into the river the fish were killed and thousands of them could be seen floating down the river. This probably was the beginning of what has since resulted in the extermination of fishing in the river.</p>
        <p>Canal Swift Packets.</p>
        <p>" There was a daily service of packets between Warrington and Manchester via the river, but Continued in next column</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>Struggle for the Candle.</head>
        <p>Struggle for the Candle.</p>
        <p>With a kindly smile and a shake of the head, the old gentleman stated that when all the family sat round the candle trying to get the best of the light, his mother used to say. " Prithee, lad, snuff th' candle"; the sudden reply to this command putting them in com- plete darkness but for the flames that made merry in the fireplace.</p>
        <p>"Our fires," he said, "had to be kindled with the aid of the flint, steel and tinder box, and I remember the useful little matches being introduced. The first matches I saw were pieces of wood about five inches long, tapered- and brimstoned at each end, so that. they could be used on two occasions."</p>
        <p>During the periods of trade depression the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>World's Tribute of interesting Items of News. QUAINT AND AMUSING.</head>
        <p>The World's Tribute of interesting Items of News. QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>Mincing-lane men predict a big demand for rubber flooring in the event of the present moderately low values continuing. On some of the big modern liners the innovation has proved acceptable, .while in the London Metal Ex- change the newly-laid rubber tiling provides a sedative much appreciated by members. Plan- tation companies are to lay a rubber floor in one of the wards of Guy's Hospital.</p>
        <p>Air Propellors for Barges.</p>
        <p>Tests of aerial propellors as motive power for barges on canals in England proved that this method is efficient and economical where high speed is not necessary. A constant aerial thrust of about 250 pounds can be obtained from a sixteen horse-power motor, and is suffi- cient to move at the rate of four miles an hour, barges loaded up to thirty or forty tons. The efficiency of such a propelling plant has been calculated to be 16.7 per cent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>Quinine Becoming Dear.</head>
        <p>Quinine Becoming Dear.</p>
        <p>An agreement has been signed between the European buyers of cinchona bark, from which quinine is made, and the growers in Java, the effect of which will be to regulate and, incident- ally, greatly to increase the price of the drug. The manufacturers agree to buy about one mil- lion pounds of cinchona bark a year at a regu- lated price. It is predicted that the present price will be doubled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Gigantic Collecting Campaign.</head>
        <p>A Gigantic Collecting Campaign.</p>
        <p>Remarkable efforts are being made to raise .£BOO,OOO in New York City for the new buildings connected with the Young Women's and Young</p>
        <p>ien's Christian Associations. The chairman Of the executive committee conducting the cam- paign is Mr. George W. Perkins, formerly of the United States Steel Corporation and One of the principal workers for Mr. Roosevelt at the last Presidential election. 'inc amount raised in one day is nearly £400,000, and includes the following donations: Mr. John 1). Rockefeller, £75,000; Mrs. 'Willards 1). Straight, £60,000; Mr. Cleveland Doage, £50,000; Miss Grace Dodge, £50,000; Mrs. Finley J. Shepherd (Helen Gould), £40,000.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>Universal Service in Victoria.</head>
        <p>Universal Service in Victoria.</p>
        <p>Figures compiled regarding the number of persons undergoing training under the univer- sal service scheme in Victoria show that out of 11,682 registrations for the militia or adult force, 5,386 men are bearing arms. The number liable for training is 5,461, making the per- centage of " shirkers " very low indeed. In the senior cadet force there are 44,313 registra- tions, 28,506 liable for training, and 28,027 actually In training. The exemptions, mainly owing to residence outside battalion and train- ing areas, total 15,571.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>GOLDEN WEDDING CELEBRATION-</head>
        <p>GOLDEN WEDDING CELEBRATION-</p>
        <p>continued.</p>
        <p>the boats were drawn by horses and took about five hours to travel the distance. This route to Manchester was outdone by that from Lon. don Bridge along the canal, where an ex!.•rtss ' service was inaugurated.</p>
        <p>"To keep in tone with the speedy journey the vessel used was named the "Swift,' and how it used to fly through the country, reach- ing Manchester in three hours ! It was re- markable then and was effected by relays of horses, who were always trotting, attached to the boat. Another old industry of our town aas the making of cannon balls, and cannons at the Old Bridge Foundry, which was situated near Bishop's Wharf.</p>
        <p>Primitive Railway, Stations.</p>
        <p>" I can remember the temporary rail w station (where Wilderspool Crossings are) which was used during the erection of the iron bridge over the river. Another station was also built close to Crosfield's Works, and was known as Whitecross Station.' This station was a very primitive affair, and a very prominent notice hung up, stating that no trains start from this station within fifteen minutes of each other. "In time I was entitled to vote, and I first exercised my privilege in the famous '6B elec- tion, although I had voted at several previous municipal elections. The excitement and rowdyism commenced on nomination day. A platform was erected adjoining Walker's Brewery wall, where the market now is, and there the work of nomination was carried on.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Election Hustings.</head>
        <p>Election Hustings.</p>
        <p>"The platform vtas divided into two sections, the respective candidates and their chief sup- porters appearing on the respective sections. As it was known how each man voted, the progress of the, poll was declared every hour, and the booth was closed at four o'clock in the after- noon.</p>
        <p>"A few days prior to the '6B election the majority of the works had to close down for the feeling was so high. All kinds•of trickery were in vogue for the winning of a seat, and in a timber yard and waggon works on Cockhedge numbers of men were imprisoned or 'bottled,' so that they would not be able to vote. They were provided with a good supply of beer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Keeping Electors Prisoners.</head>
        <p>Keeping Electors Prisoners.</p>
        <p>"Others were taken down the river in boats, as far as Widnes, and kept there out of the way. Of course, both sides were responsible for these things, and to counteract them men volunteered Jo patrol the streets to prevent any of their side from being taken away.</p>
        <p>I w,a.s a captain over twelve of these vGlun- teers, and we marched the streets on our 'beat' the whole of the night previous to the election.</p>
        <p>Old Warrington Fair.</p>
        <p>"I can call to memory the Warrington Fair held in the Market Place, opposite the big lamp by the Barley Mow.' The Council Chamber, with the Fire Engine Station underneath, was in a line with the 'Barley Mow,' in what is now Market-street, and the Post Office was also ad- joining these premises. In my boyhood there used to, be four watchmen for the four main streets of the town, and I have heard them patrolling the street and call out the time every hour, and also the state of the weather, whilst the 'guardians of peace' in the daytime were limited to two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Crosfield Clock.</head>
        <p>The Crosfield Clock.</p>
        <p>"It came within my . duties to assist in the demolition of Trinity Church old tower and the erection of the new clock, which the late John Crosfield presented to .the town. "I remember the old ' sandmen ' going round the town with their donkey carts, selling sand to householders-to sprinkle on the floors of their houses. I was acquainted with one or two of these men, and they had a permanent stable in their kitchens for the donkey, who usually found his quarters under the staircase.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>TRIBUTE TO HIS HELP-MEET.</head>
        <p>TRIBUTE TO HIS HELP-MEET.</p>
        <p>Mr. Tilling paid a great compliment to hie wife. During the conversation he frequently referred to her, and mentioned her loving and self-sacrificing nursing of him during his long illnesses, some of which had extended over a period of 12 months. Had it not been for her care and attention, he said, he would not have celebrated the golden wedding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>WORLD OF LETTERS.</head>
        <p>THE WORLD OF LETTERS.</p>
        <p>WOMAN AT HOME.</p>
        <p>The January number of the "Woman at home" contains an acceptable opening item in the form of the first instalment of a series of articles by Morley Adams, entitled "Famous Women I have met." The article describos the life and work of Mrs. Florence L. Barclay, the writer of the world-fanious novel " The Rosary." All admirers of Mrs. Barclay's novels will be delighted to be brought into such intimate touch with the wonderful, magnetic personality of this remarkable woman, and we venture to think that some Of the Impressions gathered will not only have a lasting effect, but will at the same time create added interest and throw a new ray of light on the pages of her well- known works. The text is generously illus- trated with photographs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>PANAM CANAL LOCKS.</p>
        <p>Their gigantic walls stretch towards us, ad- vancing spurs and arches, and they diverge laterally to make a wider passage. We find ourselves, flanked by bare, solemn, steep walls, in an echoing abyss on the brow of which moves a minute and distant throng. The walls are as hiqh as a seven-storey building; they display -blind and endless facades. The locks are double; each one can raise one ship and lower another simultaneously; and the central wall, which traverses and divides them is as wide as a market square. Towering, majestic, prodigious, it stretches beyond the locks in the centre of the canal, forming a fantastic bas- tion 100 yards ling. Seen from below, frdm the lower water, as one approaches on a little tug boat, these mas- sive constructions, smooth, simple, yet formid- able, have the appearance of a fabulous rock. There is an indescribable atmosphere of legend about them; one feels that one is approaching, a fairy city surrounded by fearful and immeas- urable walls, provided with tremendous gates of iron -which can only be opened by enchant- ment. One is subdued and oppressed by the immensity of everything. The three locks, which stand one above the other, in the perspective, extend for more than a mile, with their advanced walls and the machinery for working them. Their sides rise in terrace's, and-at the base they have cyclo- pean steps. The locks are nothing but immense buried buildings, apartments for battleships let into the earth.</p>
        <p>Everyone knows how they work. The prin- ciple of the lock is ancient and immutable. The same system which has raised barges for years will .cause • Dreadnoughts to rise in the Panama Canal. But it is not the thing itself which causes stupefaction here.; it is the pro- portions of the work, its bulk, t;,: :;:nnensity, and daring of this great world of cement. Gradually as the work proceeded, in the face of difficulties which geew more and, more serious, the plans, instead pf being reduced, were amplified. The progress of naval con- struction caused the earlier plans to be dis- carded. America desired a canal which should, be able to accommodate not only every existing ship, but which should permit the passage of the greatest vessel of the future. The size of the biggest ship which i could enter the Port of New York determined the dimensions Which need not be exceeded. The depth of the ports of war and commerce lays down a precise limit for the size of future battleships and commer- cial vessels. Thus, the. locks of the Panama Canal will be able comfortably to acCommodate ships with a length of 1,000 ft., a breadth of 120 ft., and a draught in proportion. The Int- perator is 82ft. shorter and 13ft. narrower than this imaginary vessel; for whieNithe American engineers have prepared hospitality, and which has been the phantom and nightmare of the whole work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>PLAIN WOMAN.</head>
        <p>THE PLAIN WOMAN.</p>
        <p>There is really no need for even the plainest of plain women to be despondent about her ap- pearance. There is, in fact, abundant hope for her in the realm of things beautiful and attrac- tive if she only knew it, and I do not hesitate to say that the plainest woman living to-day can. by following the laws of health and hygiene, and by observing a few simple and regulai toilet rules, render herself charming, attrac- tive, and pleasant to look upon. . . There are few women, indeed, who are without at least. one good point. It may be beautiful, white gleaming teeth, regular and pearly. Or, perhaps it is a wealth of glorious hair, bright and burnished like deep gold or copper, or pos- sibly an abundance of that misty brown hair that has lurking in its cloudy meshes many varying tints, or again it may be those rare and beautiful raven black tresses, shadowy, dusky. and dark as night. But whatever good point you have, cultivate it for, all you are worth, play up to it, accentuate it. . . . Plain women are usually so nice, so interesting, and withal so good-natured that they can be quite as attractive and lovable as the " beauty " ; moreover they have never been spoiled by flat- tery, and are therefore less given to petty jealousies. Now, is not fill this encouragement for you, my dear plain reader?—Marie Blanche, in. "Good Health."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>PANAMA'S DEATH TOLL.</head>
        <p>PANAMA'S DEATH TOLL.</p>
        <p>One -would never dream, amid all the canal enthusiasm and energy and -the sort of joyous- ness that prevails among the workers, that every day on an average ends more Thar one life—that thirty or forty die each month in Ancon hospital alone, and that new dozen new graves stand ready all the time at Ancor) cemetery for the occupants whose speedy com- ing may be depended upon.. The stories of the manifold sufferings of ,the workers of former years are more picturesque than those of to- day; and, in a way, sadder, in that the fearful loss of life due to ignorance of the causes of the .tropical diseases seems, in the light of our present knowledge and splendid use of it, quite needless. There is the story of the thousand Chinamen, who went to work on the railroad. and who began to die off so fast from yellow fever, that the rest of them, discouraged and heart-sick, .committed. suicide. The place where they lived and died bears record of this story in its name—Matachin—"Dead China- man." There is the story of the gang of light- hearted Irishmen, arriving full of -energy and ambition, who succumbed even more quickly to the climatic conditions than did the Chinese, and died off promptly, to a man. There is the story of the French consuls who went with their families, one after another in quick suc- cession—sometimes only six weeks apart—each one sickening in a short time, of the dread fever, and paying the penalty with heart-rend- ing unoriginality. The quarters of the French . consulate, which came into the hands of the Americans when the .purchase was .made from ' the French in 1904,. and are now the 'lime of the American legation, are still so surrounded with the traditions and Memories of those tragic , French happenings that not a servant -can be induced to sleep there at night. All go home when dusk falls, to return only with morning liAt.—From "The Diggers Who Never Got Through," in "Everyone's."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>TERRIER "TERROR" AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>TERRIER "TERROR" AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Reginald Ross Johnson, 36, Granville-street, was ordered at Runcorn to pay 255. and costs and destroy a cross-bred terrier dog. The dog had been for some time, it was stated, the ter- ror of Granville-street. Evidence was given by John While and Thomas Hicks, labourers, who had been bitten by the dog.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>New Books.</head>
        <p>New Books.</p>
        <p>Short Stories. By Tulstoy. itli an Intro- duction. Scott Library.</p>
        <p>Here are six short stories by the Russian master. It goes without saying that they are all of interest. Each one has upon it the mark of peculiar simplicity, so that it is impossible to mistake its meaning. In each case, too, it is quite clear that the writer has set out with ad definite didactic purpose. Certain principles of .conduct are here set forward, clothed in the conditions of BuSsian life. To those who may have little time to acquaint themselves with Tolstoy as revealed in his larger works, the perusal of this book will provide an insight into, his method and message.. No one will find these pages dull and uninteresting. Especially striking are the stories entitled "What Men Live By" and "The Two Pilgrims." There is an introduction standing at the beginning of this volume in the name of Mr. Frederick J. Crowest. • This is in some ways illuminating, but it is written from an unsympathetic stand- point. We do not demand agreement with an author's position from the writer of an introdhc- tion, but it is irritating to meet with criticism which is cheaply expressed. Thus we are told that Tolstoy's life is divided into two parts by his "so-called conversion " ; whilst a few lines lower down his passionate humanitarianism is termed a "social reform craze." The writer also complains that Tolstoy was obsessed with the New Testament. Doubtless to some people this is a serious offence, but it is probably one reason why vast multitudes are reading what Tolstoy wrote. It will need more insight than the writer of this introduction possesses to deal with the many-sided personality of this great figure of the nineteenth century, and it is good to turn from the forty pages of preliminary matter to the words of sfolstoy himself.</p>
        <p>Prose Dramas. By Ibsen. With an Introduc- tion. Scott Library. • is.</p>
        <p>The three specimens of lbsen's work chosen for inclusion in this volume are "liosnicrsholin,'- " A Doll's House," and " The Lady trom the Sea." A few pages' of well-written introduction give in concise form the main events in .the wandering life of Ibsen, and some well .marked features of his literary work. It is a witness to the power and influence of any writer when his work becomes in itself .the centre of a special literature. .Around the productionS of Ibsen there has gathered in Scandinavia and in Ger- many a considerable literary 'output concerned with the interpretation of his life and work. Perhaps amongst- all 'these Ibsen experts the first place should be given to Herr Saeger. Thg three dramas printed in the. present volume belong essentially' to lbsen's studies of modern life. " Bosmershohn " has its setting in the. ,political struggle which sought to 'identify the actual ministry in Norwegian Government with the representative Assembly, the, Storthing or Parliament, 9 struggle which issued in 1884 In a Victory for progressive principles. The four acts of the play give a vivid representation of the rancour and bitterness of party strife,' while behind the political framework there lies the deep note of spiritual tragedy. The whole thing is more than the mere championing of Liberal principles and democratic progress; it is in some respects a challenge to democratic thought in its easiest assumption.. Dr.- Georg BrandeS quotes from a speech delivered by Ibsen about this time by way of indicating the position he occupies' in " Bomersholm " : "There remains much to be done before we can be said to have attained that liberty. But I fear that our present democracy will not be equal to the task. An element of nobility must be introduced into our national life, into our Parliament, and into our Press. Of course it ds not nobility of birth that I am thinking of, nor of money, nor yet of knowledge, nor even of ability and.talent ; I am thinking of nobility of character, of will, of soul."</p>
        <p>The two other plays here printed ave also pertinent to the problems of the moment. To anyone realising the tremendous importance and the far-reaching implications of the Feminist movement of our own day, the reading of "A Doll's House" will be !both stimulating and illuminating.</p>
        <p>Prison Songs and Poems. By J. Robert Clarke Erskine MacDonald. 3s. 6d. net.</p>
        <p>This little book of songs is in many ways of more, than ordinary interest. It had its birth in a period of five years' penal servitude, and the first utterance of these poeins was in a prison cell. The author found in the composi- tion of these lines a method of relief to the mind during the, dreary days of his detention at Port- land,' and his work now makes this public appearance in the hope that it may cheer and hearten others in distressing and heart-break- ing circumstances. Much of the thought in this work is common- place enough, though there is a certain power of expression, too, that frequently manifests itself. In the most ambitious of these produc- tions, a long poem entitled "The Yoke Fellows," there is much sustained presentation, and a realism in the passages descriptive of prison life which compels the attention of the reader. Indeed it is when the writer portrays for us the vivid impressions of these days of confine- ment, with theii* accompaniment of much that is degrading and humiliating, that his words conic most deeply from him and find their mark. In the short poem Called "The Prisoner" ,there. is the totch of grim and tragic reality about such lines as these:</p>
        <p>Few sounds break in—no jailer speaks, Save when in speaking duty lies: The banging of a distant door Is sometimes heard—more oft the tread Of restless footsteps on the floor That lies directly overhead. Seven steps, then turn: seven steps again, From end to end the lonely cell, No. captive tiger in his den, Who hitherto was wont to dwell . Free, 'neath the canopy of heaven, Ever more restless—morn and night, Seven paces forward, backward seven,, Yearning and panting for the light That never yet in prison shone.</p>
        <p>l'his is strong writing, and there is not a little of it to be found in these pages. It will inevitably occur to every reader of these prison songs that they offer something morp than the effort .of an individual to Cheer and help discouraged hearts. They offer, too, a commentary on our prison system. The last word has not been said on the efficacy and wisdom of our method o of criminal punishment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>" BAILY'S."</head>
        <p>" BAILY'S."</p>
        <p>Both hunting and racing men will 'read with Jeep interest the first number of the 101st vol- ume or". Baily's" Magazine of Sports and Pas- times" for January, wlfich has just made its appearance. One of the Joint Masters of the Cheshire Hounds, Captain William Ifigson, LP., is the subject of the usual biographical sketch, and interest is immediately fixed in the, article by G. T. 8., which tells of the history of the Tarporley Hunt Club. This ,ancient insti- tution was established in 1762, and although it has more or less been distinct from the Che- shire Hounds, the two intermingle so closely that separation is impossible. On the story of one hangs the life of the other. Cheshire has from nine immemorial 'stood 'very prominent amongst the hunting counties of England. Leicestershire, it may be argued is more fashion-. able, but then its popularity is more peeling. In Cheshire the .heart and soul of its 'popula- tion are in the sport, and the great ancestral houses of the Grosvenors, the Egertons, the• Delampres, the Cholmondeleys, the Cottons, the Warburtons, the, Mainwarings, the Leghs, and the Tollemaches have transmitted to their descendants that love of the sport which has given such a high tone to all the proceedings kn the field. Reference to Past Presidents of</p>
        <p>the T.H.C. ; to the ninny songs and ballads of the late Mr. Boland. Egerton-Warburton ; to old Masters and Huntsmen .of the Cheshire Fox- hounds; to countless good runs and many other striking sporting incidents are mentiOned in this article, which is profusely illustrated with portraits of Mr. Egerton-Warburtoh, of the Hound Mice at Newmarket in 1762, and of the Cheshire Hunt members in 1842.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <p>gy-a-wxy-o-woo-w00=0.2,30-~0.2.0,-eol CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE JACK. t.:.ihskvO42ootOACo-a'W2dA%'-osA.i</p>
