      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>SPELL "POTATO."</head>
        <p>SPELL "POTATO."</p>
        <p>Pay great attention. What does this Spell— Ghoughplitheiglitteau? Well, according to the following rule, it spells—it spells—do you give it up? It spells uotato--for, gli stands for p, as you will find from the last letters in hic- cough; ough for o, as in dough; phth stands for t, as in phtlnsis ; eigh stands for a, as in neighbour; tte stands for t, as in gazette; and eau stands for u, as- in beau. Thus you Tiave</p>
        <p>DO WHAT YOU CAN. Do what you can, being what you are; Shine like a glow-worm, if you cannot be a</p>
        <p>'star. Be the pliant oar, if you. cannot be a sailor. Be the little needle, if you, cannot be the tailor. Be the cleaning broom, if you cannot be the</p>
        <p>SWCeper. Be the sharpened sickle•if you cannot be the reaper. -</p>
        <p>PUZZLES. A Pile of Boxes.</p>
        <p>The first one you may in the alphabet find; Me second to guess you must delve in a mine; rho third makes you sneeze, be it ever so fine; The doctors will give you the fourth, if you're</p>
        <p>ill; ['he fifth splutters terribly, do what you will. Jolly darkies, with airs, for the sixth often</p>
        <p>walk ; You yourself are the seventh, when too much</p>
        <p>you talk; A crowd of musicians the eighth join to make; rile ninth, when indoors, from your head you</p>
        <p>must take; When the cook makes mince pies, the tenth</p>
        <p>must not spare"; _ When near the eleventh you come have a care; rile twelfth oft is precious, is sparkling, is bright ; k.nd to the thirteenth listen all with delight; le last you will need it-Ist as long as you live. l'he puzzle is solved when these answers you give.</p>
        <p>Answer to Puzzle. Pat, ten—patent. -</p>
        <p>HALF OF MINE. , lie's 'lst a little orfant boy Wat goes to school with me; An' ain't' got any parents, 'euz. His folks is dead, you see. , An' w'en he sees my toys an' things— My, but his eyes 'ist shine; An' he ain't got no marbles, so I give him half of mine.</p>
        <p>An' once it's orful stfirmy w'en It 's noon an' he can't go Back where he work for board an'.,clo'cs, To •get his lunch, an' so - I had sonic san'wishes an' things 'At he thought was 'is f fine, An' Cuz he didn't have no lunch I give him. half of mine.</p>
        <p>An' 'once w'en we ' went down to fish, He come along with me, An' w'en we're there says he 'lst Wish 'At he could fish. You see He's orful poor an' brought a.pole, But didn't have a line, An' Wen I saw how lad he felt I give him half of mine.</p>
        <p>An' one time I 'ist told my ma flow he don't have much fun 'Cuz he ain't got no ma or pa Or aunt or anyone. An' en I told her how I thought 'At it would .be 'ist fine 'Cuz he ain't got no mother if I'd give him half of mine.</p>
        <p>He ain't my,brother, really true, He's 'lst an orfant, so 11Iy ma she 'took him, 'euz she knew He had no place to go. I'm awful glad we got him an' • My pa thinks it 'lst fine— He didn't have /to mother, so I give him half of mine.</p>
