      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>TEMPERANCE REFORM.</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE REFORM.</p>
        <p>United Kingdom Alliance Demand Licensing Bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>DRINK OUR MOST SUBTLE FOE.</head>
        <p>DRINK OUR MOST SUBTLE FOE.</p>
        <p>, . The following is a manifesto just issued on behalf of the -United Kingdom Alliance by the President, Mr. Lief Jones, MY.: 'We desire to impress upon all who in any degree sympathise with the cause of Temperance legislation, the vital importance of straightway using every scrap of influence they possess to bring home to Parliament and to the Govern- ment the extent and the intensity of the demand for a Temperance Reform Bill for England and Wales in .the coming Year. There is danger lest, because we have been patient, we should be supposed to be indifferent as to when the Government will proceed to fulfil their reiter- ated pledges upon this question. Nothing could be further. from the truth. We ,have been Patient only because we have relied upon pledges too explicit to be misunderstood or to be broken. The introduction .next year of a comprehensive Temperance measure into the House of Com- mons would elicit such widespread response and backing from the most various and unaccus- tomed quarters as would 'astonish all who have not followed Closely the solidifying of opinion on. the subject during recent years.</p>
        <p>Drink Factor Must Be Eliminated. •'Social reformers realise to the full the truth of Mr. Lloyd George's words: Nb reform, poli- tical or social, will avail in this country unless you precede it with Temperance reform.' All who are doing battle with poverty, disease and degradation find in drink their most powerful and most .snbtle foe ;` and they are helpless to overcome any one of the besetting social 'evils until they are armed with power to eliminate the drink factor, which is everywhere present in greater. or less degree. Such power the 'Scot- tish people have gained in the Temperance (cot- land) Act this year; it is our turn next, and now or never must the claim be presented and pressed if it is to be- met in the present Parlia- ment. The political position is favourable. If Polities .run their normal course' the great con- tentious measures at present before Parliament will be. disposed ,of by next • summer, and the autumn will be clear for our ,Temperance Bill. The Prime Minister, in his speech at Leeds on November 27th, once again gave utterance to his conviction as. to this ' urgency ' of the Temper- ance question; and • be does not use words lightly. He has .promised to deal with it 'when time and 'opportunity offer.' The Government control the time of. the House of Coinmons, and it is for them to create the necessary opportunity by devoting .the Autumn Session Of 1914 to carry- ing out the policy which the Lords frustrated in 1908. The efforts which the Government mach then, and their' repeated declarations since, leave no room for doubt as to their sincerity m their earnestness_ of purpose in the matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>One Danger.</head>
        <p>The One Danger.</p>
        <p>"The one danger ahead lies in the multitude of conflicting claims upon the time and attention Of Parliament. It is for us to insist that before passing on to' other reforms, no matter - how pressing or how important, the long arrears of Temperance legislation must be cleared up. We go to. the Government not as suppliants, hut as .creditors presenting a just and admitted claim. Let Us make the Government understand that by passing their Temperance. Bill through the Commons next year they will not only be dis- charging a debt that is due, but that they will gladden the hearts of all who are striving to better the condition of the people and Will win such widespread and • influential:suppoit- as will carry the measure triumphantly into law."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's</p>
        <p>Associaion.</p>
        <p>N.A. CENTRE.</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN ANGLERS' YEAR.</p>
        <p>In his annual report, the delegate of thc Warrington centre of the Northern Anglers' Association, says that this season be has made a little headway, and this district had risen above expectations in the estimation of all centres around Lancashire. He would always look back with pride upon the fact that the centre had the responsibility of the All-England championship upon thefr shoulders; and that the officials of the Warrington centre carried out the arrangements in such a manner aS to call forth the praise of all the centres ond lead- ing officials of the Association. They had failed in getting the next general meeting for War- rington, but they had been promised an execu- tive committee meeting early next season. At a meeting of the Council, November 29, at Bamfurlong, it was reported that sporting rights over a considerable portion of the Leeds and Liverpool Canal had been obtained, and the close season would. lie enforced. It was decided to support the Lancashire and Cheshire Federa- tion in pressing for the preservation of coarse fish in the Dee.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting followed, find was attended by 200 members—the best meeting the Northern Anglers have ever held. The 24th annual report recorded a paid membership of 7,238 against 8,238, a decrease of 1,000, which will be reduced by 300 or 400 when a defaulting centre has made its returns. Five centres, in- cluding Warrington, showed an increased mem- bership. The balance sheet showed a deficit of nearly .tl7, including loss of £6 in the cham- pionship contest on the Dee. Application for a refund in connection with this and anothef; loss will be made to the National Federation of Anglers. The defence fund amounted to £B4 155., but there were law costs to be met con- nected with the Ribble inquiry. The renting of the fishing rights on the river Gowy has been of material benefit to Warring- ton, and the voluntary contributions from Ches- ter, Wigan, Makerfield and St. Helens was an indication that the Warrington centre was con- sidered at the Executive Council meetings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>NAVAL EXPERIMENT.</head>
        <p>A NAVAL EXPERIMENT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Leicester ParliamentarY Debating Society held last week, the DIV" light Saving Bill was presented by Mr. Wll Willett, introduced for the evening as the Member for Chelsea. On the conclusion of tit debate .a resolution in favour of the Bill was carried with only two dissentients. The Daylight Skving principle has been intro- duced throughout one of the battle squadrons of the Horne Fleet .during the late autumn by the Admiral in command. _The ordinary clocks en the squadron were advanced in such a- way that all possible use of daylight was made. There was no consciousness of getting up earlier 0811 usual and the usual Pleet routine was altered.</p>
        <p>Prom a report just received from New Zell' land it appears that the second reading of the Daylight Bill introduced in the Parliament of that country was carried by a good majority in October last, but that its further progress was prevaited owing to the opposition of certaill memTers representing farm districts. Mr. Side.' the -member in charge of the Bill, intends t° re-introduce it next session: . _</p>
        <p>In the Parliament of Victoria (Australia) a similar Bill, introduced for the third time US Mr. Outtrim, was read a second time, also October last. The Prime Minister, during the debate, expressed his approval of the Bill, and stated that he would bring the subject hefere the next Conference of State Premiers, with e view to secure concerted action by all the States of the Commonwealth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>INTERNATIONALITY ESSENTIAL.</head>
        <p>INTERNATIONALITY ESSENTIAL.</p>
        <p>From Germany, Geheimrath Dr. Von ,Bottio• ger, a member of the Prussian House of Peers, wrote last week to Mr. Willett to the effect that he is still pushing on the proposal to obtain $ Daylight Saving Bill for Germany. Dr. Veil Botting,er states that he has had two confer- ences on the subject with the Prussian Minister of Public Works, that the Minister expressed verbally, and in writing, his great interest the matter, but was very firm on the point ni internationality, asserting that, at least, t 1,113 middle European States would have to make tile new ariangement together. Meanwhile, Dr. Von Bottinger is interesting the Chambers of Coln: merce in Germany, and has 'arranged that al the International Congress of Chambers of Coln' merce, which takes place in June next in Paris, the question shall be put on the order of the day. • Already in Germany resolutions in favour of such a Bill have been passed by the Chamber! of Commerce of Barmen, Elberfeld, Cassel' 'Gottingen and Nurnberg, as well as by the CO' ference of Chambers of Commerce of the Ger' man Empire in 1912. In the United Kingdom 73 Chambers of Cour meree 'have passed resolutions in favour of the Bill. Among them are those of London, Liver' pool, Manchester f Leeds, Hull, Sheffield, Bristol, Dublin, Belfast, Cardiff and Swansea, while thl Associated Chambers of Commerce have passel' similar resolutions at their meetings in London in the years 1909, 1910, 1911, 1912 and 1913..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD BEATEN BY MID-CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>Mid-Cheshire visited Latchford -and a kee° game ensued, resulting in a win for Mid-Clie: shire by four goals to nil. The visitors score(' first and held the lead until nearly half-tl' when Pearson equalised. In the second half the visitors were again 0.1,15 first to score,; Pearson equalising, and then Mi". Cheshire . were lucky in scoring from a kick into the circle. Later one goal was scored bY; each side, the .Latchford point being a good shot from Travis. For Late-raid, Pearson, Isherwood, Fletcher and Bruhl were on fairli good form.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Second Team Defeated.</head>
        <p>Second Team Defeated.</p>
        <p>The se)cond---eleven travelled to Knutsford to meet Mid-Cheshire "A," and were beaten 2-1. Furness obtained possession in the middle of the field, and after a fine individual run, scored for Latchford. Despite several attempts there was no further scoring before half-time. In the second half the play was fast, and the Mid-Cheshire centre-forward, picking up a good centre, shot hard into the net. Later on, he scored an unsatisfactory goal from a penal° bully. Latchford slid very well to run Mid-Cheshire to a goal, for they had three regular men ill, and one of their forwards, receiving a naSIJ knock quite early in the game, was practicallS a passenger for the rest of the match. Fletcher, Newbold, and Mynekyme were the best of tlies Latchford side.</p>
        <p>Teams for To-day. The first eleven are at home to Timperlei with the following: J. Bruhl; W. Fletcher AP` C. Billington; G. Woodcock, J. B. Wilkins° and A. Knight; F. W. Isherwood, W. 5.,P1.„1.- ness, J. E. Pearson, W. 11. Minitrds 'and A. Starkey. Umpire, Mr. F. Grounds. Bully 2.15.</p>
        <p>The second eleven meet Timperley at TinlPer- ley, with: G. Mynekyme; W. Eccles and Allanson; .T. Albinson, A. E. Bradshaw and Banks; J. J. McCawley,-F. C. Newbold, Ir• •11, Fletcher, A. G. Marsden and W. Starkey. Triun leaves Arpley. Station 1.16.</p>
        <p>CROSFIELD'S CLUB. The small but enthusiastic band of spectili tors at the Hood-lane ground on Saturday bilur the pleasure of seeing a really exciting and cies.' game, when the Soap Works team met Marl. chester Y.M.C.A. for the first time this season. The best side won, but Crosfield's put uP splendid fight in the absence of the captaio,t centre-half and one of the first eleven bad' They were only beaten by one goal, the final re- suit. being 3-2 in the visitors' favour. For the home team, Bloomer, Woodcock Dean played played the best hockey, but the eleven AP a whole showed a marked improvement.</p>
        <p>The report orthe Medical Officer for Flints' states that during the year there were no infail deaths at Prestatyn.</p>
      </div>
