      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>EALLY• NECESSARY</head>
        <p>EALLY• NECESSARY</p>
        <p>TI TAKE</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, DECEMBER 27, 1913.</p>
        <p>wardrobe afforded. Mrs. Grobbs, entering to 'remove* the stale remnants of the meal, threw _up her hands._ _ _</p>
        <p>"Well; I declare, I do declare! You look younger, miss, less—what's the word ?—less 'aggard like." She waxed eloquent, and a flush mine to Mary Bramhain's cheeks. It was all folly, but sometimes folly was sweet. But when, thinned by distance, the clamour of a motor-horn rose to the garret, her hands flew to her hat in a panic. She could never do it, never. The feeling uppermost with her at the 'moment was that her cloth- ing had been forcibly removed by impious hands, leaving her uncovered to the gaze of all the world. • "I daren't—oh ! I daren't !" she said, covering her face. But the sound of a hearty voice inspired her, bringing with it ' a curious suggestion of another world where ordinary cony entions were not. She rose with a faltering smile that was still on her lips, and in her eyes when she went down the stairs, a smile commingled of shyness and defiance. , The man below caught a sharp breath as the wan light filtered through the transom and fell on her face. As one in a dream she was assisted • into a long racing car. Thick fur rugs were folded 1 about her. She had never ridden in a. motor before, and when the crisp air rushed past her face as they left the mean streets be- hind, exaltation clamoured in her heart. Let the morrow care for itself, for this one • Christmas Day at least she would- live— live. Mr. James did not speak much as they entered open country. Now and then he sighed softly, as though contented; again he Deemed abstracted, his brows contracted with thought, his • gloved fingers drumming on the lap-robe. "That's the England I used to dream of— out there," he said suddenly, drawing her attention to the glowing landscape. It was like 'a picture, an embodiment of Christ- mas. Warm red houses. Their roofs atwinkle, a grey old church, a troop of laughing children laden heavily iiith par- cels. The years slipped from Miss Bram- ham's shoulders and left her young again. "Tell me something about 'Out there,'" she urged him. "You knew my brother." "Yes, for a while we worked .together,, but we had no luck. -He used to talk about you, it seemed to me I knew you well. But I had •to go away, and afterwards—he died. I felt very lonely then. I think I've been lonely ever since." She drew him on to talk, her , own loneliness s'howing her the way. "I came home. The dream of my life was realised. I was forgotten, old friends .were 'dead; not a soul cared whether I lived or died. I'd dream9d of, the welcome, and 'it wasn't there., People were too busy to care about a man who'd dropped out of their lives twenty years ago. If it hadn't' ,been for an idea I'd have gone back straight- way. I'd 'said I'd spend one Christmas in •the old country, and I'm a man of my word. I said I'd ask you to eat. that Christmas dinner with me, because your brother told me what your old Christmases were like. It wasn't easy to find you, but I made it my business to do, it." The car 'sped smoothly on its way. The landFcape rioted past. Warm fires showed, ,glowing faces, happy voices rang. "We'll lunch here. This isn't Christmas dinner, mind." It was a quaint old roadside inn, .that might have been lifted, bodily from one of Dickens's pages. They entered, to be welcomed by roaring flies and rubicund faces. They ate hungrily--; and the old. ab- straction settled afresh on Mr. James. More than once his hand went to the breast pocket of his coat and pante • away empty. And then 1114 -left the warmth and cheer for the gathering twilight of the open. Darkness grew as they went on. "We'll have to take care we don't lose our way," said • Mr. James, with forced levity. A distant church clock struck six. and something tore at Miss. Bramham's heart. What was it that was familiar in the sound? Why should her thoughts cross the bridge of the tars and bring to mind a little, creeper-covered church, lamp-lit, warm-gleaming? And her father moving .from choir to pulpit—tears came to her eves. When she could see again it was as though the years had never been. She was walking up a remembered path, i remembered windows glowed before her. She was dream- ing, of course, and in a little while she would waken to the grey rani." beneath the eaves, but meantime—meantime the dream was growing amazingly. sad. Why, the studded door was open -before her, ana some- one was bobbing and smiling; someone' old, , with wrinkled hands.</p>
        <p>Mary. God bless you, Miss Mary, And send you a happy Christmas!' "Ellen! What is it, where—" She swayed a little, but Mr. James' arm steadied her.. Through the open door of the dining-room she could see a table richly spread. This was her own old home. Yes, i exactly as it had been n the dead years. The furniture was the same—be sure, she was dreaming. "I —l—don't think I under- stand,"• she said piteously, turning to her companion. One wave of his arm sent old Ellen scurrying away. He took Mary Bramham by the hand and led her to a i chair. She sank into t like a woman in a trance. Something came, from his pocket, a plwtograph, old and yellow, but she knew it for her own: . Miss Bramham, I fell in love with that photograph ten years ago, and I told myself that someday I'd make myself w i ;rthy. And t was hard—hard work, but I tried, loving yen always—always. When the odds were heaviest I looked at that photograph and thought •of you. Every- thin'' I have become I owe to you—to you. But for you, I'd have been nothing. And then the luck came, too late to help your brother. Too late to help you, perhaps, but I wouldn't give in. I came over, I made i enquiries. Ths old vicarage was for sale. The new man wanted. a modern house; it was easy—too easy. And the furniture— bit by bit I picked it up, here and there. And then, when all was complete, I said you sliould eat ' your Christmas dinner in your own home, because it's my Christmas pre- sent to you, Mary." She Said she was still dreaming, but there was nothing dreamlike about the •strong clasp of his hands, nothing dreamlike about Es vibrant voice.</p>
        <p>"You'll take it from me?" he said. "You can't refuse—you made me; if I hadn't had you to dream of I'd have gone wider. Whatever James Stirling is, he owes to you. James Stirling! She knew him now. Often her brother had mentioned him in his Letters. "The whitest man God ever made, Mary." And, too, one of the richest Canada had made. James Stirling, the oil mil- 'lionaire !</p>
        <p>"You'll take it from me, Mary, your old home? You'll eat your Christmas dinner with me in your own home? It's complete now you're mistregis of it." "I can't. I can't take all this from you, Mr. James. Why, I've no present -to give you in exchange—nothing. I'm poor, I'm old."</p>
        <p>"You've got something that I want, something I mean to have, Mary. I want your love.. I've given, you mine these many Tears."</p>
        <p>She looked at him through happy tears. Someone needed her at last; she was no longer lonely. It was given to her to know that love would come to mer full-forced, that already it ivas. stirring in her starved heart. "I'm old—tired." The protest wa‘s over- come.</p>
        <p>"You're the loveliest woman on God's earth, my dear. When •I saw you last night I knew I'd dreamed aright. Mary, are my dreams coming true?" There was a growing warmth in her, but she thought nothing of his riches, nothing of the old grey—lite. All she thought or was this good man's love. She 'rested her hand on his bowed head, and a Smile of infinite tenderness cathe to her lips. "This is for your faithful service," she whispered, and bent forward. "Dinner is served," said Ellen discreetly, and they went in together, a king and queen entering into a heritage of joy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>-0.0-0--Q</head>
        <p>-0.0-0--Q</p>
        <p>Miss Bramham sighed very heavily and became once more conscious that she was thirty-five and very tired. Her feet ached, every muscle in her body ached; but most of all the throbbing agony in her head was hardest to be borne.</p>
        <p>The never-ceasing whirr and clatter of the money-carriers as they dashed along their wire tracks from counter to cashier's desk was a sound to drag hysterical screams from parched and pallid lips. The impatient labrdes of customers who had left their Christmas shopping until this late hour on Christmas Eve were snappish and hard to please. All these hurrying people about her had some pleasure in store on the morrow, even though now they snapped and almost snarled at one another. Across the stunning roar of voices would bite an occasional dis- tinct phrase: "Merry Christmas," "Compli- ments of the Season, some cheerful wish or other typical of the day. "A pair of suede gloves, sevens, grey—do be quick ! " Miss Bramham came back to earth with a start, and gazed through a mist of pain at a grey-faced, shrewish woman, whose lips were pursed tightly. She reached automatically for the glove-box, which she could have found, at midnight with her eyes blindfolded, and ran her fingers amongst its contents. " I'm sorry, madam, I fear we are out of sevens in the shade you require. One moment, please—" "No time to waste—catch my train— always out of things one really wants," came sharply, and the customer began to beat her way towards the door. Miss Bramham be- came aware that Mr. Boston; the chief shop- walker, pompous, terrible, was moving in her direction. She saw his hand reach to his pocket, saw it emer,ge with the hateful note- book.</p>
        <p>"You allowed that lady to go away with- out making a purchase, Miss--er—Miss--" "I'm sorry, sir, she would not wait. I tried to show her others." "Sixpence fine." It was booked in that terrible note-book. Miss Bramham knew that she must deny herself of the trivial luxury she had promised herself by way of honoyr- ing the morrow, for the loss of, that sixpence meant very much to her. \ "Be careful, Miss—er—Miss--" I said Mr. Boston. "At a time like this we expect a little extra—er—zeal. And dismissal fol- lows on neglect, remember." There it was again: the haunting spectre held up before her affrighted eyes, gibbering and mocking. She would have given much to retort, but commonsense forbade such a course.. And, too, Mr. Bostock had passed on, to compli- ment Miss Lethwick at the next counter on having sold a bewildered woman something she had no earthly need of. But Miss Leth- wick was only twenty-one; she had yellow hair, too, blue eyes, and a pink and white complexion. Miss Bramham, who possessed not one of these desirable embellishments, leaned against the edge of the counter until she saw Mr. Fosdyck's eyes resting upon her calculatingly, when sllb straightened her weary back with an effort, for weaknesses ot this kind were not tolerated at Christy's, Ltd., the World-Wide Providers. A loud, happy laugh sounded in her ears; she looked up quickly, hoping against hope that some of that laughter might, come her way. A young man, a clerk of sorts, was elbowing his way through the press, his face aglow with expectation. His eyes shone, his lips were curled in a smile, and Miss Bramham smiled back at him instinctively, with a little tingle of warmth cheering her. But his smile vanished, giving place to sur- prise and displeasure; another moment and he was smiling afresh, as Miss Grant ap- peared at Miss Bramham.'s elbow. "Oh, there you are, Floss! Thought ,you'd fallen down a trap or something. What's that! Oh! Boston watching? Well, give me two pairs of gloves—suit yourself, let me help to choose them." The glove-box was produced. The two heads bent close. Miss Bramham saw a gentle hand.,-pressure ad- ministered, heard a low-voiced conversation. Then: "Yes, those% do Al. How much? Here's half a quid. And, look here, Floss, I'll be waiting for you at ten o'clock to- morrow. Church with my people, dinner at home—we'll have no end of a time. Here—" The eleetrics glowed for a moment on the stones of the ring he half produced fr.om his vest-pocket. "That's your real present, little girl. Won't you let me buy another pair of gloves? All right—but next Christmas you won't be here. Happy Christmas—good- bye." Miss Bramham found herself fas- cinated by the swift colour in Miss Grant's cheeks. What could it be like, she won- dered, to listen to such words meant for her own ears? Such an experience had never come her way, because her youth had gone before ever she entered upon this _phase of life. There was not .a living soul to speak a cheering word to her, there never had been for many a long and weary year. Her only brother was lying in a Canadian grave, and there was no one else. _</p>
        <p>It was the sound of her own name on strange lips that brought her to herself just as she felt the shop sway around her. "Miss Bramham? Over there, at the glove counter—the elderly woman," came the answer, almost in a sneer. The crowd was thinning somewhat now; in a little while the shutters would go up for the night. Miss Bramham saw a burly figure elbow its way towards her counter; she found herself looking into a strong grey-brown face, deep lined, but possessing very young eyes. And those eyes smiled in a curiously enticing fashion, compelling her to smile, back. She did not know that ten good years were wlshecl from her face when her lips curled and little lights danced in her eyes. A broad-brimmed hat was lifted from a grizzled head, and that hat remained lifted, held in a muscular brown hand. "I'm seeking for Miss Bramham," came a strong voice, one that seemed used to com- mand. It was as though a sudden draught of fresh, unbreathed air had passed across Miss Bramham's brain. -._</p>
        <p>'Now, sir, what can we have the pleasure )f serving you with? Miss Bramham, attend to this gentleman's order, please." It was Mr. Boston again, of course, pompous, self- assertive, determined to impress the new- comer with a sense of his importance. "Considering I haven't made up my mind yet what I do want, aren't you in •a bit of a hurry?" demanded the stranger, whose hand had suddenly tightened on his soft hat. _ _ .</p>
        <p>"Quite so, sir, quite so." • Mr. Boston bowed himself away, outwardly obsequious, but the glance of his eye was malicious in Miss Bramham's direction. He lied long ago made up his mind that she must go, to give place to some younger and prettier girl, preferably of a kissable quality, for in a sneaking, hypocritical way the shop walker was by way of being an amorist. Miss Bramham knew. a little thrill of what might have been pleasure, but that desirable boon was so much a stranger that for the moment she failed to recognise it. Very seldom did she meet with chivalry in her bleak life. "Seems a bit inclined to interfere," said the frizzled man. "My, but I'd like to see him across a broncho, frock-coat and all complete." The suggestion tickled him, he smiled again, and Miss Bramham smiled sympathetically. The smiles grew in breadth until the stranger rocked with suppressed mirth. The sound of his gurgles drew Mr. Boston back. "We cannot have our assistants' time wasted in this manner, sir," was his vine- gary reprimand. "Gee! Can't you, now? Well, I s'po'se you're rimht. Gl.vpf4 you sell., don't vnu? ',lie me- ru .sew noxeci Nto oe- going on 'w-nn, please. I'll choose them, and I don't need your help, Mister Man." This was accom- panied by a hostile glance at Mr. Boston, who withdrew discrestly. Assuredly, Miss Bramham should go at the first opportunity, and as the Christmas rush was over—though the January sales would follow soon, and she was cheap and very reliable. Still, Mr. Boston would tolerate no trifling 'with • his monumental dignity, and Miss Bramham must understand that.</p>
        <p>.0- 0-0-0</p>
        <p>thought not;, please let me see your very best. And when they were produced he electrified Miss Bramham by saying: "Yes, that's a whole lot better. I'll take those, miss." "One pair, sir?" "No, the boxful." A swift mental calcu- lation apprised Miss Bramham that the total amount of the purchase was consider- able. She had a giddy vision of "spoofs," by which are meant commissions, and she could not -refrain—she was but human— from saying in a loud voice for Mr. Boston's benefit: "You will take the whole boxful, sir?" "Yes, for a start; now we'll look at some others." The next instalment he chose with scrupulous exactitude,, pair by pair. From time to time he looked upwards into Miss Bramham's tired eyes, and Suddenly sup- prised her by saying: - "Sit right down, you're about all in, aren't you?" "Bit down?" Miss Bramham faltered, aghast. "But—we're not allowed. It is, against the regulations." The customer emitted a snort. ,</p>
        <p>"You sit down, and if that man in the Prince Albert says a word I'll flatten him," he insisted. It was the kindly consideration of his manner that brought moisture to Miss Bramham's eyes. Not for a very long time indeed had any living soul taken thought for her comfort. She felt her weariness fly away as though at the compelling touch of a healing hand.</p>
        <p>"Reall, I don't need to sit down," she said. "I much prefer to stand." "Well, please yourself. Look here, Miss Brainham, what time dces this place close? Or is it always open?" She glanced at the clock; A was on the stroke of eleven. "We keep open later on Christmas Eve, of course. But we shall be closing at once." "Right. Then I'll wait outside for you. If I stay here lOnger I shall be rude to that man there." He cast a glance of no friendliness in Mr. Boston's direction, and the fist on the counter clenched again. She parcelled up his purchases like a wonma Jn a dream, unable to believe the evidence of her senses. To be spoken to like a hilman being at all was a strange experience enough, but to have someone, and that someone the possessor of a kindly smile and a resonant, powerful voice, show a distinct interest in her, to desire more of her society, was bewilderment itself.</p>
        <p>"Sign, please." Mr. Boston fussed for- ward, checked the items, the considerable sum was sent whirring to the cashier's desk. The yawning girl there whizzed the change back. It was all dreamlike.</p>
        <p>"Don't say a word, .old Brammy's got a mash," came from somewhere within near- ing. "It's Methuselah himself—they're a pretty pair." Miss Bramham awoke to realities. What a fool she was! The stran- ger had merely wanted to while away a few minutes prior to catching a train But he was waiting outside when she emerged; he came forward with a brisk step</p>
        <p>``You don't think I'm over bold, do you? Which way? Shall we get a taxi?" His manner was something more excitable than before ; he volleyed the questions at her as though consumed by nervousness.</p>
        <p>"I- should like Co walk. 'After constant standing one feels so—so--" "Yes, yes; quite so. I understa/nd." She staggered ia little as he spoke, for the day had seemed endless. Instantly he seized her hand and tucked it beneath his elbow with calm masterfulness. She leaned on his strength gratefully; it was vastly pleasant to be cared for in this manner.</p>
        <p>"I seem to have known you for ages," he said presently, more with an object of making conversation than for any other reason. "By the way, you don't know my name, I think. It's James—Mr. James. I knew your brother—" "Oh, you knew Jack!" She had said that she was lacking in all sense of decency in thus accepting the escort of an unknown man, but she had pleaded with her own con- science for a lax judgment; it was Christ- mas, when the conventions were somewhat slackened. And now she found that con- science very. tolerant. "Yes, I knew him fairly well. It was a great blow to me when he died. put I was away, prospecting; got knocked up, ice- bound; six solid months of it. He spoke once or twice to me about you, made me promise to look you up if ever I came over to the old country. This is the first chance I've had. Tell me something about life in that place. Do they treat you decently?" And the kindliness of his voice broke down her reserve completely. Suddenly Christy's, Limited, assumed to her mind the propor- tions of a huge, devastating juggernaut, crushing hope, youth, love, everything be- neath its clamorous, resistless wheels. There was a sob in her voice as she spoke of the bitterness of it all, and Mr. James patted her hand in rough sympathy. "It's the—the unendingness of tat all," she said in self-extenuation. "Forgive me, please, for wearying you with my woes, but I'm tired, and—and being shown a little sympathy— Tell me about Jack."</p>
        <p>- But the telling must needs be postponed. They had reached the mean street where she lived; sh'.. had halted at her own door and was fumbling for her key. "You mean to say you Jive here? How d;) you breathe ? " "Oh, it isn't so bad as all that." There was almost cheerfulness in her tone. "Up on the top flight, near the sky, you know, one gets the air. And it is very convenient for Christy's." He, stood back to stare up- wards, baring his head. "Now I must go." She • was amazed at the curious feeling of recklessness that pos- sessed her. She was net in the least ashamed of standing here with et man practically un- known. "Thank you so much, I should like to wish you a very hal:my Christmas." She inserted the key in the latch, and turned to hold out her hand, curiously reluctant, for behind that door were all the old grey shadows and regrets, "Wait a minute, ' Miss Bramha.m." His voice was not quite steady: " I—l—want to ask a favour of you. I don't know a soul in London beyond yourself—l'm a lonely old Colonial. Will you do me the honour, the great honour, of—of eating Christmas din- ner with me? I am selfish, but if you'd take pity on the loneliest man in London, and— and--" . - - _ - -</p>
        <p>The recklessness seized her afresh. It was more than unconventional, but—she thought of the lonely room and the meagre meal. There was a lamp just opposite, and he Faw a faint flush on her cheeks, something that was almost brightness in her eyes. _</p>
        <p>"The loneliest woman in London will eat Christmas dinner with you," she said, with a queer gaiety. In a whirl, aghast at her temerity, she heard him •making plans. A motor wa,s mentioned, eleven o'clock, and then she was inside. The door 'was closed and she ;was pressing trembling hands to her bl,—;/17 "-You ought to be ashamed of yourself," she said sharply. "At your age, too—like one of those silly girls!" But the remark- able thing was she was not ashamed; more, she had no intention of being _ashamed. The steep flights of stairs had never seemed so easy to negotiate. The room she ent,a.ed was almost cheery. Christmas had sud- denly become something desirable, some- thing to look forward to hopefully, no longer with dread. Morning broke to bring a strangely civil landlady clamouring at the door. Miss Bramham opened her eyes—another weary day lay before her. No! There was a scent of flowers about, a sweet fragrance that re- minded her of her youth. The landlady entered, a bouquet in her hands: violets, lilies; their perfume filled the room. "A merry Christmas, Miss. These was left for you." Pinned to the flowers was a card, be the name "Mr. James" written in pencil, and a cheerful greeting. Miss Bramham mentally reviewed her possi- bilities of clothing, and found herself hum- ming a Christmas carol! "Life owes me one day of happiness," she tried. "It will be something to look back on--afterward;." And long before eleven plc. _WV chewed in tha has,* har lima=</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>POLICE TRAPS AND CRIME.</head>
        <p>POLICE TRAPS AND CRIME.</p>
        <p>Serious crime flay be committed while the police are trapping motorists. In Wimbledon quite recently there were several large houses broken into., and the thieves got away'with good hauls, writes ii correspondent in the " Autocar." The robberies eontinued tint it one night, after the third attempt, the owner of a house, hear- ing someone downstairs, telephoned' to the police station and had the thieves arrested. Strange to say, one of them was a motor mechanic, who must have known that the police Were intent on their trapping duties at Wimble- don Hill and Park Side. He did not reckon with the people living in the house.</p>
      </div>
