      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>J i .../: ~. ____,,,,,-C .</head>
        <p>J i .../: ~. ____,,,,,-C .</p>
        <p>REVENGE SECURED AFTER MANY YEARS.</p>
        <p>Wigan came to Wilderspool full of confidence and went away full of disappointment. They were overwhelmed by the grit and perseverance displayed by the home side. The score of four points to nil in favour of Warrington does not represent the general character of the play, which created such a surprise to the." Colliers," who, in the past, have rarely Met with a similar experience at Warrington. Although confident of victory, Wigan left _nothing to chance, and they turned out the strongest ' side, even down to making Gwyn Thomas re-appear at full-back. It was well for. Wigan he was there, for Thomas alone kept the score from being a :very .large one against Wigan. Warrington Won by two penalty goals, lnit had it not been' for exceptionally' good luck for Wigan, at least three tries would have been scored. Three times Warrington men were badly obstructed when a try seemed certain. ,The game in its early stages was level, but the hustling tactics pursued by the Warring- tonians quite upset the Wigan men; their much-talked-about_ threequarters never got a hance, and their half-backs were completely eclipsed by the play of Nicholas and Young.</p>
        <p>By</p>
        <p>Jolley, as the Wigan custodian is a great player, and on Saturday worthily upheld the reputation he has made for himself since joining the Wigan organisation. He is one of the finest full-backs I have yet seen„ and certainly had it not been for his magnificent work Warrington would most probably have crossed the Wigan lines on more occasions than one. , Ruddick again kept up his reputation as a wing threequarter, and gave a dashing display, and most certainly had hard lines in not scor- ing. With a speedy centre, say, like Walford or Jenkins, nothing could have kept him out on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Ruddick to the Front</head>
        <p>Ruddick to the Front</p>
        <p>Ruddick soon came to the front, and only the .adroit tactics of Gwyn Thomas kept him from scoring. Nicholas a minute or two later neatly dived over the line.. Then :the for- wards, who were rather inferior to Wigan in the tight, gave a display of their masterly control over the ball in the loose, and with a Soccer rush .they almost dribbled .the 'ball over. A Warringtonian was badly obstructed, and a free kick was awarded. This Jolley took from near the touch line and kicked a pretty goal. Warrington, up to the interval, had inore than their share of the play, and half-time found them leading by two points to nil. The second half .was entirely in favour of Warrington, and but for occasional bursts, generally arising out of free kicks, Wigan were hemmed in their half of the pitch, and it was now that Gwyn Thomas brought off his clever and at times extremely lucky saves. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <head>Bradshaw's Hard Luck.</head>
        <p>Bradshaw's Hard Luck.</p>
        <p>Bradshaw experienced very hard luck through being jockeyed into touch when it seemed cer- tain that he would score. George Dickenson effected a fine kick and follow up, and had he any other back than Thomas to face a score would have ensued. Gwyn was lucky in get- ting the ball away. • gore fine , footwork by the Warrington for- Wards ensued, and in this Clare and Skelhorne Were conspicuous. Trauter, Young and End- 'dick also did fine work, but luck plus .Gwyn Thomas was opposed .to them in the way of a score. Later on Wigan were penalised, and Thomas kicked a second penalty goal. War- rington pressed right to the close, but could not Add, to the total, and ran out winners by four Points, to nil. • .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>" LAKELANDER'S " CRITIQUE.</head>
        <p>" LAKELANDER'S " CRITIQUE.</p>
        <p>A Battle Worth Watching.</p>
        <p>The Warrington supporters haVe been accus. turned, in matches of recent "date on the Wil- (lerspool enclosure, to see their faviiurites pull the match out o the fire by penalty goals. -This fact was again demonstrated on Saturday, When the Cracks Wigan team were defeated by •J'eur points to nil. It must have .been a great 'disappointment to the "Colliers'" supporters to see their team leave the "field a battle" Without having even scored a solitary point, and, it must be a long time since they have 'failed to penetrate the opposition's defence: . _ _</p>
        <p>- And a battle it was! . Well worth going miles to see</p>
        <p>It was a great game and the best I</p>
        <p>Lave witnessed on the Wilderspool enclosure for -•fiona,e- time past. . Warrington were value for their victory, and he would he a hard mortal indeed who did not give- them full credit for 'their win.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>Beaten at Their Own Game</head>
        <p>Beaten at Their Own Game</p>
        <p>They_simPly beat the Wiganers at their own game, 'and sheer dogged perseverance had its 'reward.</p>
        <p>At the close of hostilities I overheard a once Prominent official of the Warrington Club say 'that "the play, of the Wigan team was not Wigan form, or, if it was, they were in a false Position in the League tableN The same gen- tleman also remarked that •" Warrington were Weak in attack, which was proved by the fact that they had failed to 'score tries in no less than five matches of late." I have always had n high opinion of this gentleman as an authority op the N.LJ. game, but I am bound to differ him as regards Saturday's encounter. lii tho. first place I thought Wigan gave a splendid exbibition -of football, and one that certainly 110leld the great name they have won in foot- ball circles, but One thing ~stood between them 411 d victory, and that was the fact, that War- ngton played a better game, and proved that '‘.103- have at last found their true •form.</p>
        <p>Wigan's Fine Luck. I cannot see where Warrington were weak in uttack, and they certainly were not defending their lines to the same extent as Wigan, and hind the advantage of their opponents for fully till'ee parts of the game. It was sheer luck—on Wigan's part—which kept Dickeivon, Brad- shaw, Young and Ruddick from crossing over. In fact, I am of the opinion that Dickenson was So badly obstructed that be ought to have .been awarded a try. 'Wigan's threequarters Uli- !ouhtedly were a much speedier lot than the Warrington quartet, and gave us many a thrill by their fine exhibition of the ,handling code, but they we'rl3 met by a sturdy .defence, and Warrington's 'tackling was deadly in ,the ex- treme.</p>
        <p>Warrington's Half-backs Superior. . Where' Wigan were weakest, in my opinion, Was at half-back, and it was a surprise to me to Nee such two players as Johnny Thomas and " Dodger" 0,e1:1 so cleverly outwitted as they were by Young and. /sficholas. But space will /let admit me to criticise the game, so I will lifine myself. to the doings of our local players. To pick out any individual player for special olention would be a very difficult matter, as each one was a " star" artiste in himself. To start with Ben Jolley, I congratulate the Run- corn lad on his display. Be once. more 'proved Llis great worth to the Warrington Club. He judgment, and, with the ex- 'eked with great ption of misfielding on two -occasions, gave a faultless exhibition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>Jolley as Good as Thomas.</head>
        <p>Jolley as Good as Thomas.</p>
        <p>There was i;ot Inuch to choose between him 4111(1 Gwyn Thomas, and this speaks moci, for</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>Ruddick's Big Tackles.</head>
        <p>Ruddick's Big Tackles.</p>
        <p>And: his, tackling! No matter how big hi, opponent, whether the great Seeling or anyone else, down went 'Ruddick for his legs, and sel- dom did he fail. This is a ,great point I have often noticed .among Cumberland and West- morland players, and they are invariably the finest tacklers one can meet with in any of the crack N.U. teams to-day. I have often won- dered why the Warrington Committee do not make a trip up. North, instead of always angling after players in .Wales. There are plenty of clubs still in existence in both Cumberland and Westmorland, and I have 'seen many a lad in junior clubs well worthy of a trial in better class football. There have been plenty of "f" captured from there in days gone by— Lomas, Ferguson, Bob Wilson, Little, Eagers, Tom Fletcher, to mention Only a few—and why not now? But this is by the way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0214" n="art0214">
        <head>Bradshaw's Old Form.</head>
        <p>Bradshaw's Old Form.</p>
        <p>Bradsbaw, on the other wing, was more like the Bradshaw of old, and played a much-im- proved game. Though marked by a much speedier opponents, he was not disgraced by any means, and made strenuous efforts to break through on several occasions. The two centres, Dickenson and Tratiter, both sbowed great improvement, and as two defen- sive players could not be beaten. The former most certainly has lost a lot of speed, but he certainly 'made the Wiganers "go all the way" when he almost scored. As I mentioned above, I think he Wits badly obstructed, and Mr. Hirst ought to have allowed the try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0215" n="art0215">
        <head>Tranter versus Seeling.</head>
        <p>Tranter versus Seeling.</p>
        <p>Tranter has one big fault, and that is, he is rather slow in getting off the mark. But the way he gave Seeling and Co. the " dummy " on several occasions was very smart indeed. - .To como to the -half-back position,, I think bath Young and Nicholas were "great." The former, in my opinion, was the smartest half- backs, on the field. Johnny Thomas at times was made to look small at his own game. • There is a great similarity between the play of these two men, and both stand well behind the pack— a big mistake some people think. As I have mentioned in these columns before, my idea is that it proves it is possible to make openings without running round the scrummages, and at tie same time avoid being tackled. And Young did make openings! ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0216" n="art0216">
        <head>Young Made Fine Openings.</head>
        <p>Young Made Fine Openings.</p>
        <p>He got his "threes" going times without number. In fact, he played himself to a stand- still. He has the makings of one of the finest half-backs in N.U. football, and I offer him my congratulations on his fine effort against what is undoubtedly one of the best pair of half- backs playing to-day. Nicholas, too, was a worker, and played a sound game. I never saw him work so well with his partner, and a thorough understanding seems to exist between -the two.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0217" n="art0217">
        <head>Clare in the Picture.</head>
        <p>Clare in the Picture.</p>
        <p>It was a grand game between the forwards, although I thought Wigan' had the advantage in the pack. In the loose, however, Warring- ton were far smarter than their oppOnents, and are undoubtedly one of the fastest packs in the League. It would be unfair to individualise amongst them, as each one went on the field to win, and played throughout with that deter- mination. Clare was often' in the picture, and George Thomas's experience was of great assist- ance to the younger members of the pack. Thomas is a grand leader, and few forwards from the Principality' have given their club such useful service as Thomas has done to War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0218" n="art0218">
        <head>FINE WIN Al' LEIGH.</head>
        <p>FINE WIN Al' LEIGH.</p>
        <p>A Five Points to Nil Victory.</p>
        <p>Warrington spent a merry Christmas Day. They went to Leigh and found the Mather-lane' men in the pink of form. The home side were anxious to wipe out the defeat sustained at Wilderspool on the opening day of the season, and they played the best game they have yet done on the home pitch this 'season, yet they were defeated by five points to nil. There could be .no doubt but that Warrington were the smarter side, and the WildersPoolians thor- oughly deserved the victory, which materially gave them a decided leg-up in the League table. The game all through was fast and exciting, with more thrills in it than is usually fond in holiday matches. The first half was Marked by hard, even play, in which the splendid defensive •tactics developed by Warrington recently proved too Much for Leigh. At half-. time the score sheet was blank,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0219" n="art0219">
        <head>Penalty Coal and a Try.</head>
        <p>A Penalty Coal and a Try.</p>
        <p>It was once more a forwards' game, and this was most strongly demonstrated in the second' half, when the Warrington men Came away with a lot of their first-class footwork, and with this Leigh could do little. Three times Leigh: were penalised for offside. Jolley failed with the first two attempts, but with 'the third, which he kicked from near the touch-line, the back landed one of his characteristic goals</p>
        <p>-- Soon after Warrington came away- with a spurt, in which the threequarters had the chief concern, /and after Tranter had manceuvred a nice opening, he handed charge 'of the ball to Fearnley. The latter, with a terrific burst of speed, rushed over and planted a, try near the corner. The angle was too difficult to negotiate and the goal was not forthcoming. Thus by one goal and one try to nil Warrington won a fine-, interesting game.</p>
        <p>SALFORD'S VISIT TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>The New Year's Matches.</p>
        <p>Warrington have a visit from Salford to-day and a good game should ensue, with Warrington</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. First Division. Rylands' Defeat Swinton Park. Rylands' made their position at the top of the League more secure by obtaining another fine victory through defeating Swinton Park, at Swinton. They have played five matches and won all, having 10 points to their credit. Pendleton, next on the list, have 10 points for eight matches. Great interest was centred in the Swinton match. Swinton Park were the leaders of the Second Division last season, going through the competition without a defeat. They' were also strongly represented. Rylands', who were short of Stretford, kicked off before a fair at- tendance, and T. Downs, being collared in pos- session, attempted to pass to Lewis, who mulled, and H. Morris scored for Rylands' in the first minute of the game. Warren failed at goal. This reverse roused the Park, and they began to bombard the " Wiremen's" lines, but the defence held good. Carter, taking a pass from Gallop, scored, H. Morris _failing at goal. From a penalty Warren kicked a goal, half-time arriving with the score': •Rylands', one goal two tries (eight points) to Swinton Park, nil. Playing down the brow, Swinton Park com- menced to make things lively, but condition told, and Rylands', wearing the opposition down, commenced a movement among all their backs for three-parts of the length of the field, Griffiths finishing a ;grand piece of work by scoring under the posts, Warren converting. After this the Park were a beaten team, and Broadhurst, ,charging a kick, scored, Warren again converting, the final score ending in favour of the " Wiremen " by 3. goals 4 tries (18 points) to Swinton Park, nil. ' All the Rylands' team played well to a man, and Downs was the saviour of his aide, and played a grand game.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. Rylands' Recs. "A" Beat Thatto Heath.</p>
        <p>The match with Thatto Heath, in St. Helens and District League was played at orsey-lane before a poor attendance._ The " Wiremen " turned out with four reserve men. The first half was very even, until Skidmore livened matters up by scoring a try for the visitors, Fillingham failing at goal. Rylands' forced matters and Warren scored a good try. Potter failed with the goal-kick. At this stage the Thatto Heath full-back retired hurt, and this handicapped the team considerably. Further tries were scored for Rylands' by Welsby (2) and '3/Lyles (1), none being converted. Woods also dropped a good goal for the visitors from a free-kick. Final: Rylands' Recs. "A," 12 points; Thatto Heath, 5 points. Warren, Welsby and Myles played well for Rylands', whilst Skidmore and Woods were the pick of the Thatto Heath team.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION CUP. Second Qualifying Round. Rylands' Recs. v. Pemberton Rovers. This match takes place at Gorsey-lane to-day, when Rylands' will play: P. Horrocks; J. Grif- fiths, S. ,Carter, A. Horrocks and P. Morris; G. Warren and J. Gallop: A. Stretford, H. Morris, J. Whittaker, J. Broadhurst, F: MasOn and J. Brandwood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>WARRINGTON "A" BEATEN. A Set-back at Wigan.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON "A" BEATEN. A Set-back at Wigan.</p>
        <p>Warrington "A" secured the -first set-back sus- tained for some time by the defeat at Wigan, but this ought to have only a temporary effect upon their position in the Combination table: It was very hard lines upon our local " A's " to have been beaten at Wigan, for they had more than their share of the game, and this they did admirably with only eleven men throughout the second half. Waywell scored a try for War- rington in the first portion, and this was not converted. Wigan were fortunate to get a goal anfl a try. Half-time: Wigan, five points; War- rington, three points. In the second half, although two men. short, Warrington did everything but score, as their play entitled them to do. Wigan, through Price, got a penalty goal, and a try and another penalty goal fell to Wigan's bag. Baker, after a fine bit of work, scored for Warrington. Critchley converted, and the score ended 12 .points to eight in favour of Wigan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM. Pithy Gossip About N.U. Games.</head>
        <p>PICKS OUT OF THE SCRUM. Pithy Gossip About N.U. Games.</p>
        <p>Wigan came to conquer. They returned to mourn. It is a long time since Wigan met such a team. On the local pitch at Wilderspool. Who begrudges Warrington the victory? There are some folk who do. It was no use, however, attempting To minimise the local lads' fine win. They were easily the tip-top players. The home supporters were delighted. They had every reason to be so. Warrington sprang a surprise, which The Collier lads did not like. They brought their strongest possible , Twelve—and Gwyn Thomas. The twelve were badly beaten. Gwyn Thiimas alone kept, his head And kept the Warrington score down, He had one of his lucky days out. Luck, helped him to prevent the Wilderspool men from getting tries. Three times Dame Fortune thus came To the aid of Thcimas—and Wigan. Warrington all played a great game. The forwards dribbled splendidly. Their footwork might have enlightened Some crack Association football players. Clare was always in the picture. . Thomas Was again the ploughman of the side. He delighted in ploughing up Wigan. Young was the smartest half-back Seen on the field- that day, and Nicholas was a very gostl second. The Warrington pair threw the Wigan couple Pretty far into the shade. Ruddick was too much for the Wigan 'crack threequarter backs, and His tackles simply amazed them. Bradshaw played a lovely game. Some of his runs well deserved 'tries. George Dickenson was a host in himself. He played a very sound, fine game. ,Tranter likewise showed much improvement. Jolley was often in the.forefront, and, Compared with the " elassy " Gwyn Thomas, He was quite as good in every department. If such form is maintained, points will come. Rylands' are going strong just now. They are easily top of the Manchester League. Five matches they have played and won all. Pendleton are their rivals in the table, But they have played eight games for 10 points. Swinton Park have been their latest victims. This team' Iylandsl,beat at Swinton With the handsome majority of 18 points. Rylands' have an N.U. cup-tie to-day. They meet Pemberton Rovers at Gorsey-lane. Rylands' are out to win' and it looks as If it will go pretty hard with the Rovers.</p>
        <p>Yesterday's match with Oldham will be found on Page 5.</p>
        <p>There were several good games witnessed in the Widnes Medal Competition, the tit-bit of the round being the meeting of the Orford St. John's and Warrington Excelsior at Long-lane. There was a good crowd preset, and the ex- citement was high. The 'home team managed to gain the verdict by the only goal of the match, scored from a penalty kick.</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors met St. Barnabas', and a vig- orously contested game was witnessed, the visit- ors, having had more experience than their opponents, being successful in winning by two goals to none.</p>
        <p>- Emmanuel Old Boys met the Bank Quay Recs. in the 'same competition tin the Victoria Park, and after a hard and gruelling game, the Rem were declared winners by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. Oughtrington United v. Latchford. \ At Heatley. The home team was well repre- sented, while the visitors were short of the ser- vices of Siddall and E. Webb. The former was unable to-play owing to a bereavement, and the latter was assisting Altrincham. The follow- ing players turned out :—Oughtrington United: 11. Burrows; J. Thomasson and F. Moss; F. Lord, W. Lord and Owen; H. Thomasson, Had- don, Holt, Jones and Rouse. Latchford: Kite; Robinson and Haspell;. Caldwell, Downs and Carter; Smith, P. Webb, Lees, Fogg and Tin- der, Referee, J. Smallwood. The. home. team stared in fine style, and put the visitors' goal under siege, during which Jones sent in a very awkward shot, only to see,Kite make a splendid save. The home team continued to have the best of the exchanges, when Rouse sent in a rattling shot, but Kite would not yield. From this point the visitors had more of the game, and play was transferred to the home area, Moss and Thomasson playing well under strong pres- sure, and the ball was sent into the visitors' half, only to be returned by Haspell, and Downs obtained the sphere and passed out to Smith on the wing, and the latter centring finely from near the corner flag, Lees was up and passed to Fogg, to score the first goal with a capital shot. Half-time was called with the score in the visitors' favour.</p>
        <p>The visitors made • a raid on the home goal, and Fogg had hard lines with a grand effort. From this relief the home team played more vigorously, and made a prolonged attack on the visitors' goal, Haspell defending grandly. Un- fortunately' for the 'visitors, owing to a misun- derstanding between Kite and Haspell, the latter 'diverted the 'ball just out of the goal- keeper's reach, and Rouse rushed up, put the ball into the net, and equalised the score. From the centre kick some midfield exchanges were very even, and as the end was drawing near the visitors tried hard to gain the lead, but the home defence foiled all their efforts.. Result: Oughtrington, one goal; Latchford, one goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0222" n="art0222">
        <head>STOCKTOji HEATH MAKE AMENDS,</head>
        <p>STOCKTOji HEATH MAKE AMENDS,</p>
        <p>The Heathens Win the Medal tie Latchford Divide the Points_ in the League Match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0223" n="art0223">
        <head>DIVEX ARE ROUTED BY GREAT SANKEY.</head>
        <p>DIVEX ARE ROUTED BY GREAT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>By "Linesman"</p>
        <p>There was only a moderate affeiclance at West Avenue to witness the Northwich Medal tie, when a fine, keen and interesting game re- sulted in a win for the home team by the only score of the match. ' The visitors cams with a good reputation, and last season they were winners of the Crewe and District League, and up to the present they had not lost a match in the League this. season. They are also joint holders with Barnton Villa of the Northwich and District Cup for last season. By their vic- tory the home players reinstated themselves in the favour of their supporters. The visitors were not quite at full strength, their absentees not being able to leave their work for the early start from :Moulton, while there were four 'changes in the home ranks, Harrison, Glover, Jefferson and Woodhead, of the Reserves being called upon. Buckley., Cawley, E. Tomlinson, Andrews and Smith were the most prominent for the visitors, while Denny, Glover, Jefferson, Woodward and Smith were the best of the home players.</p>
        <p>In the North Cheshire League, Oughtrington United and Latchford played their return fix- ture at lleatley, when, after a keen and 'well. fought contest, the, points were divided.</p>
        <p>when in a favourable position, and at this junc- ture the visitors were playing the best football, but their final efforts were not successful. As time Was drawing hear, both sets of players had a spell of attacking, and Woodhead centred finely from the touch-line. Jones trapped the ball in front of goal, but failed to score, and at the other end the home goal had a lucky escape, Regan sending across the goalmouth. Andrews handled the ball a yard from the goal line, the score not counting. Shortly after time was called. Result: Stockton Heath, one goal; Moulton Alexandra, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE</p>
        <p>NOTES.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League a—. very interesting game was witnessed between Weston and Adult School. The game ended in a tie of one goal each. The match was keenly con- tested throughout, and the score fairly repre- sents the game. Nancollis, in the home goal, made several smart saves and was backed up by Barnes. Aspey and Kirkham also performed well, and W. Hunter, Dykes, Banner and Leigh did well for the visitors. ,</p>
        <p>The Daresbury Club journeyed to Acton Bridge, and had to return defeated by five goals to one. J. Lee-Jones, Ebroll, S. De Prez and Ravenscroft played well for the visitors. The home tea.m was assisted best by Woodward, Newton, Evans, Alcock and Hough. The meeting of the Divex and Great Sankey proved a very one-sided game, the visitors win- ning easily.</p>
        <p>The only game in the Second Division proved to be keenly fought, and resulted in the Fair- field United claiming their first victory of the season, and this at the expense of their rivals Fairfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
        <head>NORTHWICH AND DISTRICT COMPETITION. Stockton Heath v. Moulton Alexandra.</head>
        <p>NORTHWICH AND DISTRICT COMPETITION. Stockton Heath v. Moulton Alexandra.</p>
        <p>\ This cup and medal tie was played on The West Avenue ground. The following were the sides :—Stockton Heath: Harrison; Jefferson and Woodward; Glover, Denny and Gifford; Woodhead;, Lockhart, Janes, Smith and Taylor. Moulton Alexandra: Leatham; C. Buckley and Reaney; Watton,, Cawley and F. Tomlinson; -.Regan, E. ' Tomlinson, Andrews, Burgess and Smith. Referee, R. Watkins' The first ad- vance was made by the home forwards, but they were driven back • by Buckley, and the visitors raced away to the opposite end, where Harrison had, to concede a corner. The flag kick was well placed, and Denny headed away. A free kick for hands against Tomlinson put the visitors on the defensive, Cawley clearing with good play. The home team was at this time having the best of the game, when Smith missed an easy chance • in front of goal, while shortly after the same player was only inches wide with a good shot. This brought relief to the visitors' defence, and following some good midfield Play, Woodward handled just outside the penalty area, the free kick not benefiting the visitors, whilst another smart attack on the visitors' citadel was of no avail. Following up some keen exchanges the visitors attacked the home goal strongly, but could not break through their defence, Glover and Denny being promi- nent. The ball was passed out to Woodhead, and he put in a grand everhead kick, which -Reaney misjudged,,Lockhart fastened on the ball and beat Leatham with a grand shot. From •the centre kia the visitors played keenly at the home goal, and Smith's final shot went high over the bar. At the other end Leatham had to handle several times. The ball was transferred to the home • quarters by good combination, where Burgess shot over the bar with an open goal before him. Half-time found the Heathens leading by one goal to nil. The visitors at once became very aggressive, Harrison having to concede a corner, but no score was made. However, the visitors kept up the pressure and gained another fruitless corner. free kick for hands brought relief to the home defence, and Leatham was called upon to clgar a weak shot by Lockhart. Following a spell of midfield play, Regan Shot over the bar,</p>
        <p>Weston v. Adult School. The return fixture between the above clubs was played on the ground of the former, when the following players represented their clubs Weston: Nancollis; Oldfield and Barnes; G. Oldfield, Smith and Molyneux; R. Conway, Kirkham, Aspey, Hulse and Hope. Adult School: Welsby ; Garnett and Leigh; Barton, Hunter and Clarke; Rowe, Dykes, Rutter, Muckley and Banner. The tame started at a very hot pace. The visitors pressing, could not break down the home defence, Nancollis and his backs withstanding the pressure. A periai of midfield play ensued until the Adults,, by good combination, got to close quarters, Nancollis making a brilliant save with the visiting for- wards on him. Following this the home team applied pressure on the visitors' goal, but were stubbornly resisted. Hunter, Garnett and Welsby defended grandly. Each goal was visited in turn. Both sets of forwards missed opportunities of scoring, and the interval ar- rived without any score being made. The game was as keenly contested in the second as in the first half. After twenty min- utes had elapsech the visitors were awarded a penalty, Rutter taking the kick. The score was disallowed owing to an infringement of the rules, and the same player took the kick over again and shot wide of the posts. The home forwards set up an attack on the visitors' goal, when a good shot rebounded into play off one of the defenders: Aspey fastened on the 'ball and scored a good goal for the home team. Net- tled by this reverse, the Adults played more vigorously, and were soon in their opponents' quarters, and after Nancollis had saved several times, Dykes secured the ball and with a half volley shot equalised the score. An interest- ing game ended in a draw of one goal each.</p>
        <p>Weaver Refining Co. v. Daresbury. At Acton Bridge. Players :—Weaver: Mills;, Woodward and Newton; Johnson, Alcock and Beans; Evans, Hough, Newton, Lester and Powell. Daresbury: Ravenscroft ; S. De Prez and 11. Dean; Ryder, Cash and W. Forster; L. Forster, D. Adams, J. Lee-Jones, F. Forster and B. Ebroll. The opening exchanges were fairly even,. when Evans, of the home team, made a grand individual effort, running three- partsc; the length f the field, beatilig several Opponents, only to e his final shot go wide of the .mark. The visitors were kept on the de- fensive and Lester beat Ravensereft with a good effort, and immediately after Hough scored a second goal. The interval arrived with the home team leading by two goals to none. The visitors were the first to become aggres- sive, and Lee4ones tested Mills with a very good shot, which he Mulled, but managed to regain it and prevent a score being ' made. This partial success gave the visitors encouragement, and they kept the home team on the defence. Lee- Jones led his forwards well, and passing out to Ebroll, he scored a good goal for the visitors. Following this the homesters played more keenly, and Alcock sent in two very good long shots, while at closer quarters Lester was more successful, and scored the third goal. The home team were awarded a free kick for hands just outside the penalty area, from which Alcock increased the lead. The visitors now fell off in their play, and the game was not very inter- esting, the home team monopolising the play. Newton scored goal No. 5, and time was called with the result: Weaver Refining Co., five goals; Daresbury, one goal.</p>
        <p>. _ Divex v. Great Sankey. The meeting of these two teams on the ground of the former did not arouse much interest. The following players took part :—Divex Jones; Flute and Penfold ; Williams, Pender- gast and Savage; Wright, Curwen, Roberts, Bul- lock and Dronfield. Great Sankey: Bond; Stephens and Glover; 11. Ditchfield, Gardner and Payne; Bennett, Darlington, Poulton, .Houghton and J. Ditchfield.. Roberts kicked off towards the railway. The visitors pressed and several shots were very near, Darlington missing by inches. Directly after the same player was responsible for opening the score. The visitors continued to have nearly all their own way. J. Ditchfield and Darlington \added to the score. Then the home team roused themselves and in- vaded the visitors' 'lines, Bullock hitting the cross-bar with a hard drive. Bennett and J. Ditchfield scored again for the visitors, and at half-time the score was seven goals to none. The second half was a repetition of the first, and goals were scored by Darlington, Stephens, Gardner and J. Ditchfield. Result: Great San- key, 11 goals; Divex, nil. The goal scorers were: Darlington (5), Ditchfield (3), Bennett, Stephens and Gardner.</p>
        <p>Fairfield United v. Fairfield. Owing to these, clubs being near neighbours and each playing on the same pitch on alternate Saturdays, this match between them had been eagerly looked forward, to, and was anticipated in the sense of a " Derby " day, the spectators being as keen as the players. The players faced each other as follows :—Fairfield United: Hill; Butler and Woodhouse; Rigby, Bebbington and Skelmardine; Jackson, Short, Simcock-, Oxley and Gregory. • Fairfield: Dainteth; Hughes and Webb; Taylor, Roberts and Jackson; Broad- hurst, Millington„Applefon, Barker and nate. Appleton set the ball rolling and an attack on the United goal was cleared by Woodhouse. The leather was transferred to the opposite end, and after fifteen Jninutes' play Short opened the score for United. The Fairfield lads were kept in their own half defending. Jackson made a splendid solo effort and finished up witli a fine oblique shot, which completely beat Dainteth, and the United were two goal's up. Nettled by this second reverse the Lads played determin- edly, put the presstlre on the United goal and were awarded a penalty against one of the de- fenders. Hughes was entrusted with the kick and scored from . the second attempt. When half-time was. called United were leading by two goals to one.</p>
        <p>The game was very evenly contested, the de- fence on both sides giving an improved display, neither goalkeeper being seriously troubled. The play was chiefly in midfield, where some keen tussles took place for possession. However, the United gradually began to *ear down the Lads' defence and eventually Simeock increased the score for the United. From the centre kick the game was contested with renewed vigour, but neither side managed to score again, and when time was called the United ran out winners by. three goals to one. This is the first vietory the United have obtained in the League tourna- ment.</p>
        <p>WIDNES MINOR MEDAL CyMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Orford Juniors v. St. Birnabas'.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former. The game opened very keen -and fast, both goals being at- tacked in turn. The Juniors became the more dangerous. Spencer cleared with a huge kick,</p>
        <p>St. Wilfrid's Reserves met Higher Walton Reserves at Walton. Grappenhall were the first to attack, but were repulsed by the Walton backs. Cameron had "hard lines" with a nice dropping shot, missing by about six inches. Walton now got going, and a good centre by Brereton enabled Beswick to score. Grappen- hall again pressed, but could not get past the defence of the home backs, who were playing a splendid game. Walton were again aggressive, and Scattergood scored from close range, giving the visitors' goalie no chance, half-time arriving with the score 2-0 in the favour of Walton.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall swept down to the Walton end, and a hard struggle ensued, Cameron eventually shooting wide. Off-side spoiled the visitors of several good Openings. Walton, however, were now playing up well, and from a throw-in got well away, and one of the visiting backs mis- kicking, Pickering jumped in and made no mis- take with a nice shot. Grappenhall now made a determined attack on the home goal, where Woodcock received from Howells and scored Grappenhall's only goal. Result: Walton, three goals; Grappenhall, one goal. For Walton, Scattergood, at centre-forward; and Brereton, outside left, played well, whilst the pick of the Grappenhall team were Wood- cock, outside right, and Evans, centre-half.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Orford St John's v. Warrington Excelsior.</p>
        <p>On the Long-lane enclosure. - The homesters., attacked keenly, and Boyd, tripping Tickle, the referee had no hesitation in awarding a penalty to St. John's. Millvrard was entrusted with the kick and opened the score for the home team. Following this reverse, the game was stubbornly contested.. The interval arrived with St. John's' leading by one goal.</p>
        <p>There was no Score-in- the second half and the game resulted: Orford St. John's, one goal; rWarrington Excelsior, nil. Smalley, Millward and Stretford were the pick oC the home team., while Rigby, Morris and Leah were the best on the visitors' side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>Grappenhall St. Wilfrid's v. Longford United. Played at Grappenhall. Longford opened in rare style and Shakeshaft was called upon to save. Watson got going on the right, but the Longford backs were equal to the occasion. Following a combined rush Mann gave Shake- shaft no chance. Richards and JT. Ford were next prominent with a grand forward move- ment, but Ford sent outside. Richards, receiv- ing from H. Parr. sent in a good shot, which beat the Longford goalkeeper. The visiting right wing got going, but Hankey cleared with a huge punt, which Watson received and sent high over the bar, both teams being on equal terms at half-time.</p>
        <p>The second half opened in favour of the home team, and T. Ford sent outside. The visiting left got nicely going, but Evans saved the situa- tion. Booth put out to Watson, who, after a fine dribble, sent in a terrific shot, which com- pletely beat the visiting custodian. Both goals were afterwards visited in turn, but no further' goals were recorded, Grappenhall running out winners by 2-1.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>St. Wilfrid's "A" v. Walton "A."</head>
        <p>St. Wilfrid's "A" v. Walton "A."</p>
        <p>CHRISTMAS DAY MATCHES. Latchford v. Crosfield's.</p>
        <p>On the Thelwall-lane ground in the North Cheshire League. Neither team was at full strength. The sides were: Latchford—Kite; Bramhall and Robinson; Downs, Webb and Carter; Smith, Siddall, Watson, Starkey and Stubbs. Crosfield's—Clemo; Robinson and Tindall; Collins, Worrall and Bretherton; Bedda.ll, Whitfield, Cherratt, Rowlands and Bennett. Owing to some bad play in the home rear division the visitors scored the first goal. From this reverse the homesters improve 4. in their display, and the visiterS' defence ivere found plenty to do, Clemo, on one occasion, making a grand save, and Stubbs missed an easy chance of scoring. . Watson equalised a few minutes before the interval.</p>
        <p>The home forwards led an attack on the Recs.' citadel, and Siddall scored a good goal and put the home team in front. The visitors made several attempts to draw level, but Kite was never seriously troubled, and the leather was transferred to the opposite end, when Watson scored a third goal. Result Latchford, three goals; Crosfield'e, one.</p>
        <p>Wilderspool Brewery v. Emmanuel Old Boys. This Warrington League match was played at Brewery Park. The home team were not at full strength, and had to play with nine men. The Old Boy - had the best of the play and scored twice through Isherwood, and at the interval were leading by that score. On restarting the Old Boys had the game well in hand, the home team never troubling the visitors' goalkeeper very seriously, while the Old Boys pierced the ,home defence on three. occasions. Result: Emmanuel Old Boys, five goals; Wilderspool Brewery, none.</p>
        <p>Stockton Hcath Reserve v. J. Livingstone's Team.</p>
        <p>This friendly game was played at West- avenue. The play ,was evenly contested. Rich- ardson scored twice and Lloyd once before the interval.</p>
        <p>On restarting, the selected players tried hard to get on level terms, Lloyd playing a good game, while the Reserve's defence was in grand form, but the referee penalised , them for "hands" in the dreaded area, and Lloyd, tak- ing the kick, made. the scores level. Following this the Reserve put on pressure, and Isherwood put them in 'front, and Vernon was successful in equalising the score. Result: Stockton Heath, three goals; Livingstone's team, three goals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>MISCELLANEOUS ITEMS.</head>
        <p>MISCELLANEOUS ITEMS.</p>
        <p>The Adult School Boys only managed to make a draw ,at Weston. The Weston Lads are very hard to beat on their own ground. Fairfield United made the first victory of the season °yip their dear rivals, Fairfield. Stockton Heath re-asserted themselves by beating Moulton Alexandra in the Northwich Medal Competition. The Heathens introduced four reserves, and all performed very creditably. The Orford -St. John's had their revenge on the Excelsiors in the Medal Competition.</p>
        <p>They. won by a penalty goal. St. John's now have high hopes of winning the medals. The St. Barnabas' Lads beat Orford Juniors in the same competition. There are three Warrington and District Second Division League clubs in the semi-final. The prospects of the medals coming to War- rington are very good. The rumours which got abroad last week re- specting Latchford are all false. Woodhouse, of Fairfield United, is at present being watched by some of out First Division clubs.</p>
        <p>He is worth a trial in better company. The Committee of the Warrington and Dis- trict League are contemplating running a cup and medal competition for the senior clubs in the League area. .</p>
        <p>A STUPENDOUS TASK. When gold was first found in Coolgardie the district was so arid that the difficulty of living there was very great. Water, im- pure as it often was, cost half a crown a gallon. It was expensive to wash or be thirsty in Coolgardie. As soon as it was evident that the field was a good ose the government of West Australia turned their attention to the question of a proper water supply for the miners and the future town that they saw rising in the desolate and arid waste. Three hundred miles away these was a river that flowed in a narrow valley. The valley was further narrowed in one place by the projection into it of two spurs from the mountain sides. Across this gap the State built a dam which is 760 feet long and about a hundred feet high at the highest part. To ensure its strength the foundations have, in places, been carried down 100 feet below the bed of the river. It is 120 feet thick at the base while the top of it is nowhere less than fifty feet wide. By means of this dam water has been stored up and is sent to Cooigardie through pipes. Now the wonder of the whole concern is not apparent till it is remembered that Coolgardie is 1,200 feet higher up than the surface of the reservoir so that the water will not flow there of its own accord. Its natural inclination is to run the other way. The water is actually pumped through the 200 miles of pipes, there being eight sta- tions along the route, where the pumps are worked by engines that are known as 'hori- • zontal duplex triple-expansion direct-acting Worthington steam pumping engines." Why the name itself is a marvel! Such a cembina- tion of words in the -Middle Ages would cer- tainly have "savoured of an infernal com- pact.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
