      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>MARVELS OF MODERN ENGINEERING. )1/p</head>
        <p>MARVELS OF MODERN ENGINEERING. )1/p</p>
        <p>Wo live in a wonderful age, so full of mar- vels that we have almost lost the' faculty of wonder. We are surrounded by 'variety of appliances and convenielices 'the premature possef,sion of which blight in earlier days' have consigned the owner to the stake as an. accomplko of the infernal powers. We for- et hp Tivit§ri94s it is that we should send messages across the Land and water without wirt§.3 #l9,wsure o:rogfaartdheaersbyweervte that o 4tPLi9:lotttierlthyeialrrnieass: teyw;hyne r e a se am, the ioe air and as.. worltre that .he for the boys wt.. fantastic and impro— day we accept many of foretold as the veriest comilb)llYl4C4/ 61 our existence.</p>
        <p>THE RAILOI.O°N2. iel:r41111415 If you come to think of it, he the telephone, the electric tram/ .and trains, , air-ships, submarines and other wott s a 1:: ok‘ I a second • Creation and should call fortt;brow. us all at all times something more than inert/ acquiescence in their existence. A man claims now to have invented a railophone by means of which you may speak to your friends as you ride along in the train at forty miles an hour. Think of it! One man Is riding south, another north and others east and west and each one of them may be speaking to the same office at the same time. And yet, in another year or two, people will ac- cept it as a matter of course and only smile compassionately at the idea of living in a land where such useful contrivances do not exist. :</p>
        <p>WONDERFP.L TOOLS.</p>
        <p>The engineer who provides us with these marvels uses toolo just as marvellous as the work which they are employed in fashion- ing He can cut a steel girder with a circul- ar saw as easily as you can cut a bar of soap. With a little tool as big as a pistol—a form of blow pipe—he can cut a groove in the toughest steel safe that was ever invented and do it noiselessly. But the only things that make us stare in these days of scientific wonders are big things, things whose size is of such a charac- ter that we cannot withhold a passing tri- bute of admiration for the men who design and construct them. For instance there is the Blackwell Islands Bridge at New York which is over 1,200 yards long, and is built in two stories, the lower one with a wide carriage way and four oar-tracks, and the upper one with four elevated railroad tracks and two wide footpaths for passengers. There are 50,000 tons of steel in the framework; the cost of the complete structure was about five million pounds sterling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>AUTOMATIC MACHINERY.</head>
        <p>AUTOMATIC MACHINERY.</p>
        <p>There is a tendency amongst engineers to make as much of their machinery as possible automatic, and the limit in this would almost seem to have been reached in the generating- station of the Underground Railway in Lon- don. The power-house is at Chelsea and is one of the largest in the world: There are two rows of boilers on the two floors. The coal Is unloaded from barge 'S by electric cranes, dropped into the hopper of an elevator, car- vied to the top of a building and handed-over to another piece of machinery that puts it into the coal bunkers under the roof.</p>
        <p>It falls down a series of shoots only to be received by mechanical stokers which feed it into the furnaces. The ashes fall out at the back of the boiler and are conveyed by the wemingly intelligent machinery, to shoots in the ground whence they are removed out of the building by means of automatia convey- ers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>MARVELLOUS SWING BRIDGES.</head>
        <p>MARVELLOUS SWING BRIDGES.</p>
        <p>Of swing bridges there are not a few bat are worth more than a passing mention such as the great one which carries the l3o4ge- water Canal over the Manchester Ship Canal with its powerful gates at each end that can closN when the two halves are swung apart. At the entrance to one of the docks in London there is a bridge that rises off its supports and glides up the road when it is necessary to move it. In this case there is no room for it to swing in the orthodox fa- shion.</p>
        <p>There are special types of ship that are really as wonderful as a fairy tale. The Cyclops of the Ditish Navy is such a one. It looks like an ordinary cargo steamer but it is blacksmith's shop, a factory, a foundry a dozen other things, all in one. It can castings up to a ton weight on board, cal execute all ordinary repairs to a battle- ship and possesses in addition to all this pu•-ly engineering machinery a plant for the distillation of pure water, an ice ma- chine, and a bakery in which the 'dough is mixed by, an electric mixer.</p>
        <p>ar -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>•SELF-DISCHARGING COLLIER.</head>
        <p>A •SELF-DISCHARGING COLLIER.</p>
        <p>Another interesting vessel is the "self- discharging' collier which can discharge its cargo of 3,100 tons in 8 hours with scarce- ly any assistance from man except to con- trol the machinery. At Venice there is a huge dredger that sucks up mud from the places where it is at work and discharges it on the bed of the sea 2,000 feet away by means Of a huge pipe that floats on the sur- face of the water. Another suction machine in use at Bombay is so powerful that it has been known to suck up a lump of stone weighing 400 pounds. There are two of, these dredgers at work and they not only lift up the waste but they deposit it in another , area where the land is being raised. They can do as much work in an hour as 5,000 carts and an army of men.</p>
        <p>In the quctrterly report of the East Lancashire Operative Spinners' Association it is mentioned that at the present time there are only 6.5 per cent. Of non-unionists in the association's arft.</p>
        <p>Rikors 1)001WM13</p>
