      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>PUBLIC AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>"ALADDIN" ARRIVED YESTERDAY. Pantomime Starts at Royal Court. All lovers of pantomime who attended the Royal Court yesterday will not regret theii visit, for the production of "Aladdin," undel the superintendence of Mr. Nightingale, is one that really merits praise. The house was packed at each of the performances, and the applause and after-comment augurs well for a successful run. The leading characters are ably maintained with credit, not only to the actors, but to the management. The produc- tion is of the twice nightly variety, and has ten gorgeous scenes- in which to unfold the tale, the grand finale coming as a most fitting 'climax. Miss Gladys Mavius is a charming principal "boy." and makes a decided hit with' her songs, while the Abanazar of Mr. Tom llulme's would take very strenuous efforts to be beaten. There is plenty of fun, good singing and dancing. There are to be numerous matinees. The per- formance, as given yesterday, is full of merit. MORALITY PLAY AT HIPPODROME. Superb Attractions. Patrons of the Hippodrome are more than satisfied with the excellent holiday fare Pro- vided this week. "The Saint and the Sinner" is a highly dramatic morality play. A cora- binaiion of the. most daring and marvellous gymnasts are the Five Jovers, to whom the most intricate somersaults are, to all appearances, "child's play." Jack Walsh is a happy-go- lucky comedian, and scores with his ragtime Parody medley and the "candle song." Lawson and O'Dell Combine singing, dancing and humour most successfully. Albert Jenkinson, the Northern Union footballer, is a versatile comedian, who knows how to please. Ara Zebra and Vora give a novel acrobatic turn, "Pastimes on the Prairie," and Arthur Lloyd, a local entertainer, deputises for the Clarke Sisters and Sterling. On Christmas night the picture entertainment was decidedly popular, and large audipnces assembled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>An Attractive Bill.</head>
        <p>An Attractive Bill.</p>
        <p>Quite an acceptable programme has been ar- ranged for next week at the Hippodrome, where the principal "turn" will be supplied by A. C. Lilly's company in the episode "The Relief of the Devil's Pass." The clever equilibrists, Paul and Fred Willie, are booked, together with the following items: Lena Bluette, vocalist; Cyrus Dare, mimic; Ben Albert, comedian; Mdlle. Marjutti, in "From Demon to Fairy- land"; and Pat Lynch, the dancing demon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>CIRCUS AT LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>THE CIRCUS AT LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>A special Chris-ti-n-as. -seas-on.:ti;a:tion is the Great Olympia Circus at Liverpool, the enter- prise which Mr. Frank Allen, of Moss's Empires, has taken in hand. The revival of a brilliant circus on elaborate lines has proved decidedly successful, and those who witnessed the in- augural performances on Monday were struck by the excellence of the tariff. Busch's horses, a team of sixteen Arabian thoroughbreds, go through the most intricate tricks, under the able direction of Mr. Eschberger. The Pizziuttis give a daring exhibition of riding the gallop- ing horse. Capt. M. Rudolf puts two elephants, a pony and a dog through a series of amazing and wonderful evolutions. . Hickey's Comedy Mules cause roars of laughter. The Nine Fly- ing Hegelmens give. a sensational trapeze act, whilst Pali and Sellery are highly amusing in "The Clumsy Acrobat." "The Soakum Regatta" is a highly diverting water spectacle. A number of clowns add to the excruciatingly funny incidents. Mr. Pat a'Beckett is a capable equestrian director.</p>
        <p>THEATRE ROYAL. . In the early part of the week the " Stock " Company scored a success in the stirring drama, "For His Life." On Christmas night the record-breaking film, "Quo Vadis?" was shown, and last night "Jane Shore," a drama, was staged, and this will also be the attraction for to-day.</p>
        <p>The Royal Stock Company will again appear at the Theatre Royal next week, when they will present "Shadows of a Great City," a fascinat- ing drama.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>PICTUREDROME.</head>
        <p>THE PICTUREDROME.</p>
        <p>" Younita" is the title of the highly dramatic picture story shown at the Picturedrome in the early part of the week. The pictures on Chrifit- mas Day were specially attractive, and drew large houses. For the remainder of the week "The Queen's Jewel," a magnificent dramatic masterpiece, will be shown. "The Comedian's Mask" will form the chief attraction for Monday, Tuesday and Wednes- day of next week at the Picturedrome, whilst for the remainder of the week "For Ever" is advertised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>"MARY STUART" AT THE "PARK."</head>
        <p>"MARY STUART" AT THE "PARK."</p>
        <p>The Park Picture Palace has been well patronised during the week, and on Christmas night the historical dramatic film, "Mary Stuart," was shown before large audiences. This will also be shown to-day. A graphic story of Old London, entitled "Little Dorritt," will be shown at the Park Palace next week for the first three nights, the " star " film for the remainder of the week being "Victory or death."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</head>
        <p>NEW PICTURE HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Christmas bay- at the Picture House was a very happy one. The principal films were "Estrelita," "The Call," and "Fortune Smiles," or "What Happened to Mary." These films will be shown to-night.</p>
