      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>Bright Comforts for the Workhouse Inmates and Merriment at Padgate Cottage Homes.</head>
        <p>Bright Comforts for the Workhouse Inmates and Merriment at Padgate Cottage Homes.</p>
        <p>MERRY TIME ALL ROUND,</p>
        <p>Christmas passed with its usual peace and merriment. Home parties were a feature of Christmas Day, but the absence of the trams caused the only grumble to be heard. Those who had to journey from one part of the town to another found much inconvenience through the absence of the poor man's coach—the tram- car. However, the stoppage of the trains proved the harvest for the cab and taxi owners, and these folk were inundated with orders. Had they possessed the vehicles they pould have coped with double the orders they. were only able to undertake. Cabs were flying about everywhere, and it must have been a long time since the proprietors have had such a boom as that experienced on Christmas Day. Then there were feasts for the poor in the Workhouse, and for the day all care was set aside, and the old folk made merry. The out- door poor got is. 'ld. per adult and 9d. per child extra. At the Padgate Homes there were also festival rejoicings. The town itself was com- paratively quiet, many of the people having indoors until away for the day, or remained until the places of amusement opened. The hardest worked individuals were those connected with the Post Office, and the men were engaged delivering letters and parcels until a comparatively late hour. The railways were also very busy, but the rush had exhausted itelf early in the day.</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR AT THE WORKHOUSE. Old Folk Have a Merry Time. The Mayor and Mayoress, with their usual kindly thought for others spent a part of their</p>
      </div>
