      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>" Will yer take this—it's too big for the boxy" "Can't you see my hands is full." "'Ow about in yer mouth?"</head>
        <p>" Will yer take this—it's too big for the boxy" "Can't you see my hands is full." "'Ow about in yer mouth?"</p>
        <p>wt i emr ee on e eCelimr pi satnmi eads Dbayy t r .t the Wroole rdk h Smitho u s . The a n dy Mr. J. Fulton-Smith. The Institution was completely transformed. a On every hand there were evidences of the care , bestowed on the inmates, and of cleanliness I that could not be surpassed. Every year there is very keen competition between the various wards for the honour of receiving the highest comment for the decorations from the visitors, and each ward this year developed a new scheme of decoration. Here one could see a tableau of the South Pole, with sleighs, tents and dogs, all sprinkled with " snow." There was an aeroplane suspended from the ceiling. All the electric lights had lent themselves to adapta- tion in some colour arrangement, and in places, hanging from wall to wall, were festoons of artificial flowers supporting seasonable greet- ings. The large dining-room was prettily decor- ated, and had a large motto, "Success to our Guardians," but the most pleasing feature was the happiness impressed upon the faces of those who by unfortunate circumstances are forced to be classified among the inmates. The Mayor's Message.</p>
        <p>During the course of the dinner the Maym and Mr. Harold Smith addressed the gathering in suitable terms, and over this little ceremony Mr. E. L. Bent, chairman of the Board, pre- sided.</p>
        <p>Arousing cheer was given as the visitors mounted the stage, and the MAYOR replied by saying that he was very pleased at the recep- tion Mr. Harold Smith and himself had re ceived. He was sure that as he looked round the tables it was very gratifying for him to see them all enjoying themselves in the manner they were. The Mayor praised the efforts of the Guardians and officials; and 'he remarked that he was only sorry that treats of that char- acter could not be repeated more, often. No- body never knew whether they would be forced to find in such .places, and he was certain the ratepayers would never grumble at a little extra being given to the. inmates. Whilst he regretted that these, treats were not more frequently given, he said at the same time they did feel pleased that at certain times of the year they tried to make them more 'com- fortable than they otherwise would be.. lie was pleased to see that this year the Guardians had given the people in receipt of outdoor relief a little extra to help them to more fully enjoy the festive season, a remark which the inmates , heartily cheered.</p>
        <p>in which Councillor and Mrs. Peacock *ere held, and hoped that some day some of the children present might attain the honour of being the mayor of the town. The Mayor praised the work of the institu- tion, and said he hoped to visit them again shortly. The children there were receiving great advantages—advantages much greater than many others were getting—and he urged them to always do their best in whatever they had to do.</p>
        <p>The Master proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor, and Mayoress for their attendance. This was seconded by Mr. French and heartily car- ried.</p>
        <p>The Mayor responded, and included a vote of thanks to the Master and Matron and the officers for their untiring efforts, Mr. Hilditch seconding.</p>
        <p>An excellent entertainment was provided by the children, when they presented a pantomime "Babes in the Wood," whilst others entertained by dancing. Much praise is due to all the officers for the way everything had been ar- ranged, and especially to Mr. and Mrs. C. E. Lawrence, the superintendent and the matron.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>"A Splendid Thing."</head>
        <p>"A Splendid Thing."</p>
        <p>Mr. HAROLD SMITH said in this world there were people who seemed to have all good luck and others who seemed to have all bad luck, and to the latter they could only offer their sympathy, and whilst they sympathised with them, all would agree that it was a splendid thing to be able to go to a lace like that. Mr. J. FULTONSMITH also added his praise, and likened the gathering to a large and happy family of a young father and mother. Mr. E. L. BENT proposed a comprehensive vote of thanks, which Mr. GEORGE MATHER seconded, the MAYOR, Mr. SMITH and Mr. BROWN, the Master, responding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>5001 b. of Plum Pudding.</head>
        <p>5001 b. of Plum Pudding.</p>
        <p>The dinner was evidently much -enjoyed, and our representative elicited the fact that 1501 b. of beef were consumed, together with about 5001 b. of pudding, which was served with sauce. Beer and mineral waters were supplied to those desiring them. In the evening a very acceptable programme was submitted by the Theatre Royal Stock Co., and the inmates appeared highly delighted. Much praise is due to the, Master and Matron, Mr. and Mrs. W. J. Brown, and the officials, who had worked so hard for the successful and en- joyable festivities.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>AT THE INFIRMARY.</head>
        <p>AT THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>% I</p>
        <p>The majority of the patients at the Infirmary spent a very happy Christmas. The wards were prettily decorated, and when the Mayor and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>AT ORFORD BARRACKS.</head>
        <p>AT ORFORD BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>At Orford Barracks, where there are only about *4O men stationed, Christmas was cele- brated in a quiet way. The men partook of an excellent Christmas dinner, after which the usual routine, duties were performed, and those off duty spent the evening quietly. We under- stand that preparations are being made for social functions early in the New Year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>POST OFFICE AND RAILWAY.</head>
        <p>POST OFFICE AND RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>. At Warrington Post Office the work was un- usually heavy, and a large number Of extra men had to be employed. But in spite of this, postmen were giving the familiar knock at the door all through the day and well into the night. • The letters and .parcels reached a record. Yesterday the sorting-room presented a deserted appearance, for all were holidaying after the arduous time on Christmas Day. Although there are a large number who prefer to spend Christmas by the fireside, the railway officials state that this year has created another record in the number of passengers and amount of goods carried. Blackpool seems to have been well patronisesl, along with Scottish, Irish and North Wales centres. The trains have all been well loaded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>UNCONSCIOUS SARCASM.</head>
        <p>UNCONSCIOUS SARCASM.</p>
        <p>" Please excuse me using, lead. I had the misfortune to damage my pen and I write on the spur of the moment," says one of our corre- spondents, apologising for breaking our rule as to writing with ink. We confess to having been thrown back upon lead ourselves, but up to the present we have steered clear of the spur of the moment as a ' material upon which to write.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>OUTSIDE OUR EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>OUTSIDE OUR EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>_ The judgment of the youngsters often is un- consciously scathing, matching the oft-recog- nised quickness of wit. A certain mother, for instance, is given to fits of indecision. Out of these she frequently is helped by a less vacil- la I ing younger sister.</p>
        <p>" \Vliere is Aunt Mary?" recently asked a friend of. the family, arriving during one of these household crises.</p>
        <p>"Upstairs, making up mamma's mind for her," smiled the young son of the house.</p>
        <p>The Earl of Derby has offered the living of Ormskirk, vacant by the death of Canon Woodrow, to the Rev. C. E. Haynes, vicar of St. Andrew's, Liverpool, who has accepted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
