      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-12-27_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>BILLIARDS.</head>
        <p>BILLIARDS.</p>
        <p>SENIOR CONSERVATIVE WARDS FINAL. The wards competition was brought to a con. elusion on Monday evening, when St. Austin' No. 3 won by 19. Result : St. Austin's No. 3. Town Hall No. 2.</p>
        <p>C. Turner lOO R Straw A. Kenworthy ...... 59 W. Ford F. Holland . 86 T. Weir</p>
        <p>J. Sisson W. Lowe</p>
        <p>.... 71 W. Breward lOO 100 T. Horrabin</p>
        <p>ORIGIN OF THE WAISTCOAT.</p>
        <p>The waistcoat as an article of attire for men dates back to the time of Charles H. Historians tell us that that monarch made a resolution to change the fashion of his dress to one which he would never alter, and communicated this deci- sion to his Council in October, .1666. From a diary of, that period we read: "This day the King begins to put on his vest, and I did soe several persons of tile Houk of Lords and Commons, too, mat courtiers who are in it, being a long cossack close to the body, of black cloth and pinked with white silk under it." From Ilugge's diary we learn of "a close coat of cloth pink, with a white taffety under the etas. This in length reached the calf of the leg, and upon that a sercoatt cutt at the breastt, Which hung loose and shorter than the- vest six inches." A few weeks later Pepys, in speaking of the Court, said it was "all full of. vests, only my Lord St. Albans not pinked but plain black, and they say the 'King says the pinking upon the whites makes them look too much like magpies, and therefore bath bespoke one of plain velvet."</p>
