        <p>CHRISTMAS FESTIVITIES.</p>
        <p>Mayoress, Mr. Harold Smith and Mr. J. Fulton- , Smith arrived all looked exceedingly cheerful. I The visitors chatted freely with the patients, and seasonable greetings were exchanged. In the, afternoon and evening dancing and singing were indulged in in several of the wards, and altogether a most happy time was spent. AT PADGATE HOMES. The merry Christmas spirit did not fail to pay a visit to the Padgate Cottage Homes, and as a result the institution was filled with children, whose hearts throbbed with joy. In the morn- ing the boys and girls attended the churches of their religious beliefs, and afterwards were mar- shalled for dinner in the schoolroom. Here, as in other parts of the building, evidences were not lacking of a very artistic scheme of deco- ration, and it was pleasing to see the aptness with which the natural and artificial effects blended with the numerous mottoes During the morning the Mayor and Mayoress and Mr. Harold Smith visited the Home, and expressed delight at what they saw. The Guar- dians present were Messrs. George Mather, Wil- liam Houghton, McKinley and Mrs. Hall, and during the afternoon Mr. W. C. Brooks, chair- man of the Cottage Homes Committee, attended, with three of his daughters. Mayor Distributes Prizes. The evening's proceedings were again attended by the Mayor and Mayoress, and his Worship distributed prizes for schoolroom work, faithful discharge of duties, and for proficiency in the various trades. The Master spoke of the respect</p>
